1 NOTE Richard Oldage
2 CONT
2 CONT     Source:   Samuel Richardson and Josiah Ellsworth per Savage..
2 CONT [Note, this is also in Genealogical Dictionary of New England Settlers,
2 CONT Volume 3, page 307]
2 CONT Oldage, Oldige, Oldridge, or Olderige, Richard, Windsor, bef. 1640, possib. went from Dorchester, but was not kn. there to Dr. Harris, d. 27 Jan. 1661 [note: 1661 is error, probate record says 1660], and the name is extinct. His o
2 CONC nly ch. Ann m.  19 May 1645, John Osborn.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Source:   Connecticut Puritan Settlers, 1633-1845
2 CONT First Sttlers of the Colony.
2 CONT Oldage, Richard, came to Windsor with Mr. Huet in 1639.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Source:   The Sharpe Family Records
2 CONT Richard, died January 27, 1661, in Windsor, Conn.; married (???).
2 CONT Anne2 [Anne only daughter of Richard], died August 28, 1689; married, May 19, 1645-6, John Osborn. 419.
2 CONT Richard was an early settler of Windsor, Conn.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Richard Oldage, born probably in England; died Jan. 27, 1660, in Windsor, Conn. Hinman says he came to Windsor with Mr. Hunt in 1639. He owned a lot of ground 12 rods wide next south of John Stiles (between the Colonel Ellsworth 
2 CONC house and about 40 rods north of the Major Ellsworth tenant house), and extended from meadow on east to Rocky Hill on west. At his death his homestead fell to his only daughter Anne, wife of John Osborne. Richard Oldage owned land
2 CONC  in Windsor before 1653. In 1655/6 he was appointed sealer of leather.
2 CONT Child:
2 CONT    i. Anne, m. John Osborne. (See Osborne Family.)
2 CONT
2 CONT   Source:   Hartford, Connecticut Probate Records, 1729-50
2 CONT
2 CONT A Digest of the early Connecticut  Probate Records.
2 CONT
2 CONT Volume I, Page 141
2 CONT The following should have appeared on this page:
2 CONT Oldage, Richard, Windsor. Died 27 January, 1660. Invt. taken by Deacon Gaylord and Humphrey Pinney.
2 CONT And shortly after an Inventori was taken of the estat that he left conserning which before his death he had before witnes said that his will was that his sonn in law John Osbon should have all that he had and he was to alow him ma
2 CONC intenanc whilst he lived and so was to take all and paye all.
2 CONT Witnes: Mat:Grant, Tho:Dibble.
2 CONT ?--s-d.
2 CONT Imprimis his howse and home lott 8 ac 3 quartus valued at 40- 0-0.
2 CONT wood land and swamp nere pine meadow 13 ac 10- 0-0.
2 CONT ten ac of swamp neare adjoyning valued 01- 0-0.
2 CONT one the east side of the great river aboue namerok 12 rodd wide valued at 02- 0-0.
2 CONT below namerok 12 rodd wide vald at 04-01-0.
2 CONT goods with in howse valued in all 11-11-0.
2 CONT 68-11-0.
2 CONT the state indebted to Mr Henery Clarke 0-11-0.
2 CONT for rates to the towne 0-03-3.
2 CONT the men that valued the state ware
2 CONT Deacon Gaylard, Houmpery Pinne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Oldage is said, by tradition, to have been of Welsh origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Source:   Samuel Richardson and Josiah Ellsworth per Savage..
2 CONT [Note, this is also in Genealogical Dictionary of New England Settlers,
2 CONT Volume 3, page 307]
2 CONT Oldage, Oldige, Oldridge, or Olderidge, Richard, Windsor, bef. 1640, possib. went from Dorchester, but was not kn. there to Dr. Harris, d. 27 Jan. 1661 [note: 1661 is error, probate record says 1660], and the name is extinct. His 
2 CONC only ch. Ann m.  19 May 1645, John Osborn.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Source:   Connecticut Puritan Settlers, 1633-1845
2 CONT First Sttlers of the Colony.
2 CONT Oldage, Richard, came to Windsor with Mr. Huet in 1639.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Source:   The Sharpe Family Records
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard, died January 27, 1661, in Windsor, Conn.; married (???).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne [Anne only daughter of Richard], died August 28, 1689; married, May 19, 1645-6, John Osborn. 419.
2 CONT Richard was an early settler of Windsor, Conn.
2 CONT    Richard Oldage, born probably in England; died Jan. 27, 1660, in Windsor, Conn. Hinman says he came to Windsor with Mr. Hunt in 1639. He owned a lot of ground 12 rods wide next south of John Stiles (between the Colonel Ellswort
2 CONC h house and about 40 rods north of the Major Ellsworth tenant house), and extended from meadow on east to Rocky Hill on west. At his death his homestead fell to his only daughter Anne, wife of John Osborne. Richard Oldage owned la
2 CONC nd in Windsor before 1653. In 1655/6 he was appointed sealer of leather.
2 CONT Child:
2 CONT    i.  Anne, m. John Osborne. (See Osborne Family.)
2 CONT
2 CONT   Source:   Hartford, Connecticut Probate Records, 1729-50
2 CONT A digest of the early Connecticut Probate records.
