1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family legend has it that the Osborn family was descended from a William Fitzosborn, a cousin and assistant to William the Conqueror.   Wm Fitzosborn was given credit for winning of the Battle of Hastings in 1066. 
2 CONC  Later, the Conqueror conferred on Fitzosborn the area of Hereford, also the site on which the castle of Chepstow lies.  William Fitzosborn had had the original section of the castle built about 1067.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some authorities make the connection to William Fitzosborn easy to believe; some give doubt.  At any rate, It is great fun to travel this "route" which leads to Chepstow, Wales, on the border between Wales and England, across the 
2 CONC channel from Bristol.  If this is connection is viable, then the family links with all sorts of fascinating noblemen, both descendants and antecedents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other items in connection with family legend include:
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Osbourne House at East Cowes in the Isle of Wight was Queen Victoria's seaside home and her residence at the time of her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT *   William Fitzosborn may have been given the Isle of Wight. It lies one mile off the southern coast and is 23 miles in length.
2 CONT
2 CONT One reference for a portion of the above can be found in:
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Osborne's and Alley's, published by the Historical Society of SW VA, 1973, Source #628.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is not listed in any ship manifest, so is believed to have been a stowaway. The story is he was an apprenticed gardener in England and forgot to cover some vegetables one night which fell victim to frost. Fearing punishment, he
2 CONC  hid under supplies on a ship about to sail for America. He was not found for 2-3 days when it was too late to put him ashore. He got safely across but was promptly bound out again by the ship's captain to pay for his passage. In 
2 CONC Sussex County, Delaware, he was indentured to pay for his passage on June 13-14, 1682 to Norton Claypoole for three years until he turned twenty-four.  Subtracting twenty-one from 1682 has determined his birth to be 1661 in Englan
2 CONC d. On 15 June 168_? 300 acres? (copying error) of land was also granted to Matthew after he finished his term with Norton Claypoole. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He made a will in Georgetown, Sussex County, Delaware on 10 Dec. 1733, was buried 2 Apr 1738 and will was proved 5 Apr 1738.  Tradition says he was supposed to have been present when William Penn signed a treaty with  the Delaware
2 CONC  Indians.  He was so impressed by William Penn that he became a Quaker at the signing of the treaty.  Matthew's parents may have been Robert Osborn born 17 Feb 1621 Suffolk County, England. He married Elizabeth _______? and they a
2 CONC re the parents of a Matthew Osborn christened 22 Feb 1663, Gazeley, Suffolk County, England.  Matthew Osborn: 1661 England - 1738 Georgetown Sussex County Delaware married Mary _______.  Matthew spent most of his adult life in Sus
2 CONC sex Co., Delaware.  He became owner of a relatively large acreage of land. Home Meeting House was Cold Springs Neck, which there seems to be little knowledge or record. He was listed as a Yeoman.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://listsearches.rootsweb.com/th/read/QUAKER-ROOTS/2002-01/1010289835
2 CONT
2 CONT To Quaker Roots Readers,
2 CONT
2 CONT This mailing is in reply to the mailing dated 01/02/2002 from Gloria Holder, who had certain questions relating to the Osborn and Dobson families in Colonial Delaware and in adjoining Chester County, Pennsylvania.  I do not believ
2 CONC e that I mentioned either Matthew Osborn I or Matthew Osborn II in my paper in "Delaware History". Fall-Winter 1982, concerning  "Quakers in Delaware in the Time of William Penn.  Perhaps it was after writing this paper that I cam
2 CONC e across a book devoted to early civil records of Sussex County, Delaware which indicates that Matthew Osborn I was a member of a small Quaker circle who were living in the vicinity of Lewes, Delaware in the 1690's. 
2 CONT
2 CONT About twenty years ago, when I was involved in research pertaining to Quakers in lower Delaware, I recall the name of Matthew Osborn in relation to Duck Creek Monthly Meeting, centered in Smyrna, Delaware, but I do not recall that
2 CONC  he particularly represented Cool Spring Meeting in Sussex County, Delaware.  Cool Spring was a Preparative Meeting of Duck Creek Monthly Meeting.  Although I do not believe that I am a direct descendant of any of the Osborn famil
2 CONC y, I am related in various ways to people who married into the Osborn family.  My father's sister, Elsie Matilda Standing, married Robert Osborn, son of William and Samantha (Bond) Osborn of Spring River Friends Meeting (Conservat
2 CONC ive) in Cherokee County, Kansas.  A nephew of Robert Osborn, Dale W. Osborn of Colorado Springs, Colorado, is much interested in genealogy. Some three years ago I was able to secure a copy of his research concerning the Osborn fam
2 CONC ily, and I carried on a brief correspondence with him.  Dale Osborn's account of "Osborn and Related Family Lines", compiled 1986 to May 1998, includes the following passages:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The first Osborn in our line to come to America was Matthew Osborn I from England to Sussex County, Delaware in 1682.  He is reported to be a descendant of William Fitzosborn mentioned above.  (William Fitzosborn was reported to 
2 CONC be a participant with William the Conqueror at the Battle of Hastings in 1066.--- H.C.S.).   He is not listed in any ship manifest, so is believed to have been a stowaway.  The story is he was an apprenticed gardener in England an
2 CONC d forgot to cover some vegetables one night which fell victim to frost. Fearing punishment, he hid under supplies on a ship about to sail for America. He was not found for 2-3 days when it was too late to put him ashore. He got sa
2 CONC fely across but was promptly bound out again by the ship's captain to pay for his passage. In Sussex County, Delaware, he was indentured to pay for his passage on June 13-14, 1682 to Norton Claypoole for three years until he turne
2 CONC d twenty-four. Subtracting twenty-one from 1682 has determined his birth to be 1661 in England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On l5 June 168_? 300 acres? (copying error) of land was also granted to Matthew after he finished his term with Norton Claypoole. He made a will in Georgetown, Sussex County, Delaware on 10 Dec. 1733, was buried 2 Apr 1738 and wil
2 CONC l was proved 5 Apr 1738. Tradition says he was supposed to have been present when William Penn signed a treaty with the Delaware Indians.  He was so impressed by William Penn that he became a Quaker at the signing of the treaty.  
2 CONC Matthew's parents may have been Robert Osborn born 17 Feb 1621 Suffolk County, England. He married Elizabeth _______? and they are the parents of a Matthew Osborn christened 22 Feb 1663, Gazeley, Suffolk County, England.  Matthew 
2 CONC Osborn: 1661 England - 1738 Georgetown Sussex County Delaware married Mary _______.  Matthew spent most of his adult life in Sussex Co., Delaware.  He became owner of a relatively large acreage of land. Home Meeting House was Cold
2 CONC  Springs Neck, which there seems to be little knowledge or record.  He was listed as a Yeoman.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Matthew Osborn II, 1697 Delaware- 22 June 1783 Center FBG, Guilford County, North Carolina (married) Isabel Dobson (b.__________) - (d.) 19 May 1770, Gulford County, North Carolina, dt. of Richard Dobson and Abigail Hollingsworth
2 CONC .  Married 16 Oct 1723 Coolspring MM Kent County Delaware (sub of Duck Creek MM) both are buried in Center FBG.  Moved to Warrington MM in York County, Pennsylvania on a certificate dated 17 June 1749. They moved from Warrington M
2 CONC M on a certificate dated 15 Sep 1753, received by Cane Creek MM , Orange (now Alamance ) County, North Carolina on 5 Jan 1754.  Matthew helped establish Center MM in North Carolina."
2 CONT
2 CONT I have copied only portions of Dale W. Osborn's account, especially concerning Matthew Osborn II.  Since Dale probably has never visited in Delaware, some of his details are inaccurate.  Coolspring MM was in Sussex County, Delawar
2 CONC e, not in Kent County, and it was a Preparative Meeting, not a Monthly Meeting.  I mention Norton Claypoole and Rachel Claypoole and their young family on page 144 of my paper.  Norton Claypoole had arrived in Lewes in 1678. Some 
2 CONC of my Claypoole data came from:  Rebecca Irwin Groff, "Genealogy of the Claypoole Family of Philadelphia, 1588-1893" (Philadelphia:  1893).
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter to Dale Osborn dated Oct. 28, 1998, I made the following remarks:  "There is something wrong in the assertion that Matthew Osborn II married on 15 October 1723 Isabel Dobson, daughter of Richard Dobson and Abigail Holl
2 CONC ingsworth.  In my "Delaware Quaker Records", Vol. I, p. 38, I list Abigail Hollingsworth as being a younger daughter of Henry Hollingsworth and Lydia Atkinson.  She was probably born about 1700 and married Richard Dobson in 1720. 
2 CONC  Isabel Dobson, (if she was a daughter of Richard and Abigail), could not have been the mother of William Osborn, son of Matthew Osborn II, if William was born in 1725.  Isabel Dobson could possibly have been the second wife of Ma
2 CONC tthew Osborn II.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Quaker family of Hollingsworth can generally be traced back to Valentine Hollingsworth who brought his large family to Brandywine Hundred in northern New Castle County, Delaware in 1682 from the vicinity of Lurgan in northern 
2 CONC Ireland.  Valentine Hollingsworth had been married twice, first to Ann Ree who died in 2 mo. 1, 1671, then to Ann Calvert.  Valentine and Ann Ree had four children, of whom Henry was the second from the oldest, born 9 mo. 7, 1658.
2 CONC   Henry Hollingsworth came to the Delaware Valley on a different boat than the rest of the family, then returned to Ireland briefly to marry Lydia Atkinson on 6 mo., 22, 1688.  Henry was a deputy surveyor in Chester County, PA for
2 CONC  a number of years, also Clerk of Court and Coroner of Chester County.  He removed to Elkton, MD ca. 1712 and was appointed surveyor of Cecil County, MD.  According to Albert Cook Myers in his "Immigration of the Irish Quakers int
2 CONC o Pennsylvania". 
2 CONT
2 CONT I am not familiar with the name of Richard Dobson in American Quaker records. It appears that I am descendant of Richard Dobson (probably his wife was named Mary) who was a member of Thirsk Monthly Meeting near York in north Engla
2 CONC nd. His daughter, Deborah Dobson, married John Chambers of Acaster Selby (a few miles south of York) in 1698.  When I was at the Friends Meetinghouse at York in 1971, I saw records of how John Chambers was fined continually from a
2 CONC bout 1700 to about 1712 for non-payment of church tithes.  The Chambers family emigrated to Chester County, PA about 1713.  Perhaps the Richard Dobson who married Abigail Hollingsworth was a younger brother of Deborah (Dobson) Cha
2 CONC mbers."
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that, in order to solve this problem, some careful searching of documents is in order.  It is my impression that the elder Richard Dobson of Thirsk in Yorkshire was active in Friends affairs in the later 1600's.  Perhap
2 CONC s there are other records concerning him and his family, either at the Friends Meetinghouse at York or at the Friends House Library in London.  Then there should be a careful re-reading of the Duck Creek Monthly Meeting records at
2 CONC  the Friends Library at Swarthmore College, and other Quaker records pertaining to the Matthew Osborn II family which may be included in Warrington MM, PA records or in North Carolina Quaker records such as those of Cane Creek MM,
2 CONC  NC and New Garden MM, NC. I understand that some of the Center MM, NC records have been lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT As to the particular questions asked by Gloria Holder, I do not know the maiden name of the wife of Matthew Osborn I.  I do not know of any relation of William Clark and Honor Hullings to the Osborn's.   I have tried to state abov
2 CONC e that the Dobson's in America might have been related to the Dobson's in Yorkshire.  I do not know whether the Dobson's in America lived in Pennsylvania or Delaware.  I believe that John and Deborah (Dobson) Chambers and family s
2 CONC ettled in southern Chester County, PA or perhaps over the line in Mill Creek Hundred, New Castle County, DE.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is about all I can do concerning this matter at this time. I hope that I have not been more confusing than helpful.   Herbert Standing.
2 CONT
