1 OCCU soldier, military
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the son of https://www.geni.com/people/John-Ellsworth-Fictional/6000000003524043694?through=6000000006906990409 & https://www.geni.com/people/Lucia-Ellsworth-Fictional/6000000001226444245?through=60000000069069
2 CONC 90409
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Lived in Windsor Connecticut.  He resided in Cambridgeshire.  From this branch of the family belongs Chief Justice Oliver Ellsworth.  CBW Notes
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Josiah Ellsworth''' He was born in 1629. He was in Connecticut as early as 1646. In 1654 he bought a house and lot in Windsor south of the Rivulet, near the old mill, on what was afterwards known as the Gillett place. In 1655 h
2 CONC e bought the property afterwards known as the Chief Justice Ellsworth place. He was a juror in 1664; admitted a freeman May 21, 1657. His wife was admitted to the church in Windsor about 1663, and he contributed three shillings to
2 CONC  the Connecticut relief fund for the poor of other colonies. He died August 20, 1689, leaving an estate valued at six hundred and fifty-five pounds. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, November 16, 1654, Elizabeth Holcomb, who died September 18, 1712. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT *Josias, born December 5, 1655; 
2 CONT *Elizabeth, November 11, 1657; 
2 CONT *Mary, May 7, 1660; 
2 CONT *Martha, December 7, 1662; 
2 CONT *Sergeant Thomas, September 2, 1665; 
2 CONT *Jonathan, June 28, 1669, mentioned below; 
2 CONT *Lieutenant John, October 7, 1671; 
2 CONT *Captain Job, April 13, 1674; 
2 CONT *Benjamin, January 16, 1676, died April 14, 1690.
2 CONT
2 CONT ——
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: The name of "Eelstown" so often seen in texts about the history of the Ellsworth family has long ago been disproven. There is no such town in Cambridgeshire or even in England nor was there ever.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Spurious Pedigree
2 CONT
2 CONT Supposedly the Ellsworth's are a descendant of Sir John Ellsworth of the time of Edward the Third. (1312-1377). 
2 CONT
2 CONT (I) Sergeant Josias Ellsworth, the immigrant ancestor, was the son of John Ellsworth, and said to have been a descendant of Sir John Ellsworth, in the time of Edward III, who resided in Cambridgeshire, England. This conjecture is 
2 CONC derived from "Mr. John Ellsworth, who was a respectable merchant in London, early in the nineteenth century, who stated that it was a tradition in his family which had long resided in Yorkshire, that a member of it had formerly re
2 CONC moved to foreign parts; that he was a young man when he left, and never returned." 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.aylesworth.net/Confidence_family_DWT_CSS/ellsworth_family_notes.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT > "Among the earliest records of the family in England were of Albin de Ellesworthe of Cambridgeshire in 1273, [https://www.geni.com/people/Sampson-Ellsworthe/6000000059820713822 Samson de Ellesworth] and Robert de Elesworde of th
2 CONC e same country at a slightly later date. [https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-de-Ellsworthe/6000000059820037302?through=6000000059820713822 Thomas de Ellesworth] of the same place in 1292, [https://www.geni.com/people/Sir-John-Ells
2 CONC worth-Fictional/6000000003524043694?through=6000000006906990409 Sir John Ellsworth] of the same line in the early fourteenth century (who is claimed by some authorities to have been an ancestor of the emigrant [https://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/people/Sgt-Joseph-Ellsworth/6000000006906990409 Josiah or Josias].) … “. ('''Note: this pedigree is disproved'''.)
