1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Cummings Pease''' (1715–1808) was a prominent early resident of Enfield, Connecticut, and East Longmeadow, Massachusetts, known primarily for his role as a founding Deacon in the Baptist church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: He was born on November 15, 1715, in Enfield, Connecticut, to Isaac Pease and Mindwell Osborn.
2 CONT First Marriage: Around 1739, he married his second cousin once removed, Elizabeth Pease.
2 CONT Second Marriage: Around 1757, he married Sarah Hale of Springfield, Massachusetts.
2 CONT Settlement: He and his family settled at Fresh Water Brook, located approximately two miles east of Thompsonville.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Religious Leadership'''
2 CONT At approximately 30 years old, Cummings was "immersed" and became deeply involved in local religious life. He served as a Deacon and was one of the original founders of the Baptist Church located east of Enfield. This church event
2 CONC ually dissolved due to the influence of the Shaker Movement.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Baptist church where Cummings Pease served as a Deacon dissolved primarily due to mass conversion and leadership defection to the Shaker movement. 
2 CONT connecticuthistory.org
2 CONT  +1
2 CONT The dissolution was driven by several key factors:
2 CONT Conversion of the Pastor: In 1780, Joseph Meacham, the pastor of the Baptist church in Enfield, converted to Shakerism after hearing the teachings of Mother Ann Lee. Meacham was a "New Light" Baptist, a group that was already reli
2 CONC giously "fertile" for the radical, charismatic messages the Shakers provided.
2 CONT '''Follower Migration:''' When Meacham converted, he brought a significant portion of his congregation with him into the Shaker faith. This mass exodus of members effectively emptied the original Baptist church's pews and stripped
2 CONC  it of its community base.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Establishment of the Enfield Shakers:''' These former Baptists helped establish the Enfield Shaker Community in 1792. Joseph Meacham himself eventually became the lead administrator for all Shaker communities in America, organi
2 CONC zing their unique communal and celibate lifestyle.
2 CONT Theological Shifts: The Shakers offered a "purer" or more radical form of the spiritual enthusiasm that many New Light Baptists were already seeking, focusing on simplicity, gender equality, and "shaking" off sin during worship.
2 CONT Because the core leadership and a majority of the members joined the Shakers, the specific Baptist organization east of Enfield simply ceased to function.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Distinguishing from Relatives'''
2 CONT It is common to find him confused with his son, also named Cummings Pease (1744–1822), who was a Revolutionary War veteran. While the elder Cummings was a church leader, the younger Cummings served as a Private in the 10th Contine
2 CONC ntal Regiment and fought at the Siege of Boston.
2 CONT
2 CONT
