2 CONT !NAME:
2 CONT     Bygod/Beget
1 NOTE * will probated in Hartford Co. on 24 Oct 1674 
2 CONT * AKA: Bygod, Bigod, Begat, and Bagget
1 NOTE He was born in Settrington, Yorks. in 1586, then moved with his family about 1610 to Norwich, Norfolk, He was last recorded there in 1614. He next appears in 1630 in Dorchester, MA. But there seems to be no doubt that this is the 
2 CONC same man. Admitted as freeman 18 May 1631
2 CONT In 1635 he moved with other settlers to Windsor, CT  Admitted as freeman 21 May 1657 in Connecticut. In the Windsor land inventory in 1640 "Beggatt" Eggleston held five parcels, and he purchased several more.
2 CONT He signed his will "Beget Egleston." His inventory, taken 24 Oct 1674, came to £116 3s
2 CONT He was married twice, first by 1612, and second by about 1634, but the name of neither wife is known. The second one was living when he made his will.
1 NOTE Sometime before 1612, Bygod arrived at Norwich, Norfolk, England.He and his wife had four children who were born in England. They (with three of the four sons) sailed from Plymouth, England on the ship "Mary and John" on 20 Mar 16
2 CONC 30 just ahead of the Winthrop Fleet of 1630 and arrived 70 days later, on 30 May 1630, at Dorchester, MA., located at the mouth of what is now Boston Harbor. He was one of the founders  of Windsor, CT. in 1635.
2 CONT The three sons were James, John and Samuel. A daughter Mary, another son James, and his first wife,are assumed to have died before Bygod left England.
2 CONT After her death, he married again, a Mary(last name unknown). They had seven children, Thomas, Mercy (Marcy), Sarah, Rebecca (Deborah), Abigail, Joseph and Benjamin.
2 CONT   Bygod Eggleston was baptized on 20 February 1586, or possibly 1587 in All Saints Church at Settrington, East Riding, (now North Yorkshire) England. He died on 1 September 1674 at Windsor, Connecticut. His will was dated 13 Novem
2 CONC ber 1673. One of these persistent rumors is the claim that Bygod's maternal grandmother was a Bigod, this stemming from Bygod's unusual Christian name. While the family of Bigod had some importance in the 12th and 13th centuries a
2 CONC nd cadet branches of the Bigods held the manor of Settrington until 2 June 1537 when Sir Francies Bigod was put to death for treason, no link has been found of any blood relationship between Bygod Eggleston and the Bigod line. Som
2 CONC etime before 1612, Bygod Eggleston arrived at Norwich, Norfolk, England. He married and had four children who were born in England. He sailed to America on the ship "Mary and John", which sailed from Plymouth, England on 20 March 
2 CONC 1630 just a few days ahead of the Winthrop Fleet of 1630 and arrived seventy days later, on May 30, 1630, at Dorchester, Massachusetts, located at the mouth of what is now Boston harbor. The ship's captain refused to sail up the C
2 CONC harles river as planned because he feared running the ship aground in waters that he had no charts for. He instead left the passengers in a desolate locale miles from their intended destination. The settlors were forced to transpo
2 CONC rt 150,000 pounds of livestock, provisions and equipment 20 miles overland to their final destination. First settling in Dorchester, Massachusetts in 1630 and an original member of the Reverend Mr. Warham's church, Bygod Eggleston
2 CONC  was one of the founders of Windsor, Connecticut in 1635, which is at the confluence of the Farmington and Connecticut Rivers. Windsor is about six miles north of where the city of Hartford, Connecticut stands today.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Bygod Eggleston brought to the colonies with him three sons, James, John and Samuel. A daughter Mary, another son James, and his first wife, are assumed to have died before Bygod left England and little else is known about the
2 CONC m. He married again, Mary, and again, her surname is unknown. Together they had seven children; Thomas, Mercy (Marcy), Sarah, Rebecca (Deborah), Abigail, Joseph and Benjamin [my 6th Great Grandfather]. Bygod was fined 20 shillings
2 CONC  in 1645 for "bequeathing his wife to a young man." The young man, a George Tuckye, was fined 40 shillings, but he took off and didn't pay. Why Bygod thought he needed a young man for his wife is certainly questionable as Bygod wa
2 CONC s 67 years old when he fathered his youngest son, my 6th Great Grandfather Benjamin Eggleston. Bygod survived his second wife as well, and married for third and final time. Once again, her name is unknown. No known children came o
2 CONC f this marriage. Bygod Eggleston had at least 63 grandchildren and 144 great grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The will of Bygod Eggleston:
2 CONT             Hartford, Connecticut Probate Records, 1635-50,a digest of the early Connecticut Probate records, 1663 to 1677, Page 136 Name: Bygatt Eglestone Location: Windsor
2 CONT
2 CONT Invt. ?116-03-00. Taken 24 October, 1674, by Old William Trall, Thomas Dibble sen., Matthew Grant. Will dated 13 November, 1673:
2 CONT
2 CONT Bigod Eggleston (son of James Eggleston and Margaret Harker) was born 13 Feb 1585/86 in Settrington, Yorkshire, England, and died 1 Sep 1674 in Windsor, Hartford, CT. He married Mary E. (Unknown).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Notes for Bigod Eggleston:
2 CONT The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England, 1620-33
2 CONT Origin: Unknown
2 CONT Migration: 1630
2 CONT First residence: Dorchester
2 CONT Removes: Windsor 1635
2 CONT Church Membership: Bigod Eggleston is in Matthew Grant's list of those who had been members of the church in Dorchester, moved to Windsor with Warham, and remained in the church at Windsor [Grant 9].
2 CONT Freeman: Requested 19 October 1630 (as "Bigatt Egglestone") and admitted 18 May 1631 (as "Biggott
2 CONT Egglestone") [MBCR 1:80, 366]. Connecticut freeman, 21 May 1657 [CCCR 1:297].
2 CONT Education: Signed his will "Beget Egleston."
2 CONT Offices: Hartford jury, 8 July 1650 [RPCC 86].
2 CONT Estate: Granted "a lot on this side of the way going to Rocksbury," in Dorchester, 5 August 1633 [DTR 2]; "Thomas Geofry to have the lot [which] was Mr. Egelstone's by Mr. Hathorne," 2 June 1634 [DTR 6]; granted three acres "in th
2 CONC e neck behind his lot," 10 February 1634/5 [DTR 10]; granted Lot #52, four acres in the meadow beyond Naponset [DTR 321]. In the Windsor land inventory on 8 December 1640 "Beggatt Eggleston" held five parcels of land which had bee
2 CONC n granted to him: "an homelot with the addition eleven acres"; eleven acres of meadow; "over the Great River 25 rod in breadth, length 3 mile"; "near Paquannick 20 acres"; "in the palisado three-quarters of an acre" [WiLR 1:23r]. 
2 CONC He purchased other pieces of real estate (dates of the transactions unknown): of Rhoda Taylor, "her dwelling house, outhouses, garden with the land upon which it stands in the palisado a quarter of an acre ..., also in the Great M
2 CONC eadow six acres & fifteen rods ..., also across the Great River in breadth ten rods ... in length three miles ...[annotated "sold to Rich: Saxton], also adjoining to Rocky Hill eighteen acres ... [annotated "sold to Wm. Gayler the
2 CONC  elder"]; of land that was Eltweed Pomeroy's, one acre and a half in the palisado with the house on it; of George Hull "in the Great Meadow three acres & half" [annotated "sold to Hen: Clark"]; of William Gaylard the elder twenty 
2 CONC acres of land; and of William Hill "in the Great Meadow twelve acres" [WiLR 23r-22v]. On 4 March 1641 "Beggatt Eggleston of Windsor" sold to Thomas Thornton of the same twenty acres "on the south side of the rivulet near Paquannic
2 CONC ke" [WiLR 1:59]. On 8 November 1660 "Thomas Egglston of Windsor" sold to "my father Beggat Egglestone" six acres of land [WiLR 23v]. On [blank] March 1660 Richard Weller of Farmington sold to "Begat Eaggleston" two acres and a hal
2 CONC f of marsh [WiLR 23v]. In his will, dated 13 November 1673, "Bigat Egllstone of Windsor" bequeathed to "my son Benjamin" house and land (on the condition "that he shall maintain his mother and pay my debts"), to "my son Joseph" 40
2 CONC s. "should [he] come and demand a portion," to sons James, Samuel and Thomas and daughters Mary, Sarah and Abigail, 3s.
2 CONT apiece [Hartford PD Case #1890; Manwaring 1:194]. His inventory, taken 24 October 1674, totalled £116 3s. (against which were debts of £39 8s. 5d.), of which £74 was real estate: "his dwelling house & houseland 2 acres & half," £4
2 CONC 0; "in the Great Meadow six acres," £33; and
2 CONT "for poor land on the Sandy Plains 20 acres" £1 [Hartford PD Case #1890; Manwaring 1:194].
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: Baptized Settrington, Yorkshire, 20 February 1586[/7], son of "James Egeleston" [TAG 10:198]. (Rosalie Eggleston and Linda Eggleston McBroom have identified the mother of Bigod Eggleston as Margaret, daughter of Miles Harke
2 CONC r of Settrington [TAG 69:193-201].)
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: Windsor 1 September 1674 "near 100 year old" (but actually eighty-seven) [Grant 35; also CTVR 28].
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: (1) By 1612 _____ _____; not seen in any record.
2 CONT                  (2) By about 1634 _____ _____; on 5 June 1645 "Baggett Egleston, for bequeathing his wife to a young man, is fined 20s." [CCCR 1:127; RPCC 34]; living on 13 November 1673 when named in her husband's will.
2 CONT Children: With first wife:
2 CONT             i James, bp. St. Margaret's, Norwich, Norfolk, 14 April 1612; bur. there 20 August 1613.
2 CONT             ii Mary, bp. St. Margaret's, Norwich, Norfolk, 19 January 1613/4; no further record (but see comments below.)
2 CONT             iii James, b. say 1620 ; m. by 1656 Esther Kelsey, daughter of William Kelsey (eldest child b. Windsor 1 January 1656[/7?] [Grant 36; Manwaring 1:300]) [TAG 68:208-15]. After the death of her first husband Esther marri
2 CONC ed James Eno and then John Williams, so that (as George McCracken put it) "she was surnamed Williams at her death, not, as so often claimed, at her birth" [TAG 35:21].
2 CONT With second wife:
2 CONT            iv Samuel, b. say 1634; m. by 1663 Sarah Disborough, daughter of Nicholas Disborough (eldest child b. Middletown 6 March 1663; the inventory of "Nicholas Disbrowe," dated 31 August 1683, carried the names of his four ch
2 CONC ildren, including "Samuel Eglestone's wife" [Manwaring 1:298; TAG 38:209-10]).
2 CONT            v Mary, b. say 1636; m. Windsor 7 June 1655 John Denslow [Grant 33]. (He was not son of Nicholas Denslow.)
2 CONT            vi Thomas, b. Windsor 26 August 1638 [Grant 35]; no evidence that he married. Thomas Eggleston died in May 1697, and on 2 September 1697 the court ordered distribution to "Benjamin Eglestone, brother, and to the wife of
2 CONC  Jno. Pettebone senior of Simsbury, a sister of the deceased"; on 28 August 1701 distribution was made "to Benjamin Eglestone, to John Pettebone, to John, to Thomas, to Nathaniel, to Isaac Eglestone" [Manwaring 1:553-54].
2 CONT            vii Mercy, b. Windsor 29 May 1641 [Grant 35]; d. there 1657 [Grant 88; see also TAG 37:152 and NGSQ 57:255].
2 CONT            viii Sarah b. Windsor 28 March 1643 [Grant 35]; m. 16 February 1664 John Pettibone [Grant 62].
2 CONT            ix Rebecca, b. Windsor 8 December 1644 [Grant 35]; not named in father's will; no further record.
2 CONT            x Abigail, b. Windsor 12 June 1648 [Grant 35]; bp. there 18 June 1648; m. 14 October 1669 John Osborn [Grant 54].
2 CONT            xi Joseph, bp. Windsor 30 March 1651 [Grant 35]; in his will of 13 December 1673 his father
2 CONT bequeathed to Joseph 40s. "should [he] come and demand a portion," indicating that he lived at a distance. Stiles claims that Joseph married, resided in the area of North Stonington, and had three sons and "several daughters" [Win
2 CONC dsor Hist 199, 201]. One of these supposed sons had children born in the 1750s and later and another had children born in the 1770s and later; these are more likely descendants of a much later generation who have not been properly
2 CONC  located among the known branches of the family, and should not be credited to Joseph, for whom no record has been found beyond his father's conditional bequest.
2 CONT            xii Benjamin, b. Windsor 18 December 1653 [Grant 35]; m. Windsor 6 March 1678 Hannah Shaddock, daughter of John Osborn and widow of Elias Shaddock ("Elias Shadduck" died at Windsor 26 May 1676 leaving a wife and one chi
2 CONC ld, and John Osborn left a will dated 27 October 1686 naming daughter "Hanna" [surname not given] and "my grandchild Hana Shadduck" [Manwaring 1:232, 345]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Comments: Bigod Eggleston's migration pathway is one of the more interesting encountered among those who came to New England. He was born in Settrington, Yorkshire, in 1586, and then about 1610 accompanied or followed other member
2 CONC s of his family to Norwich, Norfolk. The last record for him at Norwich is the baptism of his daughter Mary on 19 January 1613/4. He then showed up in 1630 at Dorchester, which has led many to place him on the Mary & John. If he w
2 CONC ere on this ship, one would expect him to have come from one of the West Country counties of Devon, Dorset or Somerset, but we have no record for him anywhere from 1615 to 1630.
2 CONT Was the Yorkshire and Norfolk man the same as the Bigod Eggleston of Dorchester and Windsor? There is at this time no certain evidence, but the age of the New England man is in accord with the baptismal date in Settrington; the Yo
2 CONC rkshire-Norwich man had father and son named James while the Windsor man had son James; and no other man by the name of Bigod Eggleston has been found in England. A search for records for this man in Devon, Dorset and Somerset may
2 CONC  resolve this problem, and tie together the beginning and end of the career of Bigod Eggleston. In his record of "what children has been born in Windsor from our beginning hither to so far as I am able to find out," Matthew Grant 
2 CONC credited "Begat Egelston" with seven [Grant 90]. The claim has been made that the "Mary Egellston" who married at Watertown on 16 October 1645 Edward Sanderson was the daughter of Bigod Eggleston baptized in Norwich in 1614 [WaVR 
2 CONC 12], but this is unlikely for many reasons: the Mary baptized at Norwich would have been thirty at the time of this marriage; Bigod did not name her or any children of hers in his will; and the Egglestons of Windsor had no known c
2 CONC onnection with Watertown at this early date. Most accounts of the family state that James and Samuel were twins, but there is no evidence in favor of this, and few dates to help us in sorting out the chronology for these two men. 
2 CONC However, James Eggleston has an entry dated 8 December 1640 in the Windsor land inventory whereas Samuel does not; and the two men married about seven years apart. BIBLIOGRAPHIC NOTE: Clarence Almon Torrey published the basic info
2 CONC rmation on the Eggleston family in Yorkshire in 1934 [TAG 10:197-98]. This was supplemented by John G. Hunt, who pointed out the correct reading of the records for the daughter born in 1641, thus requiring that the daughter Mary n
2 CONC amed in the will must have been born earlier [TAG 37:151-53]. Genevieve Tylee Kiepura added some further comments in 1969 [NGSQ 57:255-61], expanding considerably On the clue provided by Torrey regarding the Egglestons in Norwich.
2 CONC  The most complete and extensive treatment of Bigod Eggleston was published in 1991 by Rosalie Eggleston and Linda Eggleston McBroom [Bygod Eggleston: Englishman & Colonist and Some of His Descendants (Toledo 1991)].
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical Dictionary of New England Settlers V2, p106
2 CONT Eggleston, Bagot, Bigod, Bigot or Begat, with sundry other variat. Dorchester, came, prob in the Mary and John, ask adm 19 Oct 1630 and was rec as freeman 18 May foll rem with first sett to Windsor, d. 1 Sep 1674, "near 100 yrs ol
2 CONC d" ays extrav tradit leav (p106) Samuel and James, both b. bef he rem and Thomas, b. 26 Aug 1638; Mary, 29 May 1641; Sarah, 28 Mar 1643; Rebecca, 8 DEc 1644; Abigail, 12 Jun 1648; Joseph, bp 30 Mar 1651 and Benjamin 18 Dec 1653. t
2 CONC he name of his w. is unkn but the Court rec of CT ment. that he was fin. 20sh. for bequeath. her to a young man in 1645, wh. (not the fine), must be regard. as joke. Abigail m. 14 Oct 1669 John Osborn. BENJAMIN, youngest s. of the
2 CONC  preced. m. 6 Mar 1678 Hannah, wid. of Elias Shadduck or Shattuck, d. of John Osborne, had Mary b. 2 Oct 1680; Sarah 1683; Abigail 1685; Benjamin 1687; Dorothy 1690; Joseph 1695 and Esther 1697. JAMES, Windsor, br. of the preced. 
2 CONC by w. Esther had James b. 1 Jan 167?, killed at Deerfield by the Ind. Sep 1675; John 27 Mar 1659; thomas 27 Jul 1661; Esther 1 Dec 1663; Nathaniel 15 Aug 1666; Isaac, 27 Feb 1669; Abigail 1 Sep 1671; Deborah 1 May 1674 and Hannah 
2 CONC 19 Dec 1676; and he d. 1 Dec 1679. He was made freem of CT 1657 at the same time with his f. and his br. Samuel, perhaps the elder, in yr. foll. His widow m. 29 Apr 1680 James Enno. SAMUEL, Middletown, br. of the preced. by w. Sar
2 CONC ah had Samuel, b. 6 Mar 1663; Thomas 4 Jun 1667 d. soon; Joseph 24 Jan 1669 d. soon; Sarah 26 Oct 1670; Susanna 19 May 1674; Nicholas 23 Dec 1676; Mercy 27 Jul 1679; Mary 1681 and Ebenezer 1684; d. Mar 1691, THOMAS, Windsor, br. o
2 CONC f the preced. freem. 1658, was of New London 1662 (poss more on the next page)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Bigod Eggleston and Mary E. (Unknown) are:
2 CONT +Benjamin Eggleston, b. 18 Dec 1653, Windsor, Hartford, CT653, d. 24 Nov 1732, Windsor, Hartford, CT.
2 CONT +Joseph Eggleston, b. ca. 30 Mar 1651 d. date unknown.
2 CONT   The progenitor of the Eggleston line in this country was Bygod Eggleston, who was baptized on 20 February 1586, or possibly 1587, in All Saints Parish at Settrington, East Riding, (now North Yorkshire) England, sailed to America
2 CONC  on the ship "Mary and John", which sailed from Plymouth, England on 20 March 1630 just a few days ahead of the Winthrop Fleet of 1630 and arrived seventy days later, on May 30, 1630.  First settling in Dorchester, Massachusetts i
2 CONC n 1630 and an original member of the Reverend Mr. Warham's church, Bygod Eggleston was one of the founders of Windsor, Connecticut in 1635, which is at the confluence of the Farmington and Connecticut Rivers. Windsor is about six 
2 CONC miles north of where the city of Hartford, Connecticut stands today.   Bygod Eggleston.deceased on 1 September 1674 at Windsor, CT.
2 CONT A story of the founding of Dorchester, MA., 1630
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Mary and John"
2 CONT
2 CONT By Maude Penney Kuhns
2 CONT "So we came by the good Hand of the Lord,
2 CONT through the deep comfortably."
2 CONT ---Roger Clap
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tuttle (?) Publishing Co., Inc.
2 CONT Rutland, Vermont, U.S.A.
2 CONT
2 CONT (pp. 42-46)
2 CONT
2 CONT "Part I
2 CONT The "Mary and John"
2 CONT
2 CONT On the twentieth of March, 1630, a group of men and women, one hundred and forty in number, set sail from Plymouth, England, on the good ship, the "Mary and John." The company had been selected and assembled largely through the ef
2 CONC forts of the Reverend John White, of Dorchester, England; with whom they spent the day before sailing, "fasting, preaching, and praying." These people had come from the western counties of England, mostly from Devonshire, Dorsetsh
2 CONC ire, and Somerset. They had chosen two ministers to accompany them: "men who were interested in the idea of bringing the Indians to the knowledge of the gospel." The Reverend John Maverick was an elderly man from Devon, a minister
2 CONC  of the Established church. Reverend John Warham was also an ordained minister of the church of England, in Exeter, eminent as a preacher. There is some evidence that both of these men were in some difficulties with the church on 
2 CONC account of their sympathies with the Puritans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Rossiter and Roger Ludlow, two men who were members of the government in England, were also chosen; and several gentlemen, middleaged, with adult families were next joined in the association. Among these were Henry Wolcott,
2 CONC  Thomas Ford, George Dyer, William Gaylord, William Rockwell, and William Phelps. But a large portion of the company were young men, eager for adventure, such as Israel Stoughton, Roger Clapp, George Minot, Richard Collicott, and 
2 CONC Nathaniel Duncan. 1 [Footnote 1: Clapp, Ebenezer: History of the Town of Dorchester, 1859, published by Ebenezer Clapp, Boston, Mass., pp. 17-18]
2 CONT
2 CONT So we came, writes Roger Clapp in his Memoirs, by the good Hand of the Lord, through the deep comfortably; having preaching or expounding of the word of God every day for ten weeks together by our ministers. When we came to Nantas
2 CONC ket, Capt. Squeb, who was Captain of that great ship of four hundred tons, put us on shore and our goods on Nantasket Point, and left us to shift for ourselves in a forelorn place in this wilderness.
2 CONT
2 CONT It had been their original intent to land in the Charles River, but a dispute with Captain Squeb, the commander of the vessel, caused the whole company, on May 30, 1630, to be put ashore at Nantasket. The "Mary and John" was the f
2 CONC irst of the Fleet of 1630 to arrive in the bay. At that time there could not have been pilots, or charts of the channel, and it does not seem unreasonable that the captain refused to undertake the passage, but Roger Clap has sent 
2 CONC Captain Squeb down to posterity as a merciless man. 2 [Note 2: "Stiles, Henry R.: History of Ancient Windsor, 1891, Case, Lockwood & Brainard Co., Hartford, Conn.]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to tradition they landed upon the south side of Dorchester Neck, or South Boston, in Old Harbor. Ten of the men, under the command of Captain Southcote, found a small boat, and went up the river to Charlestown Neck, wher
2 CONC e they found an old planter, probably Thomas Walfourd, who fed them "a dinner of fish without bread." Later they continued their journey up the Charles River, as far as what is now Watertown, returning several days later to the co
2 CONC mpany who had found pasture for their cattle at Mattapan. The settlement was later called Dorchester, in honor of the Reverend John White, of Dorchester, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Clap tells of the hardships that followed. They had little food, and were forced to live on clams and fish. The men built small boats, and the Indians came later with baskets of corn. "The place was a wilderness," writes Rog
2 CONC er Clap. "Fish was a good help to me and to others. Bread was so scarce that I though the very crusts from my father's table would have been sweet; and when I could have meal and salt and water built together, I asked, "who could 
2 CONC ask for better?"
2 CONT
2 CONT Here they lived for five or six years. Other boats arrived and other towns were settled. But the life at Dorchester was not entirely congenial to the lovers of liberty of the "Mary and John." The group of settlements around Massac
2 CONC husetts Bay was dominated by clergymen and officials of aristocratic tendencies. Their Governor, John Winthrop, had little sympathy with the common people. "The best part (of the people)," he declared, " is always the least, and o
2 CONC f that best part, the wiser is always the lesser." And the Reverend John Cotton put it more bluntly when he said, "Never did God ordain democracy for the government of the church or the people."
2 CONT
2 CONT These principles were repugnant to the people of the "Mary and John" who had come to America to escape such restraint. They had no wish to interfere with the methods of worship of others, and they did not wish others to interfere 
2 CONC with them. Too, they were land-hungry, after centuries of vassalage to the lords of the manors, leading hopeless lives without chance of independence. Perhaps they were influenced also, by the fact that a great smallpox epidemic h
2 CONC ad raged among the Indians, killing off so many that they were not the menace that they had been at first. The settlers turned their attention toward the fertile meadows of the Connecticut Valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT A group under Roger Ludlow set out and reached the Plymouth Trading house that had been erected by William Holmes near the junction of the Connecticut and the Farmington Rivers, early in the summer of 1635. A little later sixty me
2 CONC n, women and children, with their "Cows, heifers and swine," came overland from Dorchester. The winter was severe and the food scarce, and many returned to Massachusetts, but in the spring they came back to Connecticut with their 
2 CONC friends, and by April 1636, most of the members of the Dorchester Church were settled near the Farmington River, along the brow of the hill that overlooks the "Great Meadow". This in spite of the fact that the Plymouth people disp
2 CONC uted their claim to the land. They built rude shelters, dug out of the rising ground along the edge of the river bank. The rear end and the two sides were simply the earth itself, with a front and a roof of beams. The town was lat
2 CONC er named Windsor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following year, 1637, danger from the Pequot Indians forced them to abandon their dugouts and to come together around the area known as the Palisado Green. Their new homes were at once enclosed with a strong palisado.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1639 they began the construction of their first real meeting house. It stood in the center of the palisado, and was topped with a cupola and platform, where the sexton beat a drum to summon the people to attend services or publ
2 CONC ic meetings. About the same time was built and presented to the pastor, the Reverend John Warham, a corn mill, which is supposed to have been the first grist mill built in Connecticut. For many years it served all the settlements 
2 CONC in the river valley, as far south as Middletown.
2 CONT
2 CONT All over America today live the descendants of the fathers and mothers of the "Mary and John." Their sons and daughters have written their names on the pages of American History. They have filled the pulpits of famous churches; th
2 CONC ey have sat on judges' benches, and in the seats of Congress; they have occupied Governors' Mansions, and even the White House. Some fought at Lexington, and wintered with Washington at Valley Forge. They joined in the trek to the
2 CONC  West, and one followed Brigham Young into Utah. One marched with Sherman as he burned and pillaged his way through Georgia, and perhaps one fought on the other side with Lee . One is called the "Hero of Manila Bay," and one was h
2 CONC anged! They learned strange names like Saint-Mehiel, Chateau-Thierry, the Argonne Forest and Sedan. Perhaps one lies in Flanders Field.
2 CONT
2 CONT And even as this manuscript is being written, our boys are going again into strange lands: to Iceland, to Africa, and to Australia! One of our own correspondents wrote from Schofield Barracks in Hawaii.
2 CONT
2 CONT An effort has been made to show through the ancestry of people living today, or through famous men of history, how this little group lived together, married and intermarried, even beyond the third and fourth generations. The names
2 CONC  of descendants of the men and women who came to America on the "Mary and John" are found in every state of the Union."
2 CONT
2 CONT Source of the following page is unknown:
2 CONT
2 CONT "When Bigod Eggleston as well as the rest of the early pioneers saw fit to leave their homes in England, because of the religious oppression and venture into the unknown wilderness of America, they found conditions and hardships w
2 CONC e know little of today. It is my attempt here to relate some of these conditions, so that we can better appreciate our forefathers and honor their memory for what they achieved.
2 CONT
2 CONT For instance, in 1630, when Bigod arrived in Dorchester (Boston later) Massachusetts the forest extended like a jungle from the shores of the Atlantic Ocean to the Mississippi. The trees were so thick and large that a squirrel cou
2 CONC ld travel from the east coast to the Mississippi and never touch the ground, in fact, because of these trees there was no grass available for animal feed. Check out your local woods for grass you won't find any.
2 CONT
2 CONT It wasn't until 4 years after the Pilgrims arrived (1624) that three heifers and one bull were brought over from England. It was four years of no milk for anyone including babies and small children. It's little wonder they lost so
2 CONC  many children.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were also no bees for cross pollenation. They didn't arrive until 1638. A short time afterwards, during an Indian attack, one of the Indians knocked over a bee hive, making the bees very angry and the Indians very scarce. Th
2 CONC ey called them the Whitemans Flies. I guess the lack of bees shows why the Indians never became a real agricultural society. Poor crops.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were no horses, oxen or working cattle. In fact as to horses, there were very few in northern New England until after 1800. For many years, while cattle and horses were bred, and calves and colts were growing up, the first s
2 CONC ettlers in the wilderness, had to to depend almost entirely on human muscles. Actually the pioneers were pack animals with little but strong backs and stout hearts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Imagine, no salt, no roads, no real doctors, no medicine, no close neighbors, no pencils or pens (other than goose quills, very few tools and no matches.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, they did have wild animals, wild indians, work, insects, rattle snakes, home remedies, longevity, sickness, death and hernias and isolation and most of all much courage and determination.
2 CONT
2 CONT Home Remedies
2 CONT
2 CONT In the absence of doctors, the pioneers used various remedies and nostrums, most of which seem fantastic to us. "Drowning men clutch at straws." Many of their cures gained credit because sick folks often survived both the sickness
2 CONC  and the remedy. But in such cases the remedy got the credit. Then, too, many old-time herbs did have value. What were once the plants in a frontier woman's herb garden became, in some cases, the foundation of acknowledged worth i
2 CONC n modern medicine. For example, deadly nightshade and foxglove have blossomed out today as belladonna and digitalis. One old-time remedy for a bleeding cut was spider webs. they hastened the coagulation of blood. Another remedy fo
2 CONC r cuts and wounds was mouldy bread. Penicillin comes from a mold. Some very deadly diseases are cured without much "book larnin." Scurvy is scarcely heard of today, but in Yankee-land it was a deadly disease a hundred and fifty ye
2 CONC ars ago-and later. In the Civil War, fifteen per cent of all soldier deaths, not caused by battle wounds, were caused by scurvy. In the days of long voyages on sailing ships, members of the crew had nothing but a diet of sea busic
2 CONC uts and salt meat were very apt to come down with scurvy. A simple cure turned up, lemons or limes! British sailors used to be called "Limeys" as a result of drinking lime juice which contains ascorbic acid, or vitamin C.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lets us look at the materia medica of our ancestors;
2 CONT
2 CONT Roasted porcupine juice dropped in the ears would cure deafness.
2 CONT The gummy inner bark of slippery elm, mixed with gunpowder, was used as a poultis for rattlesnake bites.
2 CONT Potato bugs, killed in vinegar and dried in the sun, were good for blisters.
2 CONT A horse chestnut carried in the pocket would cure and prevent rheumatism.
2 CONT Warts-pierce with a red hot needle.
2 CONT A diet of skunk cabbage would cure asthma.
2 CONT A drink of vinegar was a sure cure for fainting.
2 CONT To cure a belly ache, spit under a stone. This made one stoop and brought up the gas.
2 CONT Shoes placed under the bed, soles up, would prevent nightmares.
2 CONT Dock leaf, cooked into a poultice, was a sure cure for cancer.
2 CONT A powder made from chickweed was also a sure cure for cancer.
2 CONT For consumption, inhale the smoke of burning mullein leaves.
2 CONT For a cold, take a very hot boneset, or penny royal, or catnip tea. This was also good for anyone tuckered out
