1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/103045390/person/360074711816 Ancestry Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 12 2017, 18:31:09 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==links==
2 CONT * https://images.findagrave.com/photos/2015/77/105819064_1426797875.jpg
2 CONT
2 CONT ==content to clean up==
2 CONT John OWEN - b. Dec. 25, 1624, Wales; d. Feb. 18 (or 1?), 1698/9, Windsor, CT. His gravestone by the Congregational Church in Windsor reads "John Owen aged 76 years dyed February ye 18th: 1698." However, Windsor town records indica
2 CONC te he was born Dec. 25, 1624, and died Feb. 1, 1698/9. John may have been at New Haven, CT in 1642, but was certainly at Windsor in 1650 when he purchased a lot 12 rods by 29 rods, and married. He signed a petition to establish Ki
2 CONC llingworth in 1663, was sexton of the meeting house in 1666, was paid for clapboarding for the meeting house in 1668, and contributed to the poor in 1676. One source reports he was the son of Richard OWEN and Johanna PITT. He marr
2 CONC ied Oct. 3, 1650 at Windsor, CT.
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration: before 1650 When his marriage is recorded, possibly in New Haven as early as 1642.
2 CONT
2 CONT Freeman: in 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT Founder/Proprietor: Windsor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Church/Religion: First Congregational Church, Windsor Conn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cemetery: Palisado Cemetery, Windsor; Stone, N41°51.520', W72°38.379.'
2 CONT
2 CONT The birthplace of John Owen has not been discovered but according to Windsor tradition he was born in Wales. His tombstone claims a birth year of 1622, but in a deposition he had made some years before his death, he reports his bi
2 CONC rth as 25 December, 1624. One source reports he was the son of Richard Owen and Johanna Pitt. He married Rebecca Wade of Hartford October 3, 1650 at Windsor, CT. The earliest Windsor records are lost and our first record of John O
2 CONC wen is his 1650 marriage. In the same year he became a member of the church and was made a freeman. He may be the same John Owen identified in New Haven in 1642.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1650 he bought a lot in Windsor 12 X 29 rods on the south side of the road leading west from palisado and bounded on the east by Hosford's Lane. About twenty years later he removed to a lot granted at the lower end of Strawberr
2 CONC y Meadow at a location formerly known as Wales, from John Owen's nationality.
2 CONT
2 CONT The records of the Probate Court contain the following statement about the estate of John Owen:
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Inventory taken by Lieutenant Hayes and Benajah Holcomb 5 April 1699 and found to be worth £29. Exhibited in court, being only personal estate and that given to the widow to be at her disposal, the children consenting."
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory included four coats and four pairs of britches, a powdering barrel, books and bottles, pewter platter, dishes, spoons and ladle, brass cast skillet and brass kettle, table cloths and a dozen napkins.
2 CONT
2 CONT John's meager estate does not reflect his true position. Although he was not particularly affluent, he did own lands which he disposed of shortly before his death in conveyances in 1695 and 1697 to his sons, Obadiah and Isaac. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There are conflicting sources as to his origin, that he may have been born as early as 1622. John came to the English colonies as a young man and settled in Connecticut by the 1640's. John was married on October 3, 1650 to Rebecca
2 CONC  Wade. They settled in Windsor and had the following children: Josiah, John, John, Nathaniel, Daniel, Joseph (see next generation), Mary, Benjamin, Rebecca, Obadiah and Isaac.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a farmer and a prominent early citizen of Windsor, Connecticut. He died there on February 18, 1698/9 and is buried in the burial ground behind the First Congregational Church. His widow Rebecca died in 1711.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Owen, immigrant ancestor, was born Dec 25,1624, died 1 Feb 1698.  He was of Welsh Ancestry and came from England or Wales to Windsor, Connecticut, making his home at a place called Hosford's Lane.  Afterward he removed farthe
2 CONC r north on the Farmington river to a place called Polly's Orchard on the opposite side of the stream.  
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Windsor town records show John Owen's presence there as early as 1650. In the town land records are entered a number of conveyances of land to John Owen and from him to his sons and others. His name is on the list of those who mad
2 CONC e voluntary contributions to the poor of other Colonies in 1676. The inscription on his gravestone in the Congregational Church cemetery at Windsor reads: "John Owen aged 76 years dyed February ye 18th: 1698." However, the town re
2 CONC cords state that he was born 25 Dec. 1624, and died 1 Feb. 1898/9, at age 76.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John OWENS
2 CONT b: 25 Dec 1624 in Wales
2 CONT m: 03 Oct 1650 in Windsor, Ct
2 CONT d: 01 Feb 1698/99 in Windsor, Hartford, Co Ct-
2 CONT
2 CONT From Savage:
2 CONT
2 CONT >OWEN, JOHN, Windsor, said to be b. 25 Dec. 1624, m. 3 Oct. 1650, Rebecca Wade, perhaps d. of Robert of Hartford, had Josias, b. 8 Sept. 1651; John, 5 Nov. 1652; d. soon; John, again, 23 Apr. 1654, d. at 16 yrs. ; Nathaniel, 9 Apr
2 CONC . 1656; Daniel, 28 Mar. 1658; Joseph, 23 Oct. 1660; Mary, 5 Dec. 1662; Benjamin, 20 Sept. 1664, d. soon; Rebecca, 28 Mar. 1666; Obadiah, 12 Dec. 1667; and Isaac, 27 May 1670; was freem. 1667, and rem. to Simsbury, and d. 1 Feb. 16
2 CONC 99. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
