2 DATE ABT 1804
2 PLAC Hillsdale, Columbia Co, NY
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William West. William West (1717-1804)[10] moved from Windsor, Hartford County, Connecticut to New York—into the same geographical regions as Elijah.  William West leased land in at least three patents. In 1740, he
2 CONC  farmed in the Loonenburg Patent of (today’s) Greene County. His first three children were baptized in at Zion Lutheran Church in Athens, New York. [11] In 1752, their last child, David, was born in Upper Amenia, New York, [12] wh
2 CONC ich was in the Great Nine Partners Patent of Dutchess County. In 1804, William West died in Hillsdale, New York. This was located within the Van Rensselaer Manor.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Will of William West, dated 30 Apr 1789, states: “. . . The other children to receive their portions as stated from the value of the dwelling and adjacent lands (the messuage) of my tenement.” [13] West Family genealogist Beat
2 CONC rice West Seitz says, “This last statement confirms the fact that William was a tenant farmer on the Van Rensselaer estates.” [14]
2 CONT
2 CONT * 11. William S. Pellatreau, 1884: “Athens,” History of Greene County, New York with Biographical Sketches of its Prominent Men, J.B. Beers and Company, New York City, New York, p. 177 and 179.  
2 CONT * 12. David West, Private, New York Line. Pension certificate No. 14615, Columbia County, New York, 1 June 1833. Hannah West, widow’s pension certificate No. 16464, Yates County, New York, 26 Aug 1836. National Archives and Record
2 CONC s Administration, Washington, DC. Digital images on HeritageQuest.com. Accessed November 2013. The soldier states that he was born in Upper Amenia, Dutchess County, New York.
2 CONT * 13. The Will of William West dated 30 April 1789, Book C, page 21, Book of Wills, Columbia County, New York. Cited by Seitz (1971).
2 CONT * 14. Seitz, p. 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://westproj.blogspot.com/2015/08/elijah-west-1722-1798-connecticut-new.html
