2 CONT '''Please see an excellent [http://www.tributaries.info/p1747.htm#i17461 Tributaries.info] research page. A copy of it is posted in Sources in the even the tributaries website removes the page or the website goes defunct.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ====[https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Brooks-8075 from ''Wikitree'']====
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel/2g Brooks, born 6 Sep 1662 at Windsor, Connecticut,[2,1], was mentioned in his father's inventory, Sep 1682.[3]  His parents removed to Simsbury, where he married, by 1690 (Torrey), Sarah ?Robbins.[4,5]
2 CONT
2 CONT >They had at Glastonbury:[1]
2 CONT >1. Sarah, b. January 8 1693[/4?]
2 CONT >2. John, b. 8 March 1695[/6?]
2 CONT >3. Samuel, b. 27 May 1697
2 CONT >4. Mary, b. 6 Februray 1699[/700?]
2 CONT >5. Timithy, b. 28 October 1701
2 CONT >6. Ledda, b. 23 May 1704
2 CONT >7. Hannah, b. 29 September 1706
2 CONT >8. Abigill, b. 4 May 1708
2 CONT >9. Elizabeth, b. 24 January 1711[/12?]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''He removed to Glastonbury''', perhaps in 1692, when he received a grant of 50 acres of land there.[6] [''Glastonbury was founded in 1693, so it's probable his family was the first or one of the first families to settle Glasto
2 CONC nbury..]''
2 CONT * "'''In 1712''' permission was given to Gershom Smith, Thomas Hollister, Jonathan Judd, Samuel Brooks, Ebenezer Kilbourn, and Thomas Kimberly 'to build a saw-mill on the northernmost branch of Roaring Brook' at Wassuc, and confir
2 CONC med to the above with John Kilbourn and Joseph Tryon a year or two later. The site of this mill now [1886] occupied by the mill of the Roaring Brook Paper Manufacturing Company."[7]
2 CONT * '''In 1719''', Samuel Brooks was taxed £18 on 86 acres "near Assawassuc."[8]
2 CONT * '''5 Dec 1721''', administration of the will of Susannah Brooks of Wethersfield was given to brother Samuel Brooks of Glastonbury.9] 
2 CONT * '''Jan 1721/2,''' his account of the administration of Susanna's estate was allowed, and he received £5-19-08½ in the subsequent distribution.[9]
2 CONT
