1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A085238
2 CONT
2 CONT John Owen, of which the following is an abstract:  
2 CONT
2 CONT >He was a native of Windsor, Conn., and a soldier in the French Wars and in the war of the Revolution. He came with his family from the Susquehanna river, to Warren, Pa., about the year 1806, and up the Conewango in 1808. After se
2 CONC veral removals he settled on the east side of the Conewango adjoining the state line where he resided 25 or 30 years and kept a tavern or house of entertainment. He also kept a private ferry for those wishing to cross the Conewang
2 CONC o previously to the building of the bridge. He is said to have been a very witty man. He was with the English army in the attack on Quebec in the old French War, and was a pensioner for service in the American army in the Revoluti
2 CONC onary War. He died in Carroll, Feb. 6, 1843, aged 107 years, according to the records of Windsor, his native town. Ira Owen, a son of John Owen by his third wife, came with his father to Conewango and settled on the land east of h
2 CONC is father. He was a soldier in the War of 1812. Reuben, the youngest son, lived with his father until his father's death and continued to live on the old homestead. John Owen was taxed in Conewango Township, Warren County, Pa., 18
2 CONC 06 to 1809 inclusive. In 1810 he is listed as gone. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >He and his wife Lydia Gilson had the following children: 1 Betsey, b. March 23, 1788. 2. Elsey, B. July 8, 1790. 3. Ira, b. July 16, 1793. 4. Phebe, b. June 23, 1795. 5. Reuben, b. July 12, 1800. 6. Sally, B. Oct. 20, 1802.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT LifeSketch:	
2 CONT *This is NOT the John Owen Sr married to Joanna Nash (ID#LHN4-G2R). Please DO NOT merge them.
2 CONT * His cousin, John Owen "the first", son of Joshua, was a few years older. Hence the special designations. SOURCE p.69-71: https://archive.org/details/descendantsofjoh00owen/page/69/mode/1up
2 CONT * bef 1750 - both families settled in Salisbury, Litchfield, CT
2 CONT * he served in the French & Indian War in Col. Hinman's Fourth Regiment that marched to Ticonderoga, arriving there just after Col. Ethan Allen had captured it.
2 CONT * 1775 served in the Revolutionary War from Salisbury; enlisted in the spring under Capt. Nathaniel Buel, Col. Hinman's Regiment, and served 8 months.
2 CONT * May 1776 he was impressed for 6 mos. as a private under Capt. Roger More, Col. Gay's Regiment, fighting against "cowboys" (Tory marauders).
2 CONT * Oct 1777 he served 4 wks. under Capt. Buel, Col. Whitney's Regiment, on the North River...
2 CONT * 1790 they settled at Hudson, Columbia, NY
2 CONT * 1798 settled Delaware Cty., NY
2 CONT * 1806 they moved by ox-cart through the Susquehanna Valley to western PA. He was listed on Tax List for Conewango Township, Warren, PA 1806-08.
2 CONT * 1809 he bought land in Pomfret Township (near Poland), Chautauqua Co., NY and settled there 1810
2 CONT * 1816 he sold his farm & opened a tavern on the east side of Conewango Creek, near Fentonville, Carroll Township. He also operated Owen's Ferry across the creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT * https://archive.org/details/descendantsofjoh00owen/page/70/mode/1up
2 CONT * "Connecticut Marriages, 1630-1997", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:F7LW-CX7 : 11 January 2020), Johanna Phelps in entry for John 2d Owen, 1768.
