1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ancestor #: A087251
2 CONT
2 CONT Patriotic Service
2 CONT
2 CONT MA SOLS & SAILS, VOL 12, P 36; BURNS, ABSTRACTS OF LAND RECS OF ALBANY CO NY 1774-1797 & TAX LISTS FOR 1779, P 631
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) SECOND LT & ENS, CAPT STEPHEN PEARL, COL BENJAMIN RUGGLES WOODBRIDGE, 25TH REGT
2 CONT 2) PAID SUPPLY TAX, 1779
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife # 1 Elizabeth Farrington  Marriage
2 CONT JAN 1763
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife # 2 Louisa/Lovicy Allen b. 1736 Married Canaan, Columbia, NY
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife # 3 Elizabeth Coggeshell 1745-1845
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Abner Pease (1733-84) served as ensign and as lieutenant under Capt. Stephen Pearl and Col. Benjamin Woodbridge, New York troops. Was also a patriot. He was born in Enfield, Connecticut; died in Stephentown, New York.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT wiikitree.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT Abner Pease
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 2 Mar 1733 in Enfield, Hartford, Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Isaac Pease and Amee (French) Pease
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Emma Pease, Isaac Pease, Phebe Pease, Jacob Pease, Ann (Pease) Hall, Noadiah Pease and	Luraina (Pease) Gains
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Elizabeth (Farrington) Pease — married 25 Jan 1763 in Somers, Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Abner Pease, Daniel Pease, John Pease, James Pease, Samuel Pease, Sally (Pease) Hayes and Sybil (Pease) Powers
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 1784 in Stephentown, Rensselaer, New York, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Account of David & Austin S. Pease (1869)
2 CONT
2 CONT 122. CAPT. ABNER PEASE,5 (Isaac,4 Isaac,3 John,2 Robert,1) third son of ISAAC and AMY (FRENCH) PEASE, was born at Enfield, 1733; married Elizabeth Farrington, January, 1763. [Somers Town Records.] He married, second, Lovisa Allen.
2 CONC  It appears he first settled in Somers, Ct., but subsequently removed to the eastern part of the State of New York. He held the office of deputy sheriff, in what was the county of Albany, in 1777, and came near losing his life in 
2 CONC the discharge of his duty, while apprehending riotous Tories. He joined the Shakers, which congregated near New Lebanon, N. Y., in 1780; and died in that connection at his residence in Stephentown, N. Y., in 1784, of small pox, ag
2 CONC ed about 51 years.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Account of Frederick S. Pease (1847)
2 CONT
2 CONT Then there was an Abner Pease* and Lovicy his wife, and perhaps three children, who had removed to or lived in Stephentown, near New Lebanon, before that Society began to be organized. Abner and Lovicy were both sound, true hearte
2 CONC d and zealous Shakers, till the day of their deaths; but the children, being in opposition to the faith of their parents, like Timothy's son Jonathan, "took their own heads" and heels too. Abner died at his residence in Stephentow
2 CONC n, of the small-pox, some time in the year 1784, aged forty-five; and Lovicy died at New Lebanon, some time in the year 1788, aged thirty-seven.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Abner was probably one of the four sons of Isaac, first son of Isaac who was the sixth and youngest son of John who came to Enfield in 1679.[3] Account of Rick in Taunton, MA
2 CONT
2 CONT Abner, son of Isaac (Isaac, John, Robert) and Amy (French) Pease; b. Mar 12, 1733 in Enfield, CT; d. 1784 in Stephentown, NY of small pox; m. Jan 25, 1763 in Somers, CT, Elizabeth Farrington; b. Oct 07, 1744 in Somers; d. 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Edward Farrington, a carpenter; b. Jul 09, 1699 in Andover, MA, and Ruth Killam; b. Apr 24, 1709 in Enfield, CT. Edward and Ruth were married on Jun 26, 1728. She was also the sister of Patience Farrington,
2 CONC  who married Asahel Sexton. Asahel and Patience were the parents of Prudence and Esther Sexton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Abner and Elizabeth (Farrington) Pease, 1-3 born in Somers, CT; 4-6 in NY; 7 & 8 in Canaan, NY: Abner b. Apr 26, 1763 d. Jun 26, 1836 Aurora, OH Sybil b. 1767 d. ca. 1847 Cooperstown, NY James b. Oct 14, 1771 d. May 25
2 CONC , 1813 Worcester, NY Daniel b. ca. 1773 d. ca. 1774 Somers, CT John b. ca. 1775 d. 1804 Somers, CT Samuel b. ca. 1776 d. Sally b. ca. 1778 d. Elizabeth b. ca. 1780 d. May 08, 1844 Longmeadow, MA Abner Pease m. (2) Lovicy Allen; b.
2 CONC  about 1751; d. 1788 in Stephentown, NY. He and his second wife joined the Shaker Society of New Lebanon, NY in 1780. It was reported that one of his sons was so opposed to the Shaker Society, that when he contracted small pox, he
2 CONC  purposely brought it into the family household, resulting in the death of Abner and Lovicy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1777, During the Revolutionary War, Abner Pease was a deputy sheriff in Albany County. On the way home from Albany, he and a Mr. Sheldon and Mr. White had stopped in a tavern owned by Nicholas Mickle. Mr. White, a Justice, orde
2 CONC red Pease to arrest a band of Tories who were creating a disturbance in the tavern.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tories surrendered, and were allowed to go upstairs to retrieve their belongings. They came down, armed with muskets, and a fight took place, Abner Pease was wounded in the thigh, and received a gash on the head from a cutlass
2 CONC , which scarred him for life. The Tories robbed the three, and took them as hostages into the woods. During the ordeal, Pease fainted from loss of blood. A compassionate Tory, John Sloss bound up his wounds with a piece of his own
2 CONC  shirt, and left Pease in the woods. He was discovered by a Dutch farmer looking for his cows several hours later. The farmer took Pease into his house, nursed him back to health, and lent him a horse to pursue the Tories. They we
2 CONC re captured, and all but John Sloss, who was released after giving witness against them, were hung in Albany. One of the executed was Robert Sloss. The judges at the trial were Richard Morris, Robert Yates, and John Sloss Hobart. 
2 CONC Abner Pease was later a Captain of a volunteer military company, formed in Albany County at the time of the Boston Tea Party.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
