1 NOTE {geni:about_me} REVOLUTIONARY WAR VETERAN:   Joined Col. Hinman's 4th Connecticut Regiment raised on the first call for troops April-May 1775, and recruited mainly in Litchfield County.  Upon the suprise of Fort Ticonderoga, May 1
2 CONC 0, Governor Trumbull ordered this regiment to march as soon as possible to secure that post and Crown Point from recapture.  A request to this effect was also made by the Continental Congress.  The regiment reached Ticonderoga in 
2 CONC June, and Col. Hinman assumed command until the arrival of Gen. Schuyler.  The regiment took part in the operations of the Northern Department until expiration of term of service in December 1775.  (Ref. P.59, The Record of Connec
2 CONC ticut Mn in the Military and Naval Service during the Revolutionary War). 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Connecticut military pay order (pictured on this site), signed and authorized by Payroll Committeemen, Ezekiel Williams and Oliver Ellsworth, and Treasurer John Lawrence, on April 15th 1776 paid ...
2 CONT
2 CONT "Doct. Abel Catlin, Surgeon 4th Reg.t raised May 1775 - Sum of Forty Eight pounds Three Shillings & Eleven pence - and charge the colony April 15 1776"
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Abel Catlin joined as a "Surgeon's Mate"  and was promoted to and paid as a Surgeon.
2 CONT
2 CONT U.S. PENSION NO.: "S3",  ref.: Ancestry.com
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref. "Rolls and Lists of Connecticut Men in the Revolution 1775 - 1783", Connecticut Historical Society:
2 CONT
2 CONT Abel Catlin is listed on the Marching Roll of Capt. Griswold's Company, Third Battalion - Colonel Enos, March 4, 1777.  The Torrington and Cornwall men marched eighty-five miles, and the Litchfield men marched seventy-five before 
2 CONC reaching the army, on which account the former received seven shillings and one pence, and the latter six shillings and three pence as traveling expenses.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT  THE DANBURY ALARM - APRIL 25th-28th 1777:  (Ref. Tryon's raid against Danbury, P. 128) The militia gathered hurriedly on the occasion - DR. CATLIN'S DUTY listed as "HOSPITAL".  (Ref. P.492, The Record of Connecticut Men in the Mi
2 CONC litary and Naval Service During the Revolutionary War).
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT DR. ABEL CATLIN -  
2 CONT
2 CONT Served in the American Revolutionary war as a surgeon with the 4th Connecticut Regiment, northern campaign: Fort Ticonderoga, Crown Point, Montreal, organized in May, right after the Battle of Lexington, marching from June 1775 an
2 CONC d returned the following December . Served from March 1777 under Col. Roger Enos commander of the Conn. Regiments stationed in the south-west corner of the state for the defense around the coast villages of Greenwich and Stamford,
2 CONC  quartered thereabout to guard the frontier and protect the residents from British incursions.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Dorothy Seymour and they had the following children:  Ref.  www.oxfordpast.net/oxf7617.html
2 CONT                                                                                                                                   Oxford, New Haven, CT
2 CONT    Dennis Catlin  b. 14 Mar. 1777;
2 CONT    Polly Catlin;
2 CONT    Betsey Catlin;
2 CONT    Dolly Catlin;
2 CONT    Harry Catlin  b. c.1785;
2 CONT    Mary Catlin;
2 CONT    Nabby Catlin;
2 CONT    Baron Catlin;
