2 CONT
2 CONT "Turkey Hills" was never and is not now a Connecticut town. It was one of four ecclesiastical societies in Simsbury township, in the hills lying in present-day East Granby. The Turkey Hills Ecclesiastical Society in 1786 became a 
2 CONC section of Granby, and in 1858 was incorporated as the Town of East Granby. Turkey Hills is therefore appropriately noted as a section of Simsbury township until 1786, and then a section of East Granby after 1858. Some information
2 CONC  from SimsburyHistory.org: "[Mr.] Higley held many town offices, was the first captain of Simsbury's militia, the "Traine Band," and active in the start up '''of the Turkey Hill copper mines in present day East Granby'''... [https
2 CONC ://simsburyhistory.org/simsbury-history-2/ Simsbury History]. – (Ken Shelley; Granby, CT)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=18472137
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Gillet was born on August 2, 1693 in Turkey Hills, Simsbury, Hartford County, Connecticut. His parents were Nathan Gillet and Rebecca Owen. Isaac married Elizabeth Griswold on October 29, 1719 in Windsor, Hartford County, Co
2 CONC nnecticut. He died on January 2, 1765 in Turkey Hills.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
