1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''Transcript:''' Savage, James. ''Genealogical Dictionary of the First Settlers of New England'', Vol. 1, p. 346 @[https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Transcript:Savage%2C_James._Genealogical_Dictionary_of_the_First_S
2 CONC ettlers_of_New_England/v1p346 WeRelate.org]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Pelatiah Case:''' 1 April 1734 probate in Simsbury. It’s exceedingly brief:
2 CONT ** “Jonah Case, a brother. Recog., with Samuel Case. Estate insolvent.” — This is the only evidence for Pelatiah. See ''A Digest of the Early Connecticut Probates'' @[https://www.google.com/books/edition/A_Digest_of_the_Early_Conn
2 CONC ecticut_Probat/0P8LAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA148&printsec=frontcover Google Books].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Origin of the Name "Pelatiah"'''
2 CONT Pelatiah is a biblical figure mentioned in the book of Ezekiel as a prince of Israel who opposed the prophet's messages and died as a consequence of his actions. He is also noted as a son of Hananiah from the line of David and a c
2 CONC aptain of the Simeonites in their battle against the Amalekites.
