1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Henry of Springfield, son of Benjamin, wa sborn May 19, 1700 and married Elizabeth Brookes of Suffield, February 18, 1721, and after he death, probably Jane _________.  In the records of Springfield (Volume 1, p 15
2 CONC 6 of Marriages) is is style Henry Wright, Junior.  This has led to some confusion since it would imply that his father's name was Henry.  The record of Henry, his uncle, som of Abel is here given to show that he could not have com
2 CONC e from that branch:  Henry, son of Abel of the same place, was born January 6, 1677, married first in 1705 to Hannah, youngest daughter of John Bliss of the same place, had Hannah, born May 18, 1706; Henry, born January 9, 1708 wh
2 CONC o was killed July 26 following by the Indians who at the same time carried away the mother, Hannah, and soon killed her.  He married second in 1711, Sarah Root, probably daughter of Thomas, of Westfield; had Moses,        , Stephe
2 CONC n in 1716, Caleb in 1718, Slisha, 1720, Sarah in 1723, and Deborah, probably earlier.  His will of 1760 was probated in 1769.  It names children Hannah, Deborah, Sarah, Moses, Stephen, Caleb and Elisha.  It will be noted that this
2 CONC  Henry was first married in 1705, while Henry, Jr. was married in 1721, sixteen years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT One author (Stephen Wright of Glenn Falls, 1863) gives Elizabeth Brookes as the third wife of Henry, son of Abel.  This could not be so, because children were born to Sarah Root after the marriage of Elizabeth Brookes.  Moreover, 
2 CONC the Parish records of the Springfield church show Mrs. Henry Wright and Mrs. Henry Wright, Jr. on the rolls between 1736 and 1738 in full communion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burt, in his History of Springfield, volume 2, gives Elizabeth Brookes as the wife of Henry, the son of Benjamin, the son of Abel.
2 CONT
2 CONT The only other Henry located is in Dr. Savage's Genealogical Dictionary; he is Henry, born in 1708, son of Henry, the son of Abel.  Here is a Henry, Jr. but Dr. Savage says he was killed by the Indians in 1708 when about six month
2 CONC s old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Wright, Jr. retains the 'junior' in the record of deeds from him and in the records of his marriage and of the births of his children to him and Elizabeth.  He evidently took this designation to distinguish him as a junion j
2 CONC usbandman.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the office of the Recorder of Deeds at Springfield, Mass., there is arecord in Book M at p 456, of a deed from Henry Wright in which he styles himself as Junior Husbandman and which he signs "Henry Wright, Junr."  In that deed 
2 CONC he conveys land "which was my Honored Father's Benjamin Wright State of Springfield aforesaid deceased."  This deed is dated April 16, 1725, in the eleventh year of King George.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Book F at p 206 is a deed signed by Benjamin Wright and Henry Wright, Jr. and Mary Wright the wife of Benjamin Wright and Elizabeth Wright the wife of Henry Wright, Jr.  This deed is dated November 3, 1725, the twelfth year of 
2 CONC the reign of King George.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Book M page 154 is a deed dated October24, 1740 and signed by Henry Wright Jr. and Jane Wright.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hence we are justified in concluding that Henry, Jr. was Henry, son of Benjamin.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT After the death of Elizabeth, Henry married second to Jane Doolittle on 19 May 1739.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Henry arrived in Dorchester, Massachusetts (presumably from England) in 1634. About 1643 he migrated to Roger Williams's more tolerant colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations.
2 CONT --------------------
