1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.avocadoridge.com/carney/getperson.php?personID=I21327&tree=tree1
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: William Alderman was born about 1640, parentage unknown. It is not known whether he was born in Colonial Connecticut or was an immigrant from England. He is first found in 1672 in Windsor where he made a land transactio
2 CONC n. It was also in Windsor that he married Mary Case. William and Mary lived in Windsor and later in Farmington, and even later in Simsbury. Both Mary and William are buried in Simsbury. After the death of William the younger gener
2 CONC ation moved to New York and New Jersey and other parts of the eastern coast. Many of them moved into southeastern North Carolina, settling in New Hanover, Duplin and Sampson Counties. From there, they dispersed throughout the sout
2 CONC hern states with many of them settling in "Wiregrass Georgia
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Alderman-300
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William Alderman and Mary Case:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mary Alderman b. 22 Sep 1680[1]
2 CONT * Thomas Alderman Jr b. 9 Jun 1683[1]
2 CONT * John Alderman b. 1684[1]
2 CONT * William Alderman Jr. b. 10 Oct 1686[1]
2 CONT * Sarah Alderman b. 1691[1]
2 CONT * Joseph Alderman b. 30 Jun 1697[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * Parker, William Alderman, 1879-. Aldermans In America. Raleigh, N. C.: Edwards & Broughton Co., 1957. p. 20+ [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89062953252&view=1up&seq=42 Hathitrust]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Alderman's were in Middlesex, about London, Sussex, Kent, near Canterbury, Sommerset, Hands, Suffolk and Norfolk counties. A more important cluster was near Kettering in Northampton County where they intermarried with many promine
2 CONC nt families.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a considerable disagreement about the identity of William's father. One possibility is Thomas Alderman, born about 1594. This should explain why William named his son Thomas Jr. It was not uncommon to attach this suffix t
2 CONC o a child named of a grandfather or uncle. It is believed that Thomas' father was Robert Alderman, a cloth worker, born about 1568 in Ipswich, Suffolk County, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some believe that William was born in Hartford Connecticut, however, the US and Canada Passenger and Immigration Lists Index from 1500's-1900's, has a William Alderman arriving in Connecticut in 1671. All the parental possibilitie
2 CONC s were from England, So we think that the Alderman's, from which we are descended, were from there.
2 CONT
2 CONT For more information, refer to Alderman's in America by William Alderman Parker.
