1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=59267476
2 CONT
2 CONT From Find a Grave:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Alderman is the son of William Alderman and Mary Case. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born 11 Jun 1683 or 9 Jan 1683. He married Mary Seagrave on the 27th of Apr 1702 at East Hampton, Suffolk, Long Island, New York. Their children were:
2 CONT * 1. Infant born (1704 died 31 Mar 1704)
2 CONT * 2. Mary Alderman (1705 - )
2 CONT * 3. Thomas Alderman (1707 - 1759)
2 CONT * 4. William Alderman (1709 - 1759)
2 CONT * 5. Daniel Alderman (1711-Oct 1785)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikitree: https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Alderman-299
2 CONT
2 CONT As recorded in the Book of Wills, Liber II, page 37 at the State House, Trenton, N. J.
2 CONT
2 CONT "IN THE NAME OF GOD, AMEN: the thirteenth day of August 1715, I Thomas Alderman, of Cohansey, Weaver, being very sick and weak in body, but of perfect mind the mortality of my body and knowing that it is appointed for all men once
2 CONC  to die, do make and ordain this my last will and testament, that is to say, principally and first of all -- I give and recommend my soul into the hands of God that gave it, and my body I recommend to the earth, to be buried in de
2 CONC cent Christian burial at the discretion of my executrix and executor, nothing doubting but at the General Resurrection I shall receive the same again, by the mighty power of God. "And as touching such worldly estate wherewith it h
2 CONC as pleased God to bless me in this life with, I give, demise and dispose of the same in the following manner and form. "Imprimis: I give and bequeath to Mary my dearly beloved wife, all my movable estate and the use of all my land
2 CONC  and housing appurtenances thereunto belonging in Cohansey, that is to say my movable estate to her, her heirs, executors, and administrators or assigns forever, and the land and housings and improvements as above said I give and 
2 CONC bequeath to her during the time of her widowhood, and after the time of her widowhood, as above said, is expired, my will is that Thomas, my son, shall have my plantation in Cohansey, with all buildings and improvements thereunto 
2 CONC belonging, to him, his hers, executors, or assigns forever, he or they paying ye sume of five pounds to William, my son, and five pounds to Mary, my daughter, when they shall come to age according to law. "And all my land at Simsb
2 CONC ury in New England I give to my son, Daniel, his heirs, executors, and assigns forever, with all appurtenances thereon belonging. "And I do make, ordain and constitute my well beloved wife my executrix, and Levi Preston, my belove
2 CONC d friend, my executor of this, my last will and testament, and I do hereby utterly disallow, revoke and disannul all former wills and testaments, legacies, and bequests executed by me in any way before named to be my last will and
2 CONC  testament. "In witness whereof, I have hereunto set my hand and seal ye day and year above said. Signed, sealed, published, pronounced, and declared by ye said Thomas Alderman as his last Will and Testament "Thomas Alderman "In p
2 CONC resence of us, the subscribers, David Shepard, Ephraim Daton, John Ware. "Cohansey, August ye 20, 1715."
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional Sources:  https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LBD7-18S?icid=amp_hdr_signin Alderman Family in America http://alderman.50megs.com/rich_text.html
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was the second child of William and Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went with friends to East Hampton, Long Island, NY where he met Mary. After they were married, they moved to Cohansey, Salem or Cumberland, NJ. It was there he founded the Presbyterian Church in Fairyland on May 199, 1708 of wh
2 CONC ich he and Mary were charter members. It was located n the Cohansey River in Cumberland County, where he may have lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to his will in 1715, Thomas was a weaver. He owned property in both Cohansey, NJ and Simsbury, CT and almost 60 pounds worth of household items, including a horse and cart, furniture, lumber, meat cattle and sheep. He ga
2 CONC ve his estate in Simsbury to his youngest son, Daniel, who, however, continued to reside in Cohansey, NJ. He left is Simsbury plantation to his son Thomas.
