1 NOTE Charles de Wolf kept a general store, and amassed considerable property. In his will he gave to his wife, Prudence, 1/4 of his personal property, and also the use of all his real estate as long as she remained his widow. To his so
2 CONC ns John & Stephen the lands lying in the Town of Glastonbury (which he received from Thomas & Sarah WHITE) after their mother's decease or remarriage. To his sons Simon & Joseph his dwelling house in Middletown, and all the lands 
2 CONC which he owned in Middletown, after their mother's decease or remarriage. To his daughters Prudence, Mary & Rebecca 3/4 of his personal estate to be equally divided among them. To all his sons & daughters in equal dower the lands 
2 CONC in the town of Lyme which he received from his father by deed or gift. Simon was appointed sole executor. The witnesses were Nathaniel Lavidge & John Lavidge. Joseph DeWOLF, aged 14 years, made choice of Thaddieus Wells of Glaston
2 CONC bury to be his guardian. Thaddieus Wells accepted the trust December 12, 1733.
