1 NAME Elijah /Parker/
2 GIVN Elijah
2 SURN Parker
2 _MARNM Parker
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 13 NOV 1730
2 PLAC Coventry, Tolland County, Connecticut
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1800
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} James, son of Samuel and Mary, married Tabitha Strong.  They had one son Elijah,  Tabitha died a week after his birth, I assume from birth complications.  James remarried Elizabeth Skinner.  He too is buried in the
2 CONC  Nathan Hale Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elijah moved to Belchertown Mass, there he married Martha Stiles and had a number of children, one of these, Stiles, served under Colonel J. Brooks, Captain Luke Day of the 7th Massachusetts Regiment in the revolutionary war.  Bel
2 CONC chertown was a rather poor place, the first minister was paid in firewood.  Elijah served as Constable one year, the surveyor of highways another and was generally involved in the towns affairs.  Martha died in 1776 and Elijah all
2 CONC egedly married second a “Ms.” Belknap, who we presume to be Mary (nee Newton) Belknap the widow of Samuel Belknap who died “In service” in Cambridge in 1775.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1783, after the war of independence, revolutionary soldiers returned home with little in their pockets.  They had been paid in fairly worthless Continental script, much of which they had sold to speculators at a fraction of it'
2 CONC s face value.  They returned to farms neglected by their absence.  What currency there was quickly moved offshore in a trade imbalance where the colonies imported much, but exported little, the British had closed the Caribbean to 
2 CONC American trade, and the French made poor trading partners.  There was also the matter of war debt, the colonies had borrowed a great deal for the revolution and to pay it off new taxes were imposed on towns throughout Massachusett
2 CONC s, many of these towns had no representation at the state level and no say in the matter.  Some towns and counties were years behind in tax payments, and Governor Bowdoin of Massachusetts moved aggressively to collect these.  The 
2 CONC result was a cascading effect of lawsuits as creditors called in their loans from farmers who had no money to pay them.  Farmers were thrown into debtors' prison, their lands and goods were seized to pay off their debts.  The cour
2 CONC ts of Hampshire County were crowded with these doings.  
2 CONT
2 CONT On Aug 22, 1786, fifty towns of old Hampshire county met at a convention in Hatfield.  Belchertown was represented by Elijah Parker, Jacob Wilson and John Cowls.  "Though they advised against violence, their action tended in that 
2 CONC direction".  Four days later an armed mob of about 1,500 men took possession of the court house in Northampton and notified officials that it was "inconvenient" that the court should sit until there was chance for redress of their
2 CONC  grievances, namely; to freeze debt collection, end debtors prisons, provide better representation in government, and increase the amount of currency in circulation.  No court was held for the next eight months.  A leader of this 
2 CONC group emerged, Daniel Shay, and the movement came to be known as "Shays' Rebellion".  On several occasions, rebellious men of western Massachusetts would appear to shut down courthouses throughout western Massachusetts, local mili
2 CONC tias were sympathetic to the rebellion and would not stand against these mobs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor Bowdoin called for a levy of 4,400 men (1,200 from Hampshire county) funded by Boston merchants under the command of General Benjamin Lincoln.  This force met Shays forces at the federal arsenal in Springfield Mass and, t
2 CONC o make a long story short, the rebellion was broken, leaving some ill-will between the rebels and members of Lincoln's militia for some time afterwards.  In the aftermath, Elijah and other rebels had to take an oath of allegiance 
2 CONC to the commonwealth of Massachusetts and were barred from holding many elected and appointed positions.
2 CONT
2 CONT To the farmers, the government was seen as unresponsive, to the merchants and creditors, the government was seen as helpless.  These were issues that went with the state representatives to the drafting of our Constitution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT If you drive to Amherst Massachusetts today, you may travel along the “Daniel Shay’s” Highway, State Route 202.  Elijah, and much of his family, had been communicants of the Belchertown Congregational Church, which still stands an
2 CONC d just celebrated their 275th anniversary.  Reverend Forward of the Congregational church took a dim view of Elijah’s activities in the rebellion and Elijah never returned to his church.  In March/April of 1787, the sheriffs came 
2 CONC to confiscate ~25 acres of Elijahs land for repayment of debts amounting to about 11 pounds.  The entire Parker clan vanished from Belchertown at this point and re-surfaced in Decatur New York.  The only Parkers left behind were t
2 CONC wo of Elijah’s daughters; Jerusha Parker who had married Moses Hannum, and Tabitha Parker who had married John Brown. There are a number of Parkers buried in the Decatur cemetery in Decatur, Otsego County.  Elijah may be one of th
2 CONC em.  His sons and daughters stayed in New York until about 1815.  There are records of them founding a Union (non-denominational) church in Decatur, and again as fence viewers in Pittstown (known also as Richmond and Honeoye).  No
2 CONC te that I say “them”, it’s not just his sons, but also his sons-in-law, his cousins and their offspring who show up.  In about 1815 they moved either together or in sequence to Campbell County Kentucky and the vicinity.  Stiles mo
2 CONC ved on to Ohio where he is buried.  James, son of Elijah and Ms Belknap, died either in Kentucky or Indiana, while his widow Sarah moved with all of their children to Illinois, dying in Mayfield Illinois.  Several of that generati
2 CONC on can be found there, others moved on to Kansas, Minnesota and other points west.                                                                                                                https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree
2 CONC /person/details/LJV7-YWD                                                                                https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/101784529/elijah_parker                          
