1 NAME Daniel /Spencer/
2 GIVN Daniel
2 _MARNM Spencer
2 NICK Spintzer
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1759
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1854
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/23449882/daniel-spencer
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Daniel and Patience (Olds) of Suffield, Connecticut and the  husband of Catherine Cramer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Spencer was a dark-haired young shoemaker from Pomfret, Conn. On July 8, 1780, exactly one month before turning 21, he began his service in Capt. John McGregier's Company of the 4th Infantry Regiment, Connecticut Continenta
2 CONC l Line. The outfit had suffered through the harsh winter at Valley Forge By July 4, 1853, those days of hardship and danger were relegated to the dim past. Daniel Spencer basked in celebrity as one of the last surviving soldiers o
2 CONC f the Revolution. He was honored for being part of that brave band of Americans whose sacrifice paid for our country's freedom. He died a year later
2 CONT
2 CONT "He sat up all night fanning his commanding officer, Captain George Hurlbut, who  had been shot in a fight during which the British captured a supply ship. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spencer’s account of the death of the officer differed markedly from that of  Gen. Washington's; Spencer said the wounds of the officer had nearly healed when  he caught a disease from a prostitute and this illness killed him, whe
2 CONC reas  Washington said he died of his wounds. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spencer’s pension was revoked soon after it was granted and for years he and his  family lived in severe poverty. Eventually his pension was restored. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the guest of honor during New York City’s celebration of July 4, 1853." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Flowers • 7
