1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Marriage John Bird to Rebecca Hukill-McClurg on 1 Apr 1816 in Trumbull County, Ohio Marriage date and location is stated in his wife Rebecca's pension application for her first husband's service. John is the second
2 CONC  husband of Rebecca.
2 CONT
2 CONT John's wife Rebecca Hukill-Bird applies for a pension increase after John dies. The pension was awarded earlier due to her first husband having died in military service in the War of 1812. Rebecca and her son Elliott Bird both sta
2 CONC te that her second husband John Bird died in Dekalb County, Indiana on 13 Jan 1854. There is another page where it states John died 30 Jan 1854 so date could be the 30th or they  misheard 13th for 30th and wrote it as such.
2 CONT
2 CONT The burial location of John has not been confirmed but since he died in Dekalb County, Indiana and the family lived near White Cemetery as shown by land coordinates, and his daughter Elizabeth who died three years later is buried 
2 CONC there, and the back end of the cemetery appears to have many lots with the stones missing, it would seem most probable that John Bird is buried in White City Cemetery in one of the back rows possibly by his daughter Elizabeth Bird
2 CONC . She is buried on the west side of the road in the north east section second to last row up by the trees. A picture is posted of that area. There are a number of empty lots where the tombstones are missing most likely having fall
2 CONC en over in earlier years and are buried beneath the ground. An excavation of the area would have to be done to see what can be found.  The earliest cemetery reading appears to be in the 1970s which was 120 years after these early 
2 CONC burials began; no doubt many had already fallen over and sunk below the ground. Most of the cemetery contains nicely lined rows of graves until you get to the back by the trees where Elizabeth Bird  is buried and there are only a 
2 CONC smattering of stones left standing. Probably are a number of stones underground in this area. It is also interesting to note that some of the people who still have tombstones standing and are buried in the area by Elizabeth Bird l
2 CONC ived by the Birds on the 1850 census; such as John and  Mary Rhodes. Also Jonas Emmanuel  testifies in Rebecca's pension application and is buried in this cemetery as well. He appears to have been a doctor. In summary, the doctor 
2 CONC for the area who testified, John's neighbors on the 1850 census before he dies, and his daughter who died a few years after him are all confirmed burials in White City cemetery. It seems unlikely he would be buried somewhere else.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/188838994/john-bird Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jan 2 2020, 3:43:06 UTC''
