1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Captured by Indians after they killed her husband, Benjamin and her child. She was taken as a prisoner to Canada...
2 CONT
2 CONT Eunice (cooley) Garfield seems to have been a woman of considerable personal beauty, as well as of fortitude and presence of mind. She adapted herself to the situation by forced cheerfulness and because of her evident courage gain
2 CONC ed favor by the Indians, and was thus treated fairly well on the march. For many days, she kept on the lookout for aid, expecting to be rescued by her husband of whose death she learned only on her return.With the other two women 
2 CONC she took turns standing all night to be ready for quick flight, but no help came.
2 CONT
2 CONT After many days and nights of travel, footsore and weary, the captives finally reach a French settlement in Canada. Here the Indians sold them as slaves. Eunice was sold to a French family, of which she became a beloved and truste
2 CONC d member,and was soon entrusted with the purchase of all household goods and supplies.  Longing for her freedom, she told her story to one of the tradesmen whose friendship she gained. He was a kindly man and offered to pay her $1
2 CONC 0 for every $1 she could save, to be repaid after she got home, so that she could purchase her liberty.this she eventually did.  She went to France, and from there to England and thence to Boston. She probably reached Massachusett
2 CONC s some time in 1756. In 1758 she was paid a sum of money by New Hampshire to cover her last year in Canada.  
2 CONT
2 CONT On her return to Massachusetts, she learned of her husband's death. Shortly thereafter became the second wife of Ephraim Pratt and lived nearly half a century in Hardwick where she was widely known and beloved as "Aunt Pratt". Peo
2 CONC ple came a long distance to see the old lady and to hear the story of her Indian adventures. The place of her burial is unknown as they were dug up for a in the process of building a reservoir for Boston.
