1 TITL Capt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''John Gill Pease, Jr.,''' oldest son of John Pease Sr. and Mary Goddell, was born at Salem May 30, 1654, and married Margaret Adams of Ipswich, Massachusetts Jan. 30, 1677. Complete name is John Gill Pease.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was apprenticed at Salem to a carpenter John Symonds, who died not not long after; and in his will assigned his apprentice to his son James and it seems probable that he continued in this profession until his removal to Enfield
2 CONC  as evidenced by his sale of "land, house and shop."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the most prominent men in the history of the town of Enfield; he was indefatigable to promote and advance it's prosperity when an infant settlement, and after it's incorporation. He was the first surveyor, the first 
2 CONC selectman and the first militia captain. He was a member of the  Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company of Massachusetts.  He died at Enfield, aged 80 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Founders Monument Enfield''':
2 CONT
2 CONT To Honor the Memory of the Founders of the Town of Enfield. Who led by John Pease, Jr. and his Brother Robert made the First Settlement in 1680.  Placed by Penelope Terry Abbey Chapter, D.A.R.  1680     1930
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bio'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After being awarded a land grant at the new plantation called Freshwater (later Enfield), John Pease Jr. and his brother Robert, after examining the land,  came to Enfield from Salem  in 1679, and built a hut or a cellar, in the s
2 CONC ide of the hill about 40 rods east of where an old meeting house stood, and there  lived through the winter alone, no other white person in the place. The next spring they removed their families and commenced the settlement, durin
2 CONC g the succeeding three years they were joined by nearly 30 families from Salem and its vicinity, which made Enfield a Salem colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Jr. was a surveyor, and the settlement began building roads, shcools, a meeting house. They reserved lots for the ministry as well. More settlers joined them in the first year, including brother Johnathan Pease. John was the 
2 CONC first town constable. The town erected its meeting house in 1684.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first birth occured that year, it being Margaret Pease, the daughter of John Jr. and Margaret his wife.  The first person who died in the settlement was Lot Killam who was one of the Salem colonists and left one son. Between 1
2 CONC 684 and 1691, more grants were awarded, including Robert Pease II, David Bishop, Thomas Hale and John Adams. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The town was incorporated and in 1688 John Pease Jr. and Samuel Terry were the first selectmen chosen by the people. The town erected a corn mill, a sawmill was built by Isaac Meacham and Johnathan Bush. Later a distillery. A land
2 CONC  purchase was negotiated with Indian sachem Notatuck by The Commissioners, who included John Pease Sr., Major John Pynchon, Thomas Goold (Gould), Lt. Thomas Stebbins, Deacon Johnathan Burt, Benjamin Parsons and William Denton.This
2 CONC  was dated March 1689.
2 CONT
2 CONT His children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John, b. Salem April 22, 1678
2 CONT # James b. Salem 1679
2 CONT # Margaret b. Enfield CT Jan. 24, 1683. m. Josiah Colton 1789. d. 1775
2 CONT # Johnathan b. Enfield Jan. 29, 1687 probably died as infant
2 CONT # Ann b. Enfield Oct. 29, 1685. m. Jeremiah Lord of East Windsor, Ct.
2 CONT # Mary b. Enfield May 24, 1688 m. Thomas Abbe 1714 d. 1746
2 CONT # Sarah b. Enfield September 27, 1689.m. Timothy Root of Somers, Ct. d. 1750
2 CONT # Joseph b. Enfield March 10, 1692
2 CONT
