2 CONT  The Puritan Great Migration.
2 CONT Robert Pease migrated to New England during the Puritan Great Migration (1621-1640). (See The Great Migration (Series 2), by R. C. Anderson, vol. 5, p. 418)
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Pease was the son of Robert Pease of Great Baddow, Essex, England. The passenger list of the ship Francis says he was 27, but it also says his brother John was 27. John was certainly that age, leaving to question if Robert'
2 CONC s age was either recorded wrong or mistranscribed.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert married first by 1630 to Lydia, whose maiden name is unknown.[2] . He married second by 1639 to Mary, whose maiden name is also unknown. Mary married 2nd, "probably much earlier [than 1677]. Richard Haynes."[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with first wife Lydia:[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Pease, bp. Apr. 1630, Great Baddow (day missing due to page damage)[5] mar. Sarah ____ by 1630 (birth of 1st child).
2 CONT John Pease, bp. 11 Feb. 1631/2 Great Baddow;[6] m (1). Mary Goodale by 1654 (birth of 1st child), m (2) Anne Cummings 8 Dec. 1669 at Salem.
2 CONT Children with second wife Mary/Marie:[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Pease, b. say 1639, bp. 15 Oct 1643 Salem; mar. Mary Hobbs 15 Mar 1667[/8?] at Salem.
2 CONT Sarah Pease, b. say 1641, bp. 15 Oct 1643 Salem; mar. John Sampson 22 Oct 1667 at Beverly.
2 CONT Mary Pease, b. say 1643, bp. 15 Oct 1643 Salem; mar. Nathaniel Carrell.
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Pease died in Salem, Essex, Massachusetts Bay Colony, before 3 January 1644/5, when his inventory was taken. His estate was valued at £39, 12 shillings and 6 pence, with debts of £6. This included a house, barn and eleven a
2 CONC cres. Widow Marie Pease was named executrix on that date.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Wives. Was his first wife Lydia West? See a marriage, 1634 Widford, Essex, England, claimed in Yates Publishing, U.S. and International Marriage Records, 1560-1900 (Provo, UT, USA, The Generations Network, Inc., 2004). Is there a 
2 CONC better source for this?
2 CONT (no such marriage found)
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial/Erroneous FindAGrave. The monument containing Robert Pease (1607-1644) located in the Enfield Street Cemetery in Enfield, Connecticut, is obviously a newer monument, listing the ancestry of Theodore Pease. It was not meant 
2 CONC to represent Robert's actual burial, which was probably in Salem.
2 CONT
2 CONT About his FindAGrave memorial see the 2017 G2G helpful answer by Ann B and Robert Pease Jr. memorial 68905222 and monument images; web content, FindAGrave, maintained by Dawn Braynt, monument images by Richard Bailey and Sue; biog
2 CONC raphy by Linda Mac.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are some newer works concerning the Pease family, that are unavailable on the internet. Do you have access to Pease Family History:the history of the descendants of both John and Robert Pease, the first generation in America
2 CONC , is included along with many of the allied families to the present generation. by Phillip J. Rice (Manor Pub. Co., 1982).
2 CONT
2 CONT For Richard Bart, "The Pease family from Great Baddow, England," c1999? see Vineyard.net. This link courtesy of Faylene Bailey, 2021.
2 CONT
2 CONT From a prior version of the profile, "Note: PEASE RECORD, P.5, 6, 7, 8, 17, 390; PERLEYS STORY OF SALEM P.371; HISTORY OF SALEM VOL 1 P.370. VITAL RECORDS OF SALEM ESSEX, MASS; PROBATE RECORDS OF ESSEX, MASS, P.42-4; HISTORY OF SA
2 CONC LEM VOL 1 P.370. VITAL RECORDS OF SALEM ESSEX, MASS; PROBATE RECORDS OF ESSEX, MASS, P.42-4."
2 CONT
