2 TITL Ancestry Family Trees
1 NOTE 	Source:  Bygod Eggleston:  Englishman & Colonist, by Dr. Rosalee Eggleston and Linda Eggleston McBroomm, published by The John and Mary Clearinghouse.
2 CONT Pp. 18-19
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bygod Eggleston's Grandparents"
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems easy enough to explain Bygod Eggleston's unusual Christian name by assuming that his grandmother was a Bigod.  But such an assertion without proof is foolhardy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tracing of Bygod Eggleston's maternal grandmother has cost his descendants much in time and money.  The mischief began when someone decided that because James Eggleston called Ralph Harker brother and that the once lord of Set
2 CONC trington Manor, Sir Francis Bigod, had a daughter named Dorothy and that Ralph Harker's mother was named Dorothy, too, Ralph's mother must have been a Bigod and that she had a daughter who married James Eggleston and became the mo
2 CONC ther of Bygod Eggleston.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mischief was compounded by Pedigree 3J for Leverett Farwell Eggleston published in Wurt's Magna Charta, part V, pp. 1415-19.  This so-called pedigree seems very grand and exciting with all the noble names given there until you
2 CONC  get to the very top of page 1519 where these words occur:
2 CONT
2 CONT      John Bigod married as above 13 Joan Strangeways, whose great granddaughter was:
2 CONT
2 CONT      Juliana Harker, who married James Eggleston of Settrington.
2 CONT
2 CONT What is missing here is an explanation of how this is possible.  One has to question the authority of Wurts when they publish such bold and unsubstantiated claims.  Judging from some articles which have been published, some descen
2 CONC dants of Bygod have accepted this at face value while others have been much more cautious.
2 CONT
2 CONT At first I wanted to believe the pedigree which is the same as mine from Bygod down to 1796.  But, I couldn't be satisfied until the link between the Bigods and Egglestons had been found.  The first time I worked at the Borthwick 
2 CONC Institute in York, I asked to look at Yorkshire pedigrees.  The archivist kindly allowed me to lock myself into a basement room full of old, rare books and manuscripts.  I spend several interesting hours searching the Mauleys of M
2 CONC ulgrave Castle, the Strangeways, and the Bigods.  There were no pedigrees for Egglestons or Harkers.  There was no link to be found which would substantiate the claims made by Leverett Farwell Eggleston.
2 CONT
2 CONT It should be said here that the family of Bigod had some importance in the 12 and 13th centuries when they were the Earls of Norfolk and Suffolk.  In 1302 the last of the earls, Ralph, surrendered his earldom to the crown.  Therea
2 CONC fter the family declined in importance.  Cadet branches of Bigods held the Manor of Settrington until 2 June, 1537 when Sir Francis Bigod was put to death for treason.  They also held about 16 other manors and two or three castles
2 CONC  in the north of England.  The Bigods who lived in Settrington during James's time there were of the gentry class.
2 CONT
2 CONT If the link between the Bigods and the Egglestons cannot be found in a musty room in York, then where can it be found?  In fact it has been easier to disprove than to prove that Dorothy Harker was a daughter or granddaughter of Si
2 CONC r Francis Bigod.
2 CONT
2 CONT Facts about Dorothy Bigod/Radcliffe
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parents were:  Fir Francis Bigod (b. 4 Oct., 1507) and Katherine Conyers who were married by special
2 CONT                             Special dispensation (granted 4 Nov., 1528) because of consanguinity to the third and
2 CONT                             Fourth degrees.  Date of marriage:  late 1528 or early the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their Children:  1.  Dorothy, born before 1530.  Her name appears in Tong's Visitation of 1530
2 CONT                              (see Surtees, XLI. P. 67).
2 CONT
2 CONT                         2.  Ralph born about 1531.  He was affianced at the age of three to a daughter of
2 CONT                              Lord Latimer who paid Sir Francis 700 marks.  For reasons which will be revealed
2 CONT                              later Lord Latimer broke off this engagement.  Ralph Bigod did marry someone, but
2 CONT                              apparently died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dorothy             married Roger Radcliffe before 1551
2 CONT
2 CONT Their Children    1.  Francis baptized 1 April, 1551; buried 1 February, 1592.
2 CONT Were
2 CONT                         2.  Katherine baptized 1553/4 and died unmarried in 1615.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis              By his wife Fidswide Sevile had these children:  Roger, Henry, Thomas, Helen and a
2 CONT Radcliffe:           daughter Frances (Surtees, III, 129).
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of            she died before 22 October, 8 Eliz (1565-66).
2 CONT Dorothy
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger:              he married again and had four more children:  Jane, Isabel, Margaret, and Ralph.
2 CONT                         Roger's will was dated 1588 and proved 27 March, 1589.  He died twelve years
2 CONT                         after Dorothy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miles Harker      was buried 6 November, 1574, eight or nine years after the death of Dorothy Radcliffe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comment:         There is no way that Miles Harker could have married Dorothy Bigod/Radcliffe.  You can
2 CONT                         See the complete Radcliffe pedigree in Surtees, III. P. 129.  If both Miles Harker and
2 CONT                         Roger Radcliffe were alive when Dorothy died, she would have to be married to bothmen at the same time.  That was just as illegal then as it is today.  In addition, you can see
2 CONT                        from sample page 41, that Dorothy Harker was still alive when Miles died.  It is
2 CONT                         inconceivable, too, that a poor villain like Miles Harker could have married the daughter
2 CONT                         or granddaughter of the former lord of the manor.  The social strictures of the time
2 CONT                         would not allow that.
2 CONT
2 CONT                         We have to look elsewhere for a reason why James Eggleston named his son Bygod
2 CONT                         and called Ralph Harker brother."
1 CHAN
2 DATE 24 MAY 2018
3 TIME 00:04:31
