1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Samuel Bronson
2 CONT * Birth:	1665 - Farmington, Hartford, Ct, USA
2 CONT * Death:	Jan 23 1742 - Wethersfield, Hartford, Connecticut, America
2 CONT * Parents:	Richard Bronson, unknown 
2 CONT * Siblings:	Abigail Bronson, John Bronson, Cornelius Bronson, Hannah Bronson, Elizabeth Bronson, Edith Bronson, Mary Bronson
2 CONT * Wife:	Sarah Gibbs, Lydia Warner
2 CONT * Children:	Elizabeth Bronson, Samuel Bronson, Sarah Bronson, Benjamin Bronson, Hezikiah Bronson, Timothy Bronson, Daniel Bronson, Daniel Bronson, Nathaniel Brownson, Michael Bronson, Anna Bronson, Aaron Bronson, Mercy Bronson, El
2 CONC isha Brunson, Lydia Bronson, Thomas Brownson, Rebecca Brownson, Jacob Brownson, Margaret Brownson, Samuel Brownson
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Brownson (Bronson, Brunson) married secondly, perhaps ca. 1646-7, ______ ______, whose name is completely unknown, and who probably died at the birth of her son Samuel at Farmington in 1665.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.Samuel, b. prob. At Farmington in 1665: d. at Wethersfield, Conn., 23 Jan. 1741/2, aged 76.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT The cemetery where Samuel is buried is now located in the town of Berlin, CT. It is very nicely maintained, and his grave and that of his wife are still there with legible headstones.  Death and burial data taken from the headston
2 CONC e. His marriage reported by some sources as 1 Apr 1687.  Farmington V/R indicates 4 May 1687, which is the date I am using. Source also indicates Sarah Gibbs was of Windsor, CT. I cannot account for the fact that Farmington V/R, B
2 CONC arbour collection, Vol LR6, p 305, says he died 18 Jan 1741/42. Perhaps the date on his stone is his burial date.   Berlin Town Library card file has a card on him, says Bronson, Samuel (Sr) & Sarah (Gibbs), he b 1665, bpt 4 Jun 1
2 CONC 666, Kensington, m 4 Jan 1687 Sarah & d 23 Jan 1741/2, bur Christian Lane Cemetery. The m date is an obvious reversal of day and month from the 1 Apr date shown in some sources - a good reason to avoid numerical dating. I will con
2 CONC tinue to use V/R, 4 May 1687. Some sources also give his birth and marriage locations as Kensington CT, which is no doubt correct, but since Farmington V/R is our source, and Kensington was a sub-parish of Farmington, we will leav
2 CONC e Farmington as generally correct.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enderton indicates Samuel joined the church 4 Jan 1707/1708, and that he was 76 years of age at death. His dates do not agree with those on the headstone, though they are close.  This is probably due to his getting his data from c
2 CONC ivil or church records, which often differed by a few days.  Also, there were calendar changes in this period, and they probably add to the confusion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Dr. E. R. Brownson's 1951 work, copy in our files, p 8, says: Samuel Brownson, the 3rd son of Richard, was the owner of the mill at Kensington, where he was b. 4/1/1661/2. He died at Wethersfield, Conn., 18/1/1741/2. He ma
2 CONC rried, 1/4/1687, Sarah Gibbs, dau. of Benjamin Gibbs of Windsor, where she was b. in 1666. She died at Wethersfield, Conn., 25/10/1741.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grave stones in Christian Lane Cemetery for both husband and wife, but the vital records of Wethersfield and Kensington do not agree on the exact dates of death of either. He joined the church 4/1/1707/8. They had a very large fam
2 CONC ily and all of the children but two (2) grew to manhood or womanhood, married and had large families. p 3, says: Samuel, bapt. June 4, 1661/2, (Savage says 1666) Farmington, d. (Two dates given, Jan. 23, 1746 aged 76, Wethersfield
2 CONC , V.R., and Jan. 18, 1741/2, Kensington, V.R.); m. April 5, 1687 to Sarah Gibbs. This differing on dates some.  p 5, says:  Samuel Brownson--This Present Writing made ye Twenty first day of of Janury one thousand six hundred eithy
2 CONC  six Witnesseth That I Richard Brownson of Farmington in ye Colony of Conecticut 7 County of Hartford for good & lawful reasons Do by these presents freely fully and absolutely give grant and bequeath & Bestow alienate & Passover 
2 CONC unto my Well Beloved Son Samuel Brownson & his heirs executors administrators & assigns to him & theyer use forever all my Housing and homestead as also four acres of land at Pequabuk (etc).  Farmington Land Records vol. 5, page 5
2 CONC 66, Recorded March 5, 1734/5. Then an added note: I don't understand the date 1686 as Richard died 1684 if I remember correctly, but the 6 is correct. This deed is the home lot referred to in the record of the home lot belonging t
2 CONC o Samuel on p 93 of Dr. E. R. Brownson's work.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have done much research on Samuel's descendants, particularly those of his son Benjamin, since Dr. Brownson and Col Enderton maintained in their work on the family that our branch of the family descended through these two to Ric
2 CONC hard. I have since concluded that this is in error, with the help of Mr. Andrew Clark Pikosky of Litchfield CT, who provided me information that, though Benjamin did have a son named Asa, his Asa could not possibly be the Asa in o
2 CONC ur line, as he had no son named Asa.  Further checking revealed compelling reasons why Benjamin, through Samuel, to Richard could not be the way my line runs. I now conclude that my line runs backward from Asa, Sr., to Jonathan, s
2 CONC on of John, son of John, oldest son of Richard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other than the error of Asa being the son of Benjamin, much of the work of Dr. E. R. Brownson and Col Enderton appears to be correct, and their work is my primary source for many of Samuel's descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel was a miller and farmer. The following is taken from History of New
2 CONT Britain, David N. Camp, 1889.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the oldest mills in the Kensington Society was the Bronson mill, located on the site of the principal shop lately occupied by the Peck, Stow and Wilcox Company, on Mill River, about a half mile southwest of the Berlin rail-
2 CONC way station. Samuel Bronson, the owner of this mill, furnished sawed lumber for the first meeting-house built at Christian Lane in 1710, and when this meeting-house was seated in 1716, he was known as "Samuel Bronson, miller," and
2 CONC  was seated in the "first seat in the square body." He also furnished sawed timber for the second meeting-house built in 1732-33.  At this mill-site there was a saw-mill and also a grist-mill. These were probably the oldest mills 
2 CONC in the Great Swamp Society....[See also Samuel's son Samuel on this subject.] The Bronson Mill...which has already been described as one of the oldest mills in the town, was bought by R. Moore & Sons about 1836....
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is taken from The Story of Brownsons Mills, Pikosky, 1970.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE KENSINGTON MILL
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard's son, Samuel (2) Bronson, built the next Bronson Mill, in Kensington, known for many years as "Percivals Mills" (Shepard Manuscript),which passed to his son, Samuel, brother of Benjamin (3) Bronson. Samuel (3) Bronson, (1
2 CONC 692-1752), continued the mill operation which then went to his son, Charles (4) Bronson (1723-1779), whose first wife was Thankful (5) Hart, daughter of Nathaniel (4) and Abigail (Booker) Hart, thence to grandson, Ashel (1760-1830
2 CONC ). By 1873, these mills had grown into a large establishment and were known as G.T. Hart & Co. and during World War II were part of the Continental Can Company's operation. The Compiler's father had occasion to inspect the PBX tel
2 CONC ephone switchboard at the Harts Mills around 1921, during his career with the Southern New England Telephone Company, Harts Mills was then still a flourishing enterprise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  275th Anniversary Book, Kensington Congregational Church, 1987.
2 CONT
2 CONT A HISTORICAL SKETCH, KENSINGTON CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, 1712-1987
2 CONT
2 CONT Three hundred one years ago Captain Richard Seymour led a band of fourteen families from Farmington Village to the southeastern section of that town.  In 1686 and for many years thereafter this area was known as the Great Swamp se
2 CONC ttlement of Farmington. Our forefathers traveled the path to Farmington Village on foot or on horseback at least once a week - to go to church on Sunday. The trip was long and tiring, especially in the winter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1705, the settlers petitioned the General Assembly to be set off as a separate parish. In 1707, the Assembly granted the Great Swamp Area the right to lay and collect its own taxes instead of paying taxes to Farmington. The mem
2 CONC bers of the parish met to lay plans for the establishment of its own church.  Mr. William Burnham was called to lead the group.  After several years of work and planning, the Great Swamp Church was gathered on December 10, 1712 wi
2 CONC th a membership of ten - seven men and three women.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first meeting house was located near Deming Road, just south of the original settlement on Christian Lane. The sanctuary was unfinished as was the parsonage. The church members worked on the buildings so that by 1722 the churc
2 CONC h had pews, a pulpit with a suitable cushion, and a gallery. Most important of all the Great Swamp name was finally replaced by the present name, Kensington, which the settlers had requested in 1707. In many early churches men and
2 CONC  women were seated separately. In 1717 the names of men assigned to seats in the Great Swamp Church numbered about 60. In 1720 galleries were built similar to those in Farmington. In 1724 Thomas Hart and Samuel Brownson were chose
2 CONC n to oversee the youth on the Sabbath in the time of exercises "to restrain them from irreverent behaviour."
2 CONT
2 CONT The growth of the church was such that in 1732, it was necessary to think of a larger building. There developed a strong disagreement in the parish concerning the location of this building. Finally the General Assembly had to step
2 CONC  in, appoint a committee to select a site and erect the church. The second church was on Porter's Pass diagonally across Farmington Avenue from the Victory Market. The article goes on to describe later happenings in the church, in
2 CONC cluding the building of the third church in the middle 1700s. The church split in two groups in the 1770s, and a new church was erected for the Kensington parish, the other group also getting a new building, and called the Worthin
2 CONC gton parish. The Kensington church was dedicated 1 Dec 1774, though not finished until 1798.  As of 1987, this church was still in use as a house of worship. I believe that, from these dates, we can conclude that the Samuel Bronso
2 CONC n who initially furnished lumber for the first church was probably Samuel, Sr., and his son later provided similar help, after taking over duties of the mill.  The Samuel who was appointed to control the youngsters activities was 
2 CONC undoubtedly Samuel, Jr. In any event, it does not matter too much which Samuel actually was running the mill at those times help was needed for the church - both father and son were undoubtedly involved in providing it. This artic
2 CONC le has a photograph of the old well on Christian Lane, taken about 1900. The well was dug and used by the first settlers who came there in 1686. It was still in use at the beginning of this century (about 1900). So far as is known
2 CONC , there is nothing left of it now.  A photocopy of the picture is in our files.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Enderton says Samuel had a dau, Mercy, who m John Judd. Mr. Pikosky, of Litchfield CT, who has done research on this line, indicates he thought that was not so.  I found in Barbour, Waterbury V/R, Vol 1, p 47, the followin
2 CONC g entry: Bronson, Marcy, dau Samuel, decd., of Kensington, m John Judd, son of John, decd., of Waterbury, 6 Jan 1731/2.  The key to the puzzle is the fact that the Samuel who was the father of Mercy (or Marcy) was deceased in Janu
2 CONC ary 1731/2. This Samuel was not dead.  His contemporary, in the third generation of the family of John Bronson (the immigrant), was indeed deceased. I have therefore concluded that IF Samuel Bronson, here, had a dau named Mercy, s
2 CONC he is not the one who married John Judd. I am deleting Mercy from this family, as it is not established in view of the above that this Samuel ever had a dau named Mercy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  Barbour, Farmington V/R, Vol LR2, p 145, says Brownson, Samuel, of Farmington, son of Richard, m Sarah Gibbs, of Windsor, 4 May 1687.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wayne and Shirley Brownson, descendants of Micah, this man's son, sent me a typed transcript of a deed where Samuel gives land to Micah.  On the back of the document is: Micael Brownson His Deed of Gift from his father Sam
2 CONC uel Brownson Sen Rec to be Recorded April 7, 1729 it is Recorded in Farmington Records Book 4  p 538:  pd J Hooker Regisd
2 CONT
2 CONT The document reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT Know all men by these Presents that I Samuel Sen of Farmington in the County of Hartford & Colony of Connecticut in New England, for the great Love & Paternal affection which I Bear to my beloved Son Micah Brownson of aforesaid Fa
2 CONC rmington, & for other good Confer me hereunto moving have Given, Granted, Assigned, Set over & Confirmed, & Do by the Presents, fully freely and absolutely Give, Grant, Assign, Set over & Confirm, unto the said Micah Brownson & un
2 CONC to his Heirs & Assigns for Ever, all the Right, Title, Interest Property use Claim & Demand whatsoever I the said Samuel Brownson have had or in Time to Come might out or Should have in or to Several Parcells of Land Lying within 
2 CONC the Bounds of the Township of Aforesaid Farmington viz. one Parcel Lying in the South Division Containing by Estimation Seventy one acres be it more or Less, being Twenty Eight Rods wide from the West End, for the Space of one mil
2 CONC e & a Quarter and from there to the East End - Twenty one Rods wide, & is Bounded North with Land belonging to Daniel Brownson, South with Land belonging to Robert Boothe, East Partly with land belonging to Daniel Brownson & Partl
2 CONC y with a high way, & West with a high way; also one Parcel Lying in the Blew mountin Division, containing Sixty arces be it more or Less, & is the one Half of a Lot Laid out in the Right of mr Anthony Hawkins the whole of which sa
2 CONC id Lot is Bounded North with Land Laid out in the Right of John Lee, South with Land Laid out in the Right of Matthew Woodruff; East & West with high ways. Also one Parcel Lying in the Blew Mountain Division, Containing forty Eigh
2 CONC t acres & Three Roods, it being one Half of the Lot Laid out in the Right of my Father Richard Brownson, & the whole Lot is Bounded North with Land Laid out in the Right of Abraham Brownson, South with Land Laid out in the Right o
2 CONC f Capt John Hart East & West with High wayes; To Have and to Hold the abovementioned Parcells of Land to himself the Said Micah Brownson his Heirs & Assigns for ever together with all fruits Profits Priviledges & advantages to the
2 CONC  Same belonging or in any wise appertaining free & Clear of & from all incumbrances of Title whatsoever, & moreover I the Said Samuel Brownson do hereby for my Self my Heirs Executors & Administrators Assure & Promise unto the sai
2 CONC d Micah Brownson his Heirs Executors Administrators & Assigns in Manner following that is to say that I the Said Samuel Brownson am Seized of the above granted parcels of Land in my own Right in Fee Simple at the time of the seali
2 CONC ng & Delivery of these Presents and have full power good right & Lawfull Authority to dispose of the Same as is above Expressed, and the said Micah Brownson his Heirs Executors administrators & Assigns Shall & may peaceably & Quie
2 CONC tly have hold & possess the Same to his or their own Proper use & Behoof from the Day of the Date of these Presents hereafter for Ever, without any molestation disturbance or denyal from me the Said Samuel Brownson or any other pe
2 CONC rson or persons Lawfully claiming any Estate or Title in or to the Same from by or under me; and moreover I the Said Samuel Brownson do hereby oblige my Self my Heirs & assigns in the Quiet Possession of the above granted Parcells
2 CONC  of Land against the Lawfull Claims of all persons whomsoever, alwayes Provided & it is hereby provided that the Said Samuel Brownson Shall have Liberty to Sell any part of the above granted Land, for his Support, if through Sickn
2 CONC ess or Lameness or old age he be reduced to Straits. Provided the Said Micah Brownson his Heirs or assigns may have the first offer thereof; In Witness whereof I have hereunto Set my Hand & Seal this Ninetheenth Day of February, A
2 CONC nno. Dom. 1728/ & in the Second year of his majesties Reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Brownson
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed Sealed & Delivered
2 CONT In Presence of us
2 CONT William Burnham Sen
2 CONT William Burnham Jun
2 CONT
2 CONT Farmington the 7th Day of April Ano Dom 1729 --
2 CONT Samuel Brownson Sen Subscribor to this foregoing
2 CONT Instrument... Came personally and acknowledged the Same
2 CONT to be his own free act and Deed --- Before Mr John
2 CONT
2 CONT - FamilySearch.org
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1686, Richard Seymour led a group of fourteen or fifteen families to a site near a branch of the Mattabesett River now known as Willow Brook, but then called Gilbird's River. They erected a fort using poles 16 feet high, and bu
2 CONC ilt cabins inside, to protect them from the Indians and the wild animals. It is probable that they were encouraged in this enterprise by Captain Belcher, who would have benefited from the increased trade. They called this first st
2 CONC reet Christian Lane. Farms spread out from the fort, going westward to what is now Porter's Pass, out to West Lane, where a mill was built by Samuel Brownson in the early 1700s at the current site of Paper Goods Pond.
2 CONT
2 CONT - Berlin Historical Society
2 CONT http://www.berlincthistorical.org/history.shtml
