1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Colonel Fisher Gay'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A043712 DAR Ancestor #: A043712]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/73866069/fisher-gay
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary War Veteran in Colonel in Colonel Wolcott's Regiment
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Colonel Fisher died before battle and was subsequently reprimanded by George Washington, who did not realize he died.''
2 CONT
2 CONT To Colonel Fisher Gay
2 CONT
2 CONT New York, September 4th 1776
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir,
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether you do not get the General Orders, with that regularity which is to be wished or whether (which is hard to suppose) you do not attend to them, I will not undertake to determine; But it is a melancholy truth, that Returns, 
2 CONC essentially necessary for the Commanding Officer to govern himself by, & which might be made in an hour after they are Calld for where care and Order are Observed, are obtained with so much difficulty. Nor can I help regretting th
2 CONC at not only regular Returns, but that orders in instances equally important should be so little attended to. I therefore address my self to you in this manner, requesting in Express & peremptory terms, that you do without delay ma
2 CONC ke out & return to the Adjutant General's Office immediately an exact state of the regiment or Corps under your Command, and that the like return be given in every Saturday at Orderly time without fail. I also desire in terms equa
2 CONC lly express, that you do not suffer the men of your Corps to straggle from their Quarters, or be absent from camp without leave, and even then, but few at a time. Your own Reputation—the safety of the Army, & the good of the Cause
2 CONC  depends, under God, upon our vigilance & Readiness to oppose a Crafty & enterprising enemy, who are always upon the watch to take advantages. to prevent straggling let your Rolls be called over three times a day, & the delinquent
2 CONC s punished. I have one thing more to urge, and that is, that every attempt of the men, to Plunder Houses, Orchards, Gardens &c. be discouraged not only for the Preservation of property & sake of good Order, but for the Prevention 
2 CONC of those fatal Consequences which usually follow such Diabolical practices. In Short Sir, at a time when every thing is at stake, It behooves every man to Exert himself. It will not do for the Commanding Officer of a Regiment to C
2 CONC ontent himself with barely giving Orders, he should see (at least know) they are executed. he should call his men out frequently and Endeavour to impress them with a Just and true sense of their duty, & how much depends upon subor
2 CONC dination and Discipline—Let me therefore not only Command but Exhort you & your Officers, as you Regard your Reputation your Country, and the sacred Cause of Freedom in which you are engaged, to manly & Vigorous exertions at this 
2 CONC time each striving to excel the Other in the respective duties of his department. I trust it is unnecessary for me to add further and that these & all other Articles of your duty you will Execute with a Spirit & punctuality becomi
2 CONC ng your Station. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I am Sir Your Most Obt Servant
2 CONT
2 CONT G.W.
