1 NAME James /Graves/
2 GIVN James
2 _MARNM Graves
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1712
2 ADDR
3 CITY Colchester
3 STAE Connecticut
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1787
2 ADDR
3 CITY Shelburne
3 STAE Massachusetts
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for MASSACHUSETTS with the rank of Private.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A047271  DAR Ancestor # A047271]
2 CONT
2 CONT James Graves resided in East Haddam. Later he sold his land there and moved to Shelburne, Massachusetts, where he was taxed as late as 1787. James enlisted in June 1745 for the expedition to Cape Breton. He was a member of the 5th
2 CONC  Company Militia under Captain William Throop. He served in the Revolutionary War as private in Captain Joshua L. Woodbridge's Company, Colonel Nathan Tyler's Massachusetts Regiment. He enlisted June 25, 1779 and was discharged De
2 CONC cember 25, 1779. He also served one month and seven days in Rhode Island in the same company and regiment. His sons James and Ansel served as substitutes for him in 1782.
2 CONT
2 CONT At a town meeting in Shelburne, where James, Haynes, and Eliphalet Graves lived, held on June 26, 1776 (ten days before "Our Declaration of Independence"), the following resolution was adopted: "Resolved. That this town will stand
2 CONC  by the Honorable Continental Congress with their lives and fortunes if their Honors think it expedient to declare as independent from the Kingdom of Great Britain, for the safety of our rights and privileges."
