1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Unknown-384394
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann (Unknown) Davis (abt. 1626 - bef. 1701)
2 CONT Born about 1626 [uncertain] in Somerset, England  [uncertain]
2 CONT Died before 1 Apr 1701 before about age 75 in Barnstable, Province of Massachusetts Bay 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann "Anne" Davis formerly [surname unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of [father unknown] and [mother unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of Robert Davis — married about 1645 in Barnstable, Plymouth Colony  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother of Deborah (Davis) Geer, Josiah Davis, Sarah (Davis) Young, Mercy (Davis) Mayo and Joseph Davis
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann (Unknown) Davis is a part of Massachusetts history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Unknown was born in about 1626.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: This is not Anne Kingman, the daughter of Henry Kingman and his wife, Joanna Unknown, married to Unknown Barnard or Bernard. (In Great Migration: Immigrants to New England, 1634-1635, Volume IV, I-L, page 180, Anderson discu
2 CONC sses Anna Kingman, dau of Henry, and concludes that she married to ___ Barnard, not to any Davis. (Her sister Bridget married Tobias Davis of Roxbury 13 December 1649.)[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann's birth date, birth place, and parents are unknown. We know her only as Robert Davis's wife, per his will of 14 April 1688.[2] She is often confounded with Anne Kingman (see above), but there is no evidence for the maiden name
2 CONC  of Robert's wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann married to Robert Davis. Their first child was recorded born on Jan 1645[/6?], so they may have married by 1645. Some propose she may have been a 2nd wife, in which case they may have married c. 1657.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Davis's Children:[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Deborah Davis, b Jan 1645[/6?]; m Thomas Greere, of Enfield
2 CONT Mary Davis, b May 1648
2 CONT Andrew Davis, b May 1650
2 CONT John Davis, b 1 Mar 1652
2 CONT Robert Davis, b Aug 1654
2 CONT Josiah Davis, b Sep 1656; m 25 Jun 1679 Anne Taylor
2 CONT Hannah Davis, b Sep 1658; m Unknown Dexter
2 CONT Sarah Davis, b Oct 1660; m Unknown Young
2 CONT Joseph Davis*
2 CONT Mercy/Marcy Davis;* m Unknown Young
2 CONT *Two of the children above were not recorded at Barnstable. A son named Joseph was mentioned in both Robert and Ann's wills, but no son named John. The son named "John" in the Barnstable Vital Record is assumed to have died young.
2 CONC  An alternate explanation is that the record may have been written Jos and was transcribed as Jo. Some propose Joseph was an eldest son born in England, and some propose he was a youngest son born in Barnstable, but not recorded.[
2 CONC 2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert's will mentioned "my two Daughters Sarah and marcy" and also "my Daughter mary." For this reason, we assume there were two different daughters named Marcy/Mercy and Mary. Ann mentioned only the one daughter, "Mercy Young." 
2 CONC This discrepancy is explained by making Ann a second wife, who only mentioned the younger children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caution: a different Robert Davis, of Sudbury, had two daughters: Rebecca and Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann's will was dated 5 May 1699 and was proved on 1 April 1701.[2] She named Robert Davis, my son Joseph's son, daughter Hannah Dexter, grandchild Sarah Dexter, son Josiah's wife, daughters Sarah Young and Mercy Young.[2][5] Accor
2 CONC ding to Otis, these were the names of the younger children only, thus proving that Ann was a second wife.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann died on March 31, 1701, in Barnstable, Massachusetts, having lived a long life of 75 years.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT The sources quoted actually say:
2 CONT Anderson: Ann, the daughter of Henry Kingman, married a Barnard, not a Davis. It was her sister Bridget who married Tobias (not Robert) Davis.
2 CONT SAR application: Ann the 2nd wife of Robert Davis died in 1701 (no yob, no maiden name).
2 CONT US passenger list: Anne Kingman aged 9 arrived in 1635 (so born 1626, but nothing to link this record to Ann the wife of Robert Davis).
2 CONT US marriages: Robert Davis (born 1630) married Ann (surname not given)(born 1635) in 1656 - nothing to link Ann to being a Kingman.
2 CONT AGBI: an Anne Davis was born in either England or Massachusetts in 1626 - nothing to connect her to either Ann the wife of Robert Davis or Ann the daughter of Henry Kingman.
2 CONT If you take all of these as being facts, then the only valid conclusion that can be drawn is that Robert Davis (born 1630) was married twice and his second wife was named Ann and she was born in 1635.
2 CONT Note: user-contributed sources can be used as hints for further research, but they do not substitute for primary records or peer-reviewed journal articles.
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ Anderson, Robert Charles, (1995) "Henry Kingman," Featured name. Great Migration 1634-1635, I-L. NEHGS AmericanAncestors.org. accessed 12 September 2016 (Volume IV, I-L, Page 180).
2 CONT ↑ 2.0 2.1 2.2 2.3 2.4 2.5 Otis, Amos, (1888) Genealogical Notes of Barnstable Families, Vol 1. Barnstable, MA: F.B. & F.P. Goss, Hathitrust.org (Vol 1, Pages 276-).
2 CONT ↑ (1902) "Barnstable, Mass. Vital Records." The Mayflower Descendant ..., Vol 4. Archive.org (Vol 4, Pages 222-24, citing Barnstable, Mass., Vital Records, p 408)
2 CONT ↑ Freeman, Frederick, (1869) The History of Cape Cod: the Annals of the Thirteen Towns of Barnstable County, Vol 2. Boston: W.H. Piper & Co., Hathitrust.org (Vol 2, Page 280, footnote).
2 CONT ↑ "Probate records v. 1-3 1686-1747" database with images, FamilySearch (https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-C9YB-N9L7 : 22 April 2024), Film# 00770540, Barnstable, Massachusetts, image 143, Vol 2, Page 124.
2 CONT See also:
2 CONT
2 CONT Freeman, Frederick, (1858) The History of Cape Cod ; the Annals of Barnstable ... Vol. 1. Boston: Archive.org (Vol 1, Page 578) Caution: Confounds Robert Davis with the Robert Davis, b 1608, m Margaret, sailed aboard the Confidenc
2 CONC e as a servant of Peter Noyes and settled at Sudbury.
2 CONT Freeman, Frederick, (1862) The History of Cape Cod: the Annals of the Thirteen Towns of Barnstable County ... Vol. 2. Boston: Geo C. Rand & Avery, Archive.org (Vol 2, Pages 182, 280 footnote).
2 CONT Genealogical Notes of Barnstable Families
2 CONT New England Marriages Prior to 1700
2 CONT Index type sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT U.S. and Canada, Passenger and Immigration Lists Index, 1500s-1900s. [search.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/sse.dll?db=pili354&h=1438695&ti=0&indiv=try&gss=pt//Page 127 Ancestry,com]
2 CONT U.S., Sons of the American Revolution Membership Applications, 1889-1970 [search.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/sse.dll?db=sarmemberapps&h=1161924&ti=0&indiv=try&gss=pt//Ann Davis Ancestry.com] Death Date 1701
2 CONT SAR Membership 53876, Role Ancestor, Application Date 1 Feb 1935, Spouse Robert Davis, Children, Joseph Davis
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT  Pioneers of Mass.:. (This is husbands will, Ann's will is listed below) Will Written 14 Apr 1688, proved 29 Jun 1693, names wife Ann, sons Joseph, Josiah, Robert & Andrew; daughters Deborah Geere, Sarah & Mary Davis, Hannah Dexte
2 CONC r.
2 CONT
2 CONT Disconnected from parents [http://www.geni.com/people/Henry-Kingman/6000000000426572412 Henry Kingman] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Joanna-Kingman/6000000000426572432 Johanna Kingman]  12/12/2015 by Hatte R. Blejer.  They had a
2 CONC  daughter Anne, who married a Mr. Barnard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical notes of Barnstable Families copy pasted Below:
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Davis died in 1693. His will is dated April 14, 1688,and proved June 29, 1693. He names his wife Ann. To his son Joseph he devises the land in the New Common Field, which he bought of the Indians ; and to Josiah he devises 
2 CONC the two acres of land in the Common Field, which the town granted to him in 1657. He also names Josiah's house lot, now owned by Lot Easterbrook's. He also names his son Andrew, to whom he gave five shillings, and his son Robert ;
2 CONC  also his daughters Deborah Geere, Sarah, Mercy, Mary Dexter, and Hannah Dexter. His estate was apprised at £75,13, a small sum ; but it must be remembered that money had not then been that depreciated, and that land at that time 
2 CONC was not valuable. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His widow, Ann Davis, died in 1701. Her will is dated May 5, 1699, and was proved April 1, 1701. She named Robert Davis, my son Joseph's son, daughter Hannah Dexter, grandchild Sarah Dexter, son Josiah's wife, and daughters Sarah 
2 CONC Young and Mercy Young. The fact that she names only the younger children, indicates that she was the second wife of Robert Davis. 
2 CONT
