2 CONT
2 CONT "The Rufus Parks Pedigree" by Brian J.L. Berry, Information: Pages 74 - 77, Chart: Page 76
2 CONT
2 CONT See Notes of WALTER GEER.
2 CONT
2 CONT      "JOHN GEERE, described as eldest son and heir of JOHN, in the Visitations of Devon, born about 1510, married, (1) Juliana, daughter of Anthony Butside, of St. Bodockes, Devon, (2), in November 1546, BEATRIX, daughter of JOHN 
2 CONC JERMYN, of Exeter, by whom he had four children: Nicholas, Andrew, GEORGE, and Alice. Andrew married, and had a daughter Anne, who married Richard Ashe of Sowton, in January 1616.  Alice married the Reverend William Varley, of Exe
2 CONC ter, in April 1583.  GEORGE, the youngest son, was the father of JONATHAN and three other sons, and the grandfather of GEORGE and Thomas, the American ancestors.
2 CONT
2 CONT      "Heavitree is a parish in the hundred of Wonford, County of Devon, one mile east of Exeter, containing about 7,500 population.  It was the western headquarters of the Parliamentary forces during the Civil War.  The parish is 
2 CONC a suburb of Exeter.  The church, dedicated to St. Michael, is in the later style of English architecture.  The name of Heavitree is said the have been derived from its having been a place of execution, the gallows being called "he
2 CONC avy (i.e. grievous) tree."
2 CONT
2 CONT      "Kenn is a parish in the hundred of Exminister, Devon, four miles south of Exeter.  The living is a rectory in the diocese of Exeter, formerly rated at less than fifty pounds sterling.  The church, dedicated to St. Andrew, ha
2 CONC s a stone front in the early style, and a good wooden screen.  John Geere, a cousin of JONATHAN, was a resident of Hallaway in this parish, and two of his descendents, both named Gilbert, were rectors of Kenn, between 1668 and 172
2 CONC 7.
2 CONT
2 CONT      "But little is known about JONATHAN [JOHN] GEERE, the immediate ancestor of the family in England. No record has been found of his baptism, or of that of his sons, as the Parish Register of Heavitree does not begin early enou
2 CONC gh to contain these records, the earliest entry being dated 1653.  Nor has any record been found of his will, after a careful search for wills of testators of that name proved in the Prerogative Court of Canterbury.  JONATHAN seem
2 CONC s, nevertheless, to have been a man of considerable property, and it is possible that he did not live in Devonshire the later years of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT      From the best information available, JONATHAN was born about 1580, and died prior to 1635, soon after the death of his wife.  He left two young sons, under the guardianship of an uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The Libraries of Ken, Kevin, Karen, Kristin, Brian, Adam, Amy, and FAL.
