1 NOTE Source notes:   Josiah Higley, the first born child of Josiah Higley and Dinah Gillett, was born in the parish of Turkey Hills, simsbury, Conn.  He was only 26 when his father died and received a special legacy from his father in 
2 CONC that he received two and a half acres more land than his siblings.  Soon after his fathers death he purchased more land in Turkey Hills from his cousin Johathan Higley, land that had once belonged to his grandfather, Captain John 
2 CONC Higley.  He moved the family to Westfield, Mass. in 1753 and later to Salisbury, conn. in 1756.  By 1770 however, he had returned with his family to Turkey Hills.  It seems probable that upon his mother's death, he took possession
2 CONC  of the ol home farm "on the highway that goes from Hatchet Hill to Windsor."  the entire famly, at about the same time, appears to have turned to the English church, or Episcopal faith.  this was in part due to the great disunity
2 CONC  and conflict in the churches of Simsbury.  Josiah and many of his cousins were confirmed in St. Andrew's parish, founded by the Rev. Roger Viets who had gone to England to be ofeained an Episcopalian.  Josiah, being of a patrioti
2 CONC c nature, enlisted in the Revolutionary Army "for the war, May 5th 1778," joining the 3rd Connecticut Regiment, Captain William Judd's company.  He was then a little past middle life.  It is probable that he was in the engagement 
2 CONC at Monmouth, NJ, as he was with Washington's main army that summer and went into camp at White Plains.  He was reported "missing" the following November.  The tradition in his family and descendants has always been that he was dro
2 CONC wned while crossing a river when his regiment was transferred, late in autumn, to winter quarters at Reading.  He was never heard from again.  Josiah married Hephzipah Cotten, but when and where are unknown.  they had seven childr
2 CONC en: Charity, Anna, Josiah, Jesse, Phillander, Sylvester and Theodocia.  SOURCE:  the Higleys and their Ancestry, 1963.
