2 CONT He was a man possessing an unshaken name for stability and integrity; a quiet citizen, who lived in sober style, attending to his farming industry. He was evidently faithful in his church relations. Mention is made of him in conne
2 CONC ction with town matters, February 6, 1771, and on the 13th of July, 1793, he sold land to his sons, Horace and Nathan, Jr. His name again appears, in June of the latter year, in an old sawmill account at Suffield, from which place
2 CONC  he probably purchased lumber.
