2 DATE 1776
2 PLAC Natchez, Adams, Mississippi, United States
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 2 SEP 1776
2 PLAC Natchez, Adams, Mississippi, United States
2 ADDR
1 TITL Rev
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Reverend Jedediah Smith (b 1721 (?) d 1776) and his wife Sarah Cook(e).  Rev. Smith was the first Presbyterian minister of the Natchez Territory. He died shortly after arriving from Granville, MA in 1776 with his t
2 CONC en or eleven children.  His lineage goes back five generations to the origins of the Hartford Connecticut Colony.  Three of his sons obtained Spanish land grants in about 1790 along Second Creek south of Natchez in Adams County wh
2 CONC ere they had plantations.  The three Smith brothers married three sisters named Cobb.  Israel was one of the three who married a Cobb, it's unclear who the other two were.  Information about four of the Smith brothers follows. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Calvin Smith (1768-1840)  Retirement Plantation - Adams MS - T6N-R2W section 42 owned by Calvin Smith. Monmouth - Adams MS - bought at public auction by Calvin in 1825.  Mr. Hankinson built the beginnings of the big house on Monmo
2 CONC uth in 1818. Calvin sold Monmouth to General Quitman in 1826. The Quitmans enlarged the big house and kept the plantation until after the Civil War. In the 1818 Adams County Census, Calvin Smith had 119 slaves on Retirement Planta
2 CONC tion nearby on Second Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Smith - owned T6N-R2W section 35 on Second Creek, which adjoined Retirement Plantation
2 CONT
2 CONT Philander Smith - owned T6N-R2W section 41 on Second Creek, which adjoined Retirement Plantation, In the 1818 Adams County Census, Philander Smith had 85 slaves on land which adjoined Retirement Plantation on Second Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT Israel Smith (b 1763 d 1821) - Rev. Jedediah Smith's son. Israel's plantation on Second Creek was this plantation - Independence Plantation. Also known as Mount Independence. Israel Smith and his second wife Jane Lorenzo Doane (fi
2 CONC rst wife was Sarah Cobb) - owners.  They had a lot of children - Ann Nancy, Courtland, Elizabeth, Hester, Horatio Nelson, Israel Philander, Jane Amanda, Josua Doane, Luther Sidney, Mary Isabella, Philomela, Susan and maybe a son n
2 CONC amed Calvin. In the 1818 Adams County Census, Israel Smith either had 30 or 38 slaves on Independence Plantation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Nancy Smith (Israel Smith's daughter) married Dr. John T. Scott.  They lived on Waverly Plantation in Adams County on Second Creek.  Waverly wasn't really a plantation with only 7 slaves and 75 acres.  It is mentioned in the b
2 CONC ook Tumult and Silence on Second Creek .
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Smith Scott (Dr. John and Ann Nancy Smith Scott's daughter - Israel Smith's granddaughter) was the second wife of Louisiana Supreme Court Justice Abner Nash Ogden.  There is an Ogden Road and an Ogden Creek near the location
2 CONC   of Independence Plantation south of Natchez along Highway 61 and Second Creek.  You can find them on the map at:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.gomdot.com/Divisions/IntermodalPlanning/Resources/Maps/pdf/CountyHighwayMaps/Adams.pdf .  Justice Ogden and his wife Julia lived in New Orleans.  Four of their sons fought in the Civil War.  Julia died at Mt. Independen
2 CONC ce Plantation in 1856.
2 CONT
2 CONT CPT William Frederick Ogden CSA - , attended Oakland College in Claiborne County, MS just before the Civil War.  After the war he attended law school at the Univ of VA and then married David Hunt's daughter Elizabeth.  David Hunt'
2 CONC s home plantation was Woodlawn Plantation MS in Jefferson County, MS.  William and Elizabeth inherited Hole In The Wall Plantation in Concordia Parish, LA and another plantation in Mississippi from David Hunt when his estate was d
2 CONC ivided shortly after the Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Robert Buhler - resident at Independence Plantation.  Grandson of Israel Smith.  John's wife was Mary Reynolds Buhler.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
