1 OCCU Arrived in Dorchester, MA in 1630 on the "Mary and John"
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Wikitree: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Thrall-8 William Thrall]
2 CONT '''The curator''' apparently doesn't accept Sandridge as William's place of origin. In Bank's book below, it shows William's origin coming from "Sandridge Parish" on a ship of unknown name.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Sicking-Points Queries
2 CONT # What reasonably legitimate source(s) proves that:
2 CONT ## that William Thrall was "Jr." to a William Thrall Sr.?
2 CONT ## this William was born on the specific date of 8 Aug 1605? If he was born in Sandridge parish, there is only one FamilySearch birth records for a William Thrall born around the same date – 18 Oct 1607 – whose father shows as Rap
2 CONC he Thrale. It seems reasonable that IF this William was baptized in Sandridge in 1605, then his birth record would exist.
2 CONT ###  "England Births and Christenings, 1538-1975", [https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:J94V-K9H database, FamilySearch]; (4 April 2024):
2 CONT ###  Name: William Thrale - Male
2 CONT ### Christening – 18 Oct 1607 – Sandridge, Hertfordshire, England
2 CONT ###  Father's Name: Raphe Thrale - Male
2 CONT
2 CONT ====BOOKS
2 CONT * '''Manwaring, Charles William, ed. 1902.''' ''A Digest of the Early Connecticut Probate Records.1677 to 1687''. Vol. I: Hartford District. Hartford, Connecticut: R. S. Peck & Company. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/A_Dige
2 CONC st_of_the_Early_Connecticut_Probat/T9MLAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=William+Thrall+1679+Windsor+Connecticut+probate&pg=PA370&printsec=frontcover Google Books].
2 CONT ** William Thrall – Will & Probate
2 CONT ** Page: 27-8
2 CONT ** Location: Windsor
2 CONT ** Died 3 August, 1679. 
2 CONT ** Invt. £158-09-06. Taken by Christopher Sanders, Jacob Drake. 
2 CONT ** Will dated 11 December, 1678.
2 CONT *** I William Thrall of Windsor do give and bequeath my Outward Estate, my House Barn & all my Lands of one sort or other, Upland or Meadow, to my son Timothy Thrall, to be his & his heirs forever, yt with this Proviso, that upon 
2 CONC his possession thereof he stand bound to discharge the Legacies as followeth: 
2 CONT ***First: that he pay to Daughter Phillip(i) Hosford the sum of £45, to be paid within 3 years after my decease. 
2 CONT ***Second: that he pay to Samuel Cross, & also to her that was Mary Cross, both which live with me, 5 Shillings apeice. 
2 CONT ***Third: That he pay to Mr. Benjamin Woodbridge 20 Shillings. It is my Will & I do give to my gr. child Sarah Hosford the little House I have begun to build on John Hosford's Land. For my Household Goods, what is in the possessio
2 CONC n of my son Timothy Thrall I do bestow on him, & what part of my Household Goods is in the possession of John Shepherd I do give to my daughter Phillipi Thrall. A Cow of mine in John Hosford's Hands I do give to Daughter Phillip(I
2 CONC ) Hosford, & the Cattle or Stock in my son Timothy Thrall's Hands I give to him. 
2 CONT ***I ordain my son Timothy Thrall Executor. Also I desire Mr. Benjamin Woodbridge and Abraham Phelps to be my Overseers.
2 CONT ** Witness: Job Drake, Mary Woodbridge.
2 CONT ** William X Thrall.
2 CONT ** Court Record, Page 18--4 December, 1679: Will & Invt. proven.
2 CONT * '''Banks, Charles Edward. (1957) 1976.''' ''Topographical Dictionary of 2885 English Emigrants to New England, 1620-1650''. Edited by Elijah Ellsworth Brownell. Baltimore, Maryland, USA: Southern Book Company; Genealogical Publi
2 CONC shing (Reprint). [https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/48159/images/TopDictNewEngland-007437-70?treeid=174604283&personid=132609541035&hintid=1037509445072&usePUB=true&pId=265652 Ancestry.com].
2 CONT * '''Stiles, Henry R. (1892) 1999.''' “Surnames, T-U.” In The History and Genealogies of Ancient Windsor, Connecticut…including East Windsor, South Windsor, Bloomfield, Windsor Locks, and Ellington 1635-1891. Vol. II: Genealogies 
2 CONC and Biographies. Hartford, Connecticut, USA: Clearfield. [https://archive.org/details/historygenealogi02stil/page/761/mode/1up?view=theater Archive.org].
2 CONT ** THRALL (Thrale, Thrail, Trall), William, came to W. before the Peq. War, in which he was a soldier from W. Ld. gr. 1640 (see p. 167, Vol. I); res. in vicinity of the Old Mill. In 1646 he and Robert Wilson bought out Simon Hoyte
2 CONC  in Hoyte's Meadow, where he lived alone with his family several years, the Court exempting him from the duty of "watch and ward," so that he might be at home nights with his family. His son Timothy succeeded him, and some of the 
2 CONC Thralls have ever since res. in this meadow. He d. "Augt the 3d, Sabbath day, 1679, 73 old." -0.0.R. "Old Goode Thrall," prob. his wife, d. 30 July, 1676. He contrib. 2s. 6d. to the Conn. Relief Fund for Poor of other Colonies, 16
2 CONC 76.-0. O. R. Ch. (2 acc. to O.C.R.):
2 CONT *** 1. Timothy, "b. 25 July, bp. 1641."-O C. R. – FAM. 2. | 2. David, acc. to Thrall Gen.
2 CONT * '''Thrall, Walter and Edward G. Randall. 1890.''' ''Genealogy of the Thrall Family: Also of the Rose Family to the Year 1862.'' Poultney, Vermont: Randall Brothers. [https://archive.org/details/genealogyofthral00thra/page/9/mode
2 CONC /1up?view=theater Archive.org].
2 CONT ** First Generation. — William Thrall was born in 1605; his wife’s name [called] Goode. They had two sons, Timothy and David; perhaps others. William died August 3d, (Sabbath) 1678, aged 73 years. His wife died July 30, 1676. Dr. 
2 CONC Styles, in speaking of the first Indian war in New Eng land, says: “Windsor was required to furnish thirty men for the offensive war against the Pequods, the names of only fifteen of whom can now be found.” William Thrall, our anc
2 CONC estor, was one of the fifteen. The great battle was fought on the 20th of May, 1637. The danger was imminent, and so complete the victory, that it caused universal rejoicing throughout New Eng land, and “ a grant of land was given
2 CONC  to each soldier and officer; and to this day the memory of an ancestor who was in the Pequod fight, is an honorable heirloom in every Connecticut family.”
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''William Thrall''' 
2 CONT BIRTH: 8 Aug 1605 Sandridge, St Albans District, Hertfordshire, England
2 CONT DEATH: 3 Aug 1679 (aged 73) Windsor, Hartford County, Connecticut, USA 
2 CONT BURIAL: Palisado Cemetery Windsor, Hartford County, Connecticut,
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/74934355/william-thrall
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of birth has also been (erroneously?) reported to be 1606.
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of death has also been (erroneously?) reported to be August 3, 1678.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT William Thrall, was born in England in 1605, and in 1630 became a member of the Congregational church, which was formed in Plymouth, England, under the ministry of Rev. John Wareham. On March 20, of that year, a company set out fr
2 CONC om Plymouth in the “Mary and John” And were put ashore at Nantasket Point, Massachusetts. May 30 Mr. Wareham and the larger part of his congregation settled at Windsor, Connecticut, in 1635, and William Thrall was probably among t
2 CONC hese. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He took an active part in the Pequot war, and in 1646 purchased land in Windsor, where he settled with his family. As this location was distant from any neighbors and exposed to attacks from the Indians, he was exempted from the d
2 CONC uties of “watch and ward,” to which all able bodied men of the settlement were liable. This location has ever since been occupied by his descendants, who have maintained the reputation of the family for thrift, industry, public sp
2 CONC irit and all that constitutes good citizenship. He contributed two shillings and six pence to the Connecticut relief fund for the benefit of the poor in other colonies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife “Old Goode Thrall,” died July 30, 1676. They had sons Timothy and David.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=74934355
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 	Aug. 8, 1605 Sandridge Hertfordshire, England Death: 	Aug. 3, 1679 Windsor Hartford County Connecticut, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT William Thrall was born in August, 1605 in Sandridge, Hertsfordshire, England, and baptised there on August 8. His father was also named William. The younger William Thrall came to Windsor before the Pequot War, in which he was a 
2 CONC soldier from Windsor. He was granted land in 1640. In 1646, he and Robert Wilson bought Hoyte's Meadow, which must have been far from the town, because he was exempted from the duty of "watch and ward", so he could be home nights 
2 CONC with his family. William died August 3, 1679 at 73 years. "Old Goode Thrall", probably his wife, died July 30, 1676. They were the parents of at least three children.
2 CONT
2 CONT William left a will, dated December 11, 1678. In it, he names his son Timothy and daughter Phillipi Hosford and granddaughter Sarah Hosford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography created by Ken. 
2 CONT
