1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Timothy Thrall BIRTH	25 Jul 1641 Windsor, Hartford County, Connecticut, USA DEATH	Jun 1697 (aged 55) Windsor, Hartford County, Connecticut, USA BURIAL	 Palisado Cemetery Windsor, Hartford County, Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/74978116/timothy-thrall
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of death has also been (erroneously?) reported to be January 7, 1694.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Timothy Thrall, son of William Thrall, was born July 26, 1641, died in June, 1697, aged fifty-six years. He was a prominent citizen in Windsor, and his name is found often on the records. He owned much property in the town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dawes-Gates refers to a church conflict in which Timothy was involved on the dissenting side. As early as 1666/7 dissension had arisen in the Windsor church: the dissenting group with permission of the General Assembly engaged the
2 CONC  Rev. Benjamin Woodbridge resulting in friction between the first and second church; on 13 May 1670 Timothy. Representing the second church, requested that the “disaffected might have the meeting-house their share of time on the S
2 CONC abbath and not cause a division.” Subsequently there was a bitter dispute over the matter of repair of the meeting house and the old town hall; townpeople refused to support it; Timothy and others wrote against “scandalous and off
2 CONC ensive writing” against the members of the Second Church and sought redress from the general assembly.
2 CONT
2 CONT The town of Tolland was originally the east part of Windsor, and in 1713 the town of Windsor appointed Matthew Alljn, Roger Wolcott, and Timothy Thrall to lay oot a settlement on the east side of Windsor, on lands purchased of the
2 CONC  Indians, which the committee performed and reported. Joseph Benton who had emigrated to Tolland from Hartford, made the record as town clerk in 1719. Notwithstanding in 1715, M. Allyn, R. Wolcott, T. Thrall, and John Ellsworth pe
2 CONC titioned the General Court to lay out a township, to be bounded east upon Willimantic river, &c., to contain 36 square miles, and to be called Tolland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The petition was granted, and a town 6 miles square, called Tolland, allowed to the Windsor petitioners. In May, 1719, the four petitioners conveyed the town of Tolland to 53 persons, but reserved to each of themselves 300 acres, 
2 CONC and these 53 became the actual settlers of Tolland, some of whom had been settled there as early as 1713 or '14. Amy Hatch was born there as early as 1713. Joseph Hatch the son of Joseph, was the first white male child born in Tol
2 CONC land in 1715. In 1719 Joseph Benton was one of a committee to build a meeting house there; the tame year he was appointed to procure a minister to preach there, and the Rev. Stephen Steel officiated at Tolland in 1720. Mr. Benton 
2 CONC was sworn first town clerk in 1719, and Shubael Steams in 1720. Delano, West, Cobb, Steel, Shepard, Chapman, Wells, Lathrop and Grant were among the early settlers of Tolland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Timothy, sen'r. was an early settler at Windsor. He died in 1697. He had an estate of neatly £800. Timothy Thrall married Deborah Gunn November 10th, 1659 in Windsor and had four sons and five daughters all born in Windsor
2 CONT * Deborah born August 19th, 1660; died May 16th, 1715 in Simsbury; married John Moses
2 CONT * Timothy born December 7th, 1662; died 1724 in Windsor; married Sarah Allyn
2 CONT * Mehitable born March, 1665; died August 15th, 1723; married Samuel Clark
2 CONT * Elizabeth born May 1st 1667;  died January 25th, 1714; married James Cornish
2 CONT * John born June 5th, 1671; died April 18th, 1732; married Mindwell Moses
2 CONT * Martha born May 31st, 1673; died 1710; married Nathaniel Pinney
2 CONT * Thomas born 1677; married Elizabeth Hoskins
2 CONT * Samuel born February 22nd, 1681 (twins); died 1709; married Elizabeth Owen
2 CONT * Abigail born February 22nd, 1681 (twins); died July 10th 1725; married Joseph Wilcoxson
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT * New England Families, Genealogical and Memorial: A Record of the Achievements of Her People in the Making of Commonwealths and the Founding of a Nation, Volume 1
2 CONT * Four American Ancestries: White and Griggs
2 CONT * A catalogue of the names of the first Puritan settlers of the colony of Connecticut
2 CONT * New England Marriages Prior to 1700
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Christened 25 Feb. 1641 in Windsor, Hartford, CT.
2 CONT
