2 CONT # Note: !BIRTH: The Hosford Genealogy book 1593-1993 - compiled and edited by Norman F. Hosford and David H. Hosford. p.10 !DEATH: The Hosford Genealogy book 1593-1993 - compiled and edited by Norman F. Hosford and David H. Hosfor
2 CONC d. p.10 !CHRISTENING: The Hosford Genealogy book 1593-1993 - compiled and edited by Norman F. Hosford and David H. Hosford. p.10 !BIOGRAPHY: Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England, 1620-33 The Great Migration Begins Ske
2 CONC tches !PRESERVED PURITAN  !BIOGRAPHY: WILLIAM HOSFORD  !BIOGRAPHY: ORIGIN: Beaminster, Dorset !MIGRATION: 1633 !FIRST RESIDENCE: Dorchester !REMOVES: Windsor 1635, Springfield 1653 !RETURN TRIPS: Returned to England permanently in
2 CONC  1654 !CHURCH MEMBERSHIP: Admission to Dorchester church prior to 1 April 1634 implied by freemanship. He would have retained his membership in this church when it moved to Windsor, and in his land inventory entry he is called eld
2 CONC er. In 1653 he was called to Springfield to be minister there after the departure of Rev. George Moxon, but he lasted no more than a year [Horsford Gen 13]. !FREEMAN: 1 April 1634 [MBCR 1:368]. !EDUCATION: He signed his will with 
2 CONC a firm but shaky hand. !OFFICES: Chosen fenceviewer for North Field in Dorchester, 8 October 1633, 10 February 1634/5 [DTR 3, 10]. !ESTATE: On 2 December 1633 it was "ordered that William Hosford shall have one of the two great lo
2 CONC ts that were Captain Southcott's" [DTR 4]; on 6 January 1633/4 it was "ordered that the marsh and swamp before Goodman Hosford and Davy Wil[ton] shall be divided among themselves and Symon Hoyte" [DTR 5]; granted four acres of mea
2 CONC dow, 17 April 1635 [DTR 11]; "Mr. Hosford" received Lot #61, two acres, in the meadow beyond Naponset [DTR 321]. In the Windsor land inventory of 2 February 1640[/1] "William Hosford elder" held seven parcels: "his houselot five a
2 CONC cres" (annotated "given to Stephen Tayler"); another "parcel on the west side of the highway containing three acres and half" (annotated "sold to Henery Clark"); "in the great meadow fourteen acres"; twenty-one acres in the Northw
2 CONC est Field; "over the great river in breadth next the fence twenty-nine rod to the end of the 160 rod & then it is in breadth thirty-five rod" (annotated "This to Stephen Tayler and Richard Samways"); "one acre of swamp joining to 
2 CONC the west bounds of the back lot" (annotated "this sold to Wa[l]ter Fylar"); and "by purchase of Walter Fyler three quarters of an acre" [WiLR 1:48]. On 3 March 1655[/6?] William Hosford granted all his lands and cattle to his son 
2 CONC John Hosford for his own use [WiLR 1:48]. On 7 April 1646 William Hosford of Windsor purchased three acres of meadow from George Stucky of Windsor [WiLR 1:115]. In undated transactions he exchanged three acres of meadow with Walte
2 CONC r Fyler and purchased of Elias Parkman a parcel of meadow and swamp of about eight acres [WiLR 1:115]. "William Hosford hath by his wife that was the wife of Henry Foukes deceased the homelot and land adjoining twenty-three acres 
2 CONC more or less that was the land of the said Henry Foukes" [WiLR 1:115]. On the reverse of the page that contained the land inventory of Henry Foukes is the entry that "Jane the wife of William Hosford hath reserved to herself" twen
2 CONC ty acres of meadow and swamp in the lower end of the town meadow [WiLR 1:156]. In his will, dated 27 August 1650 but not proved, "Will Horsford" bequeathed to "my wife Jane Horsford ... my dwelling house, half my barn and half my 
2 CONC cow house during the time of her life," also "that meadow I bought of Elyas Parkman ... during her life ..., also ... one hog, half the apples that grow in the orchard this year, five bushels of wheat, ten bushels of Indian corn, 
2 CONC also she is peaceably to enjoy the lands that were her own before my marriage only half an acre of the orchard ... I give to my son John Horsford" executor, also to him all lands and goods "after my wife's life"; "in case my son d
2 CONC ie before his mother" without children, then to "my two daughters' children equally 
