3 CITY Topsfield
3 STAE MA
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 FEB 1743
2 ADDR
3 CITY Hebron
3 STAE CT
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Ensign
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Samuel Waters, son of James Waters and Mary Stalworthy, was born in Topsfield, Essex Co., Massachusetts on May 22, 1679. He probably married in Lyme, Connecticut about 1702, with ANNE STERLING, dau. of William Ster
2 CONC ling and Anne Nichols. She was born in Haverhill, Massachusetts on Mar. 14, 1683/4. There is no record of the marriage, and no proof that Anne, the wife of Samuel, was a Sterling, but the naming of their grandson as Sterling Water
2 CONC s would seem to be more than conincidence. Also, there is an entry in the Hoyt Genealogy, a deposition of Aaron Haight, who married their granddaughter Jemima, who says his wifes grandmother  was Anne Sterling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children were: Joseph, Stalworthy (another family name), Samuel, Adam, Gideon and daughters Temperance, Ann Nox (Knox?), May, Lois, and Eunice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel moved to the Saybrook/Lyme, Connecticut area around 1700, probably because of Indian attacks on towns in Massachusetts. Anne moved with her family from Haverhill, Massachusetts to Lyme for the same reason. After Samuel and 
2 CONC Anne married their first three children were born in Saybrook, which is just across the Connecticut River from Lyme. In 1706, Samuel was a witness in court in Saybrook. In December, 1708, Samuel bought some land in Hebron, Connect
2 CONC icut, and the deed says that Samuel was a resident of Saybrook at that time. By 1710, they had moved to Hebron, and Samuel was granted a 100 acre lot. All the rest of Samuel and Anne's children were born in Hebron. Samuel was a pr
2 CONC ominent man there and was called upon to inventory several estates. Samuel was commissioned Ensign of the Train Bank in Hebron in October, 1722.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel wrote his will in Hebron on Jan. 24, 1742/3 and died there on Feb. 12, just a few days later. The will was exhibited on March 1st of the same year. In his will he names his wife, Anna, sons Joseph, Stalworthy, Samuel, Adam,
2 CONC  Gideon, and daughters Temperance, Ann Nox (Knox?), May, Lois and Eunice and grandson Timothy. The record of the death of Anne has not been found, but she was alive July 4, 1749, when she appeared in probate court in Colechester. 
2 CONC Samuel was buried in Godfrey Hill Cemetery in Hebron.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Find a grave
