1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Biography Reverend Samuel Peters son of John Peters and Mary Marks was born in Hebron, Hartford, Connecticut, British America on 1 Dec 1735. [1] [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages He was first married to Hannah Owen on 14 Feb 1760 in Hebron [1] but she died on 25 Oct 1765[1] at the age of 24.[1] However, he next married Abigail Gilbert on 25 Jun 1769 [1] but she died 19 days after their marriage o
2 CONC n 14 Jul 1769 at the age of only 17. Finally, on 21 Apr 1773 in Stratford, Connecticut, British America, [1] he married his 3rd wife Mary Birdseye
2 CONT
2 CONT United Empire Loyalists On 3 Apr 1783 Reverend Peters sought protection for his 8 year old son William Birdsaye Peters. Evidently, as he was going to England.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death He died on 19 Apr 1826 in New York state. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial "He was buried in Hebron's Godfrey Hill Cemetery, near the graves of his three wives, at the rear of his former church. In 1841, the body was transferred to the small cemetery at the rear of St. Peter's Episcopal Church whi
2 CONC ch was consecrated during the year that he died. An obelisk marks the grave. Erected by a grandson of the cleric, the monument has the following epitaph:"[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT His life was full of adventure, adversities, and trials which he bore with fortitude, patience and serenity. Sources ↑ 1.0 1.1 1.2 1.3 1.4 1.5 1.6 Edmond Frank Peters and Eleanor Bradley Peters, Peters of New England: A Genealogy,
2 CONC  and Family History (New York, NY, USA: The Knickerbocker Press, 1903), Pages 153, 155, 257, 258 ↑ Connecticut Marriages, 1640-1939," database with images, FamilySearch [1]: 12 May 2016; Connecticut State Library, Hartford ↑ "Carl
2 CONC eton Papers – Loyalists and British Soldiers, 1772-1784," Government of Canada, Library and Archives Canada [2]: viewed 29 January 2017), transcription, "British Headquarters Papers," document page 7305; microfilm M-361, ref MG23 
2 CONC B1, Item 42096 ↑ Bologna Connecticut Valley Historical Society, Papers and Proceedings (The Society, Springfield, Mass., 1881) Vol. 1, Page 91 Bologna, Sando. What Sort of Tory Was Reverand Peters?, "My Country" (My Country Societ
2 CONC y, Litchfield, Conn., Winter, 1994) Vol. 28, No. 1, Page 2-9. This source gives his birth date as Nov. 20, 1735.
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 1805 he (Samuel Andrew Peters) returned to America. He spent several years in Washington, petitioning for confirmation of a grant of land near St. Anthony's Falls and in October, 18`17, then in failing health and eighty-two ye
2 CONC ars of age, he visited that part of the country where he passed over a year and spent the winter at Prairie du Chien. In 1818 he removed to New York and lived in that city and in Jersey City until his death." (from "Peters of New 
2 CONC England: a genealogy, and family history",  By Eleanor Bradley Peters)
