1 NAME George /Phelps/
2 GIVN George
2 SURN Phelps
2 _MARNM Phelps
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1613
2 ADDR
3 CTRY England
1 BURI
2 DATE 1687
2 PLAC Old Burying Ground
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 45 Mechanic Street
3 CITY Westfield
3 STAE Massachusetts
3 POST 01085
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 8 MAY 1687
2 ADDR
3 CITY Westfield
3 STAE Dominion of New England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Phelps Family research, NEHGS research, and DNA have all since proven that George and William Phelps were not related.
2 CONT
2 CONT History: The brothers George and William Phelps emigrated from Gloucestershire, England, to Dorchester, MA, c. 1630. Five years later they moved to Windsor, CT. George's sixth-generation descendant, Anson Greene Phelps (1781–1853)
2 CONC , rose from being a penniless orphan to the status of a major industrialist and a prominent CT philanthropist.
2 CONT *Dictionary of American Family Names © Patrick Hanks 2003, 2006.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''There is no proof found that he was the son of William Phelps and Dorothy James. They have been detached as parents.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ===George Phelps
2 CONT *b. circa 1613 England 
2 CONT * d. 8 May 1687 Westfield, Hampshire County, Dominion of New England
2 CONT *Place of Burial:	Old Burying Ground, Westfield, Hampden County, MA, United States
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT George Phelps, parents unknown. Married (1) Phillury Randall, daughter of Philip Randall and Joan (__?__), circa 1637. (2) Frances (__?__) (widow of Thomas Dewey and (__?__) Clark).
2 CONT
2 CONT American Marriages Before 1699
2 CONT Web Page (Link to the Record)
2 CONT https://search.ancestry.com/collections/2081/records/6889
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of George Phelps and Phillury Randall
2 CONT # Capt. Isaac Phelps+ b. 20 Aug 1638, d. 2 Jun 1725  
2 CONT # Capt. Abraham Phelps b. 22 Jan 1641/42, d. 25 Jan 1727/28  
2 CONT # child Phelps d. 1647  
2 CONT # child (Abigail?) Phelps d. 1649  
2 CONT # Capt. Joseph Phelps b. 24 Jun 1647, d. 1695 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of George Phelps and Frances (__?__)
2 CONT # Jacob Phelps+ b. 7 Feb 1649/50, d. 6 Oct 1689 
2 CONT # Sergt. John Phelps+ b. 15 Feb 1651/52, d. c 1741/42 
2 CONT # Nathaniel Phelps b. 9 Dec 1654, d. Jun 1723
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT It would be nice to think that this is the George Phelps who came over on the Mary & John, but the most reliable sources suggest that it is not.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Phelps married (1) Phillury Randall, daughter of Philip Randall and Joan (__?__), circa 1637. He married (2) Frances (__?__) (widow of Thomas Dewey and (__?__) Clark) on 2 November 1648 in Windsor, Hartford Co., Conn.. He d
2 CONC ied on 8 May 1687 in Westfield, Hampshire (Hampden a. 1812), Mass..
2 CONT
2 CONT * Immigration: before 1635, Possibly the "George P--?--" on the "Recovery" which arrived from Weymouth in June/July 1634. 
2 CONT * Freeman: on 6 May 1635 in Dorchester, Suffolk Co., Mass.. 
2 CONT * Religion/Church: A member of Mr Warham's Church. 
2 CONT * Education: Signed with his mark. Books were included in his will. 
2 CONT * Residence: in 1635 From Dorchester to Windsor with Warham. 
2 CONT * Residence: in 1667 Removed from Windsor to Westfield, Massachusetts. 
2 CONT * Residence: on 9 January 1667/68 Listed as one of the first of Main St. in Westfield, MA. 
2 CONT * Military Service: 1658 - the Troop of Horse serving under Capt. John Mason; Undoubtedly served in King Philip's War in 1675.6 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Seen as born circa 1605 possibly in Porlock, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The origin of George Phelps is unknown. Though many claim that he is the brother of William Phelps, there is no documentation to support this so we must consider this conjecture. It is likely that the English origin of George migh
2 CONC t be found in either Porlock or Crewkerne. In some manner as yet undetermined, George Phelps was an uncle of Elisha Hart, son of Edmund Hart.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly the George P[ __ ] on the "Recovery" which sailed from Weymouth and arrived in Massachusetts Bay in June/July 1634, our first record of George Phelps is when he was chosen as one of ten men "to order the affairs of the Co
2 CONC lony for one year." He was made a Freeman on 6 May 1635 and was probably among the first of those who made the trek to the Connecticut River and settled Windsor in 1635.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 11, 1658, the General Court met in Hartford and established a Troop of Horse to act in the colonies defense under the direct command of Captain John Mason. These troops were to outfit themselves with their own horse, armo
2 CONC r and firearms. The thirty-seven men who made up this troop were, for the most part, of the second generation and sons of some of the most influential families in the colony. Many of the men found among these original thirty-seven
2 CONC  would eventually help shape the future of the Colony. Among these influential men we find George Phelps. In 1637 George married Phillury Randall and their first child would be born the following year. On Sunday 11 October 1640 "W
2 CONC hile Mr. Hunt [sic. Huitt] was preaching to the good people of Windsor, from Romans 12-17, during the lecture George Phelps's house was burned so that it (the house) went over." After the fire he resided on the Dewey lot North of 
2 CONC the Palisado. Phillury died in April of 1648 leaving George with three young sons. The following November George married Frances who would give George Phelps three more sons. Frances was the widow of Thomas Dewey and the widow of 
2 CONC an unknown Clark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier assertions that Frances was first married to Joseph Clark of Windsor appear unfounded so there is little known of her origins. In Dorchester Massachusetts in 1640 there were three brothers William, Thomas and George Clark.
2 CONC  These brothers "died soon after, and nearly at the same time." It could be possible that Frances was of this family.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1668 George applied for renewal of a grant of land he had received in Westfield Massachusetts and, in 1670, he moved the remainder of his family to Westfield. It's probable that some of his sons had already taken up residence i
2 CONC n Westfield. Indian trouble was brewing and these were dangerous times to take up residence in outlying areas. Such a move would not be for the faint of heart and George's military experience was probably valued in the new settlem
2 CONC ent.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April of 1687 George deeded a considerable portion of his Westfield land to John Gunn. It may be that he intended to return to Windsor, however, this he did not do as he died the following month. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Fall of 1635, he removed to the founding of Windsor, Ct, with the Rev. Mr. Warham's Church, of which he was a member, in all about sixty.  He located in Windsor, at the junction of the Farmington and Great (Connecticut) Riv
2 CONC ers.
2 CONT
2 CONT APRIL: YE 24TH: 1683:  The last Will & Testament of George Phelps of Westfield, in the County of Hamshire, in the Colony of the Massachusetts Company, who being cracy of Body though yet my Memory of understanding by God's Goodness
2 CONC  is continued:  yet not knowing the tyme of my departure which I wait for, & Desire to commit my Soule to God that gave it & my Body to christian Burial; & Leave this as my Last Will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Concerning my wife If it shall please God to continue her after me, & concerning my children:  And before I come to the disposal of my outward Estate, I desire thanke fully to acknowledge ye great and abundant Goodness of God to m
2 CONC e in takeing care of me & providing for me the Comforts of this life, the hopes of a better, in and through the abundant riches of his grace through Jesus Christ:  And I desire to Leave this as a word of most solemn & weighty coun
2 CONC sel to my deare children, firstly and chiefly above all things that they cleave to God, & to the covenant of his grace with all theire hart, & with all their souls laboring in sincerity to keep his covenant & to walke in his wayes
2 CONC , to observe his statutes & do ym. & to associate ym selves with them that feare God to live in love & peace both one with another & with all men, that so the God of Love & peace may dwell with them & keep them in his feare & crow
2 CONC n ym with his blessings especially with spiritual blessings in Cnt Jesus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Concerning my Dear wife, I exhort my children to be careful & tender of her, Loveing and dutyful towards her in all things, that she want nothing that may be necessary to her comfortable subsistence, to hearken & attend to her Cou
2 CONC nsel from time to time: and I give unto my wife, all my bedding & house hold stuffe for her use & to be at her free dispose at her decease, and twenty pounds which is due to me from Taham Graunt of Windsor, if she need it not hers
2 CONC elfe, to be disposed of by her to such of her relations as she shall judge have most need:  And for her maintainence while she lives, I give unto my wife Two pounds fifteen shillings p Annum, to be paid by my Son Joseph Phelps of 
2 CONC Windsor, in halfe wheate and the halfe Indian Corn, during her life:  Also I give unto my wife six pounds per Annum to be paid by my three Youngest sons, Jacob, John & Nathaniel, each of them forty shilling to be yearly paid durin
2 CONC g her life:  further I give unto my wife the use of my Land at Windsor, which Lyes on the east side of the great River, being Ten Rods in breadth more or less, & a quarter part of yt orchard & if these be expended & the use of yea
2 CONC rly income be not sufficient for her comfortable maintenance, then a part of all this Land shall be disposed of as need shall be:  But if the Sale of this Land be not needed for my wive's necessary use whilst she lives, then after
2 CONC  her decease with what other Lands, now in Common may afal to me at Windsor, I give to my Sons as followeth:
2 CONT
2 CONT To my son Isaac a Third Part & to my son Joseph a Third part, & the remaining third part I give to my sons Jacob, John and Nathaniel:  Also I give to my son Jacob four acres of the Northerly end of my home lot here in Westfield, w
2 CONC ith the  house, barns & orchard  upon it, only reserving one end of the house for my wife whilst she lives:  And seeing he hath all my buildings here, he shall help his brothers John & Nathaniel, if ye build in Westfield, each of 
2 CONC them a month work with his own hands, & his Team six days:  And a third part of the fruit of the orchard to John if he live here, & a third part to Nathaniel for seven yeeres after the date here of.   They maintaining each of ym a
2 CONC  third part of the fence of the orchard:  Also I give to my son Jacob a third part of my Meadow Lands in Westfield, the whole being in estimation four score acres:   further I give to my son John all my land on the East side of th
2 CONC e Highway at my house with the Low Land by the River, for a home lot, one third part of my meadow Land in Westfield, the whole being in estimation 80 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give my son Nathaniel the remainder of my House Lot, being about seven acres & one third part of my Meadow in Westfield, the whole being in estimation four score acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give to my son Isaac, the best coats of my wearing apparel & My Mare, the colt I give to his son Isaac my grandchild:  the rest of my wearing apparel, I give to my Sons Jacob, John and Nathaniel.  That this is my last will & tes
2 CONC tament if I doe not otherwise order before my Decease.  I testify by subscribing my hand; And doe desire Ensign Lumes & my Son Isaac Phelps be over seers, & take care that my Last will be pformed to the true Intent & meaning there
2 CONC of:  And I doe order my three youngest Sons to se al my Just debts paid.  And if anything appeare dubious, or any difficulty arise in or by the Interpreting, or understanding of my will or anything relating thereunto, I order it m
2 CONC ay be referred to the hearing & determination of or Reverend Pastor Mr. Taylor and Ensign Lumes, whom I impower to determine the same as they shall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jude right:  That this is my Last will & Testament I testify by subscribing my hand the day & year before mentioned.
2 CONT
2 CONT the Mark  X  of George Phelps
2 CONT
2 CONT Subscribed in the presence of:  Edward Taylor, Samuel Loomes, Isaac Phelps.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Edward Taylor & Samuel Loomes did Personally appeare before John
2 CONT
2 CONT Pyncheon Esq., the Judge of the prerogative Corte for the tyme of George Phelps deceased his signing the writing on the three sides this Instrument, & that he did sign it as his last Will and Testament, and that he was of sound me
2 CONC mory & mind to the best of their discerning, and Isaac, Jacob & John & Nathaniel Phelps, four of said deceased sons, are approved & legally confirmed to be Administers to the Estate of ye said father & to see to the true performan
2 CONC ce of this their said father's last will and testament according to the true Intents year of: 
2 CONT
2 CONT As attests John Holyoke, Clerke of Said Corte.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 15th, 1687, this is here recorded in these records for Will from the original last will and Testament of the above said George Phelps of Westfield late deceased.
2 CONT
2 CONT P. John Holyoke, Clerke for said Records
