2 CONT THE PHELPS FAMILY OF AMERICA - STORY HIGHLIGHT 
2 CONT
2 CONT “Some branch of the family left Plymouth many years ago, for some part of America.” Abraham Phelps
2 CONT The author of the book “The Phelps Family of America” begins by mentioning various places in Gloucestershire, Somersetshire, and Staffordshire in Old England where Phelpses are discovered, and then continues with letters of corres
2 CONC pondence.   In his book, he states that families and historians alike knew the colonists came from Somerset and Dorset area, but then looks further away to Gloucestershire for the connection.  The author erred in his conclusion th
2 CONC at the William Phelps born in Tewkesbury in 1599 was the colonist and our connection to the Phelps family.  Much of his correspondence with other branches of the Phelps family, and the conclusions drawn by readers of his book, is 
2 CONC tainted by statements made throughout the first 72 pages such as that the William Phelps born in 1599 in Tewkesbury is “unquestionably” the colonist, that William and George were brothers, and that this Tewkesbury branch is suppor
2 CONC ted by sources as being the connection we are searching for.  While the family of James Phelps born about 1520 through his son William born in 1560 could very well be the ancestors of the Somerset families, there is still the poss
2 CONC ibility they originated from some other branch in Gloucester or Staffordshire.  Many of his correspondents believed their roots went back to this Tewkesbury family.  However, his letters and research remain—research.
2 CONT
2 CONT If we disregard his statements supporting this theory in his writings, we can see from the correspondence he received that the strongest indicator of the colonists’ origins lie in the statements made by Abraham Phelps of Somerset 
2 CONC that it was his family’s tradition that “Some branch of the family left Plymouth many years ago, for some part of America.”  (William Phelps’ ship sailed from Plymouth; George, who probably arrived on the Recovery, sailed from Wey
2 CONC mouth.)  The author concluded Abraham Phelps “was confident that he was of the same stock as the Tewkesbury family” and the author determined, correctly or not, that Abraham was “probably of the descendants of James, a brother of 
2 CONC Mr. William Phelps” (p 35).
2 CONT
2 CONT As I study the family of Abraham Phelps, the family that lived in the Porlock area, I find a good number of Richard Phelps, but not William.  Almost nothing is known of the Richard Phelps who arrived in America about 1630, then sa
2 CONC iled for Barbados and was never heard from again.  Other than the fact that the Recovery sailed from Weymouth rather than Plymouth, George Phelps in America named his sons Abraham, Isaac and John, this Porlock family seems to be t
2 CONC he family in the Somerset area who used these names within their family; not so within William Phelps' (the colonist) family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first letters in the book are about the Irish line of the Phelps family who trace their first ancestor in Ireland as Thomas Phelps who was born in 1623, the son of Thomas Phelps [born 1590] who was the son of William Phelps [b
2 CONC orn 1560]; this family originated in Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire, England.   “Thomas Phelps, the founder of the Irish family of Phelpses, born as we have it in 1623, came from the neighborhood of Tewkesbury…” (p 24).  “The Phelps 
2 CONC who went over to Ireland, as tradition tell us, about 1646…” (p 21).  “We have undoubted authority for claiming this Thomas Phelps as our ancestor, for the estate which was descended from father to son from his time, and which was
2 CONC  granted to him by O. Cromwell, and confirmed by Charles II, and was in my father’s hands until about forty years ago.  I am the only child of my father Thomas Phelps, and was therefore heir to this estate.  It is situated in the 
2 CONC County of Tipperary, Ireland…  Now Thomas Phelps is reported to have come from near Tewkesbury in Gloucestershire, and our arms are a wolf salient in shield with six crosslets.  This Thomas Phelps is reported to have been a Quaker
2 CONC  convert soon after he went to Ireland” (p 22).  The tradition of my family is this, that they originally came from the neighborhood of Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire; that the family resided previously for 800 years in that locality
2 CONC  (p 23).
2 CONT
2 CONT This evidence is followed by his research in the Somerset area.  The following are excerpts from the letters he received from his inquiries.  Mention of Abraham Phelps starts at about page 35 and dated 1860.  The heading is Porloc
2 CONC k, Minehead, Somerset, December 13th, 1860.  “My Dear Sir:  I have seen Abraham Phelps often lately, and have read your letters addressed “to the Clerk of Porlock, and thinking that you will get but little information from them, r
2 CONC elative to your inquiries about the Phelps family, I have taken it on myself to give you an outline of the matter, gleaned from Abraham Phelps, this morning, and will prosecute any further inquiries you may wish to institute relat
2 CONC ive to the Phelps pedigree.
2 CONT
2 CONT “The Phelps family were originally Italians, and were then called “Welfs.”  From Italy they removed to Germany, and took the name of “Gulphs,” thence to Scotland, where they were called Phelps (the tradition is) from Scotland, the
2 CONC y sailed for Bristol, but through stress of weather they were driven to Porlock, where they settled down and bought property.  The father of the present Abraham Phelps (now about fifty-four) died about twenty-seven years ago at th
2 CONC e age of ninety-seven.  [Abraham’s father was born about 1736.  This was only 106 years after the departure of the “Mary and John.” For this information regarding their knowledge about the branch of their family that left Plymouth
2 CONC  “many years ago” for America to be their family tradition, should carry a lot of weight considering this time frame.]
2 CONT
2 CONT “In the village church at Porlock, there are believed to be papers written in old Latin characters, which may explain more of the family. [This could be important research.]
2 CONT
2 CONT “Some branch of the family left Plymouth many years ago, for some part of America.  These are the outlines of the Phelps family, furnished me by Abraham Phelps this morning… I would also say there was one of this family, Isaac Phe
2 CONC lps, living near Bridgewater, who died some time since, leaving his property, something considerable, to some people he had about him at the time, though no relatives…  Faithfully yours, Robert Frekke Jould, Rector of Stoke Minehe
2 CONC ad.”
2 CONT
2 CONT “My Dear Sir:  Circumstances having brought me to Porlock for a short time, my uncle, Mr. Abraham Phelps, has given me your letters, and begged me reply to them as he has a great dislike to letter writing…  Minehead is about forty
2 CONC -four miles from Exeter.  Next, my uncle Abraham says, that there is no doubt that Mr. William Phelps (b. Tewkesbury, 1560, A. T. S.) you mention in your letter, was the great-great-grandfather of the late Mr. Henry Phelps [Abraha
2 CONC m’s father], of whose family these are only two now living, viz: My uncle, Abraham, and my mother, once Eleanor Phelps… My uncle is the last of the name. He is unmarried, a great sportsman, lives on his property, and is about fift
2 CONC y-six years of age. He is the youngest of my grandfather’s family.”  She continues, “The church book seems to have been very badly kept, as there are scarcely any notices of baptisms, marriages, etc., and upon examination there do
2 CONC  not appear to be any papers in the parish chest, which throw any light upon the Phelps family… But Mr. Rowcliff, solicitor, Stogumber, Somerset, has been their legal adviser for many years, and doubtless he knows more of the fami
2 CONC ly pedigree than any one else.  Your Sincere English Friend, Ellen Elizabeth Phelps Drew” of Barnstable (p 37).  (It is really too bad correspondence was not initiated with this solicitor.)  Her uncle Abraham died, and the only in
2 CONC formation she could provide began about 1647.  (There is a delightful letter dated 1874 written by Abraham Phelps to Oliver which completely typifies the English gentleman and his love of the sports, but provides absolutely no inf
2 CONC ormation.  He died the following year, “the last of a long line of Phelpses, the name as far as our family goes, will become extinct as a surname.”)
2 CONT
2 CONT **This mention of a branch of Abraham Phelps' family going to America years before... There are a lot of Richard Phelps in the Phelps family of the Porlock area.  Abraham's father was Henry Phelps.  Besides William Phelps of Crewk
2 CONC erne, and George Phelps the colonist, there is Richard Phelps who sailed for Barbados.  (Porlock is 52 miles west of Crewkerne, on the coast. Porlock is at the mouth of the channel; Tewkesbury is another 108 miles up the channel t
2 CONC o the head, a port area.)  A few years after William and George immigrated, a Henry Phelps immigrated to America on the "Hercules" in 1633/34, but it sailed from London bound for Salem.
2 CONT
2 CONT The remainder of the letters seem to support the belief that the Phelps family spread to Somerset from the Tewkesbury family.  More research should be conducted about the family of James Phelps and his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Correspondence from Dursley, Gloucestershire in 1863, follows the line of Thomas Phelps who “came from Salisbury and settled in Dursley, where he was buried Feb. 14th, 1647…  It seems probably that my ancestors came to Dursley at 
2 CONC about the same time yours settled in America.”  After listing his lineage, he states “Of this last I am the only surviving son, neither of my brothers nor myself having married (p39).  The author concludes “Thomas Phelps, the foun
2 CONC der of this family, removed from Salisbury as far back as this family’s records go.  He may have been connected with the Tewkesbury Phelps family, and a descendant of James Phelps, through his son Thomas, born 1563.  Thomas, son o
2 CONC f Thomas and Ann of same time; of this we have no authentic dates or authority.  Many of the descendants of James Phelps settled in the lower counties.  Salisbury is about 70 miles south of Tewkesbury” (and east of Minehead about 
2 CONC the same distance.)  (Later, the author states that it is the Salisbury Phelps family, an offshoot of the Tewkesbury family, that are the connection to John Phelps who was the clerk of the court that convicted Charles I, p 50.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The next letter (p 40) is from a William Phelps of Exeter written in 1872.  He is a “neighbor” of Abraham Phelps’ niece who corresponded and related the death of her uncle.   He is relating information from the area directly south
2 CONC  and west of Minehead; Barnstaple is 44 miles west, Exeter is 45 miles south of Minehead.  The niece, Mrs. Drew, mentioned both Barnstaple and Exeter as her location.  William forwards information from his uncle, “the Rev. John Ph
2 CONC elps of Hatherleigh, North Devon, the oldest living Phelps of our clan, who remembers some 70 winters… The earliest of our family of whom we have any authentic record, was one William Phelps, my great grandfather, born of respecta
2 CONC ble, and more than that, well to do parents in Wilton, three miles from Salisbury…  William Phelps, however, had a soul above the scissors” and became a schoolteacher.  He settled in Exeter.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Plymouth letters dated 1861 written by Lizzie Phelps pertain to the family of Richard Phelps, who had a son (James, Lizzie’s brother) emigrate to Canada in 1854.  Her branch of the family was very limited and her great grandfather
2 CONC  was James Phelps of the City of London.  Her grandfather, James Phelps always lived in Plymouth.  However, she states “My father has three sisters.  My mother, sister and myself are all who are left of our family:  in fact there 
2 CONC is no one of the same name as ourselves any where here at all” (p 42).  (Interestingly, she mentions the “great bell of St. Paul’s, London, was made by Richard Phelps, whose name is inscribed on it.  My father having once ascended
2 CONC  the belfry to see it, from curiosity, it being his own name.  The bell was placed there about 1710.  There are also some descendants of my great grandfather’s family still residing in London…”)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bristol is in North Somerset about 48 miles south of Tewkesbury and 58 miles north of Bridgewater, and the next letter is from an Isaac Phelps.  He doesn’t have the time to help with the research, but states that his grandfather’s
2 CONC  name was William Phelps, a tailor by trade as was his father.  His grandfather had brothers, but doesn’t know anything about them.  By his grandfather’s first wife, his father Esau was born and a brother, Isaac, a stonemason.  Is
2 CONC aac was in the English army in North America and never returned…1773 (p 43). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Discounting his conviction that he had made the correct connection between William Phelps, the colonist, to the Phelps family of Tewkesbury, the author summarizes his conclusions and the history of the Phelps family from Italy and
2 CONC  Germany and the Royal connection on pages 44 through about page 50.  “The earliest trace of the family, dates back to the eleventh century or thereabouts.  The family was originally settled in the north of Italy, and was early tr
2 CONC ansplanted to Germany, there assuming the name ‘Guelphs.’  The identity of the name ‘Guelphs’ and ‘Phelps’ accords with a tradition which I have met in several branches of the Phelps family, viz:  that we are from the same stock w
2 CONC ith the House of Hanover, of whom Queen Victoria is a representative.  The Queen, it is well known, derives her linage through the ‘Guelphs’ of Germany, from the old Welfs of Lombardy.  The history of the ‘Guelphs’ you will have n
2 CONC o difficulty in tracing” (p 47). 
2 CONT
2 CONT “When the change from Guelph to Phelps took place; when the family became domiciled in England; from what branch of the Guelphs the Phelpses descended; how near related the Phelpses of England are to the noble stock, from which th
2 CONC e Queen derives her descent across the channel—I do not know.  Any such questions as these, could be determined only by a visit to the oldest library of Great Britain” (p 49).
2 CONT
2 CONT “Bennett’s History of Tewkesbury, page 182, refers to Francis Phelps with Francis Morris as purchasing of King James I, in 1608, the rectory of Tewkesbury under privileges.  We have no record of this family, but suppose he belonge
2 CONC d to the Montacute family.  From these correspondents and other records” the author determined the emigrants to America were descendants of James Phelps of that place, born about 1520.  This seems to be true from every indication,
2 CONC  just not through his son William and grandson William.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further mention of this Montacute family on page 43 seems to indicate a possible separate branch of the family dating back a little farther.  “Montacute family are Phelips not Phelps—the latter is a common name in Somerset and Dor
2 CONC set:  and a family of Phelps, bearing for their arms “a lion rampant” were respectable landed proprietors of this parish, time of Elizabeth.”
2 CONT
2 CONT In the author’s conclusions on page 44 he also mentions Montacute.  “Charles H. Phelps, Esq., son of Hon. Charles A. Phelps, M.D., of Boston, Mass., who has resided abroad for many years, refers in his family tree to James Phelps 
2 CONC of Tewkesbury, England, born about 1520, as a brother of Francis Phyllyppe, who is referred to in “Burke’s Commons” as of Nether Teyne in the parish of Checkley, County of Stafford, and thinks they were sons of Richard Phyllyppe o
2 CONC f that place.  This family, first, as early as the 15th century, spelled their name Phyllyppe, in the 16th century as Phelep, and recently as Phelips.  Wilfiam Phelips, of Montacute, is the present head of this family.”  And “ther
2 CONC e were no emigrants at any time” from this family (p 44) nor did they ever spell their name as Phelps (p 46).  It is at this point that the author mentions the “History of Tewkesbury” reference and Francis Phelps purchasing the re
2 CONC ctory of Tewkesbury and supposing this purchase was made by the Montacute family of which he has no records.  This would suggest the centuries old origins of the Phelps (Felpe, Phelf, Philips or otherwise, p 47) family in Tewkesbu
2 CONC ry and divided family lines still in the area.   (Montacute is very near Yeovil, and not far from Crewkerne.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Here begins the author’s statements and conclusions regarding the origins of William Phelps and George Phelps.  I will relate his conclusions leaving out his mistaken references regarding the relationship between William and Georg
2 CONC e, and his supposition that William was the son of William and born in 1599.
2 CONT
2 CONT “From these correspondents and other records, we trace the following emigrations from the Tewkesbury Phelps family, descendants of James Phelps of that place born about 1520.
2 CONT
2 CONT “1st William Phelps… and George Phelps.  We find no record of the latter’s baptism…  (p 44) These two… emigrated from Plymouth, England, in 1630, settling in Dorchester, Mass. 
2 CONT “Says the Rev. Robert Freke Jould, rector of Stokes Minehead, Porlock, England, referring to the family of the late Abraham Phelps of that place, who believed he was of the Tewkesbury family, “Some branches of this family emigrate
2 CONC d to America many years ago.”
2 CONT “We believe this emigration to have been the two… William and George Phelps, first referred to; of these, William Phelps married in England, and had six children, who emigrated with him from Plymouth in 1630.  ['Six' was not accur
2 CONC ate, he included Richard and Sarah. He actually had four living children; three more were born in America:  Sarah, Timothy and Mary.]
2 CONT “We find no record of his marriage or children… It is quite probable he married and settled father south in Somerset, Lancaster of Dorsetshire, which would account for his having been referred to by many of his American descendant
2 CONC s as coming from either of these counties (p 45).  (He found four other emigrants connected with the Tewkesbury family, but they are all of later dates.  There were four other emigrants, also of a later date, which he determined w
2 CONC ere not connected to the Tewkesbury family, p 46.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham Phelps lived at Porlock, Somerset, England not far from Minehead near the top of the map on the coast.  The Phelps family was established in Tewkesbury for centuries and branched out from there.  They can be found in Somer
2 CONC setshire, and even into Ireland (p 34), stemming from Tewkesbury, Gloucestershire.  
