2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Daniel and Mary (Newberry) Clark. Married circa 1685 to Mary Crowe, daughter of Christopher & Mary (Burr) Crowe. She m. 2nd William Randall. Inventory & distribution of will at Windsor, CT, 1715.
2 CONT
2 CONT DESCENDANTS IN THE LINE OF JOHN CLARKE, SON OF DANIEL CLARKE, THE SETTLER. 
2 CONT SECOND GENERATIONL
2 CONT V.
2 CONT John Clark, first of Simsbury, Conn., where he was admitted an inhabitant, December 24, 1686, and after 1698, of Windsor, Conn., was married to Mary Crow, daughter of Christopher Crow, of Windsor, and of his wife Mary, Daughter of
2 CONC  Benjamin Burr, of Hartford, Conn., 1685. She was born in October, 1665. After his death, she was married to William Randall, of Enfield, the under the jurisdiction of Massachusetts, now [1856] under that of Connecticut, about 172
2 CONC 0.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. John Clark died September, 1715, in the 60th year of his age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Mary Clark, alias Randall, died ____.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT 175 Hannah, b Aug 6 1686, Simsbury. M. Thomas Gillett Feb 26, 1705
2 CONT 176 John, b --, Simsbury, d Mar 7 1709
2 CONT 177 Mary, b --, Simsbury. M. Samuel Cooley, of Springfield, Mass., Oct 24 1711
2 CONT 178 Jemima, b --, Simsbury. M. a Cooley.
2 CONT 179 Martha, b Mar 19 1697, Windsor. M. James Eggleston, of Windsor, Aug 28 1718. She d May 25, 1728
2 CONT 180 Solomon, b May 20 1699, Windsor. M. Ann Eggleston, of Windsor, Feb 24 1720.
2 CONT 181 Elizabeth b May 16 1701, Windsor.
2 CONT 182 Sarah, b Oct 28, 1702, Windsor. Died young.
2 CONT 183 Daniel, b Dec 31, 1704, Windsor
2 CONT 184 Ann, b Jan 12 1707, Windsor. d Aug 13 1716
2 CONT 185 Benoni, b Oct 21, 1708, Windsor. d at Enfield, Jan 16 ____.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Goodwin, Nathaniel. Genealogical Notes, or Contributions to the Family History of Some of the First Settlers of Connecticut and Massachusetts. Hartford, CT. 1856. Page 36, 37.
2 CONT  ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN CLARKE — John Clarke appears on the Lebanon records only as a recipient of a full allotment at Lebanon Village.  When receiving the first installment of his lands in January 1701/2, he is called of Northampton. This all sugge
2 CONC sts a connection with William Clarke, who had both a brother and a nephew of that name. The former was more likely the grantee. He did not come to Lebanon. 
2 CONT (LD 1:168, 322; Warner 128-31)
