2 CONT Militia Service: He served as a private in the 18th Regiment of the Connecticut Militia, an armed, combat-ready unit responsible for state defense. Members of the militia trained and were called up for active duty when needed for 
2 CONC battles or defense of the state's coastline and interior.
2 CONT
2 CONT Higley was a productive farmer and landowner in what was known as "Higley Town" (an area now mostly in East Granby and Canton). He was likely involved in the various local industries that supported the thriving community. Other me
2 CONC mbers of his extended family were highly influential, involved in everything from mercantile shipping (tar, pitch, and rum) to operating copper mines and even minting some of the first Connecticut coppers. John Jr. held a respecte
2 CONC d position as a good citizen and a worthy son in a prominent family.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/61993065/john-higley
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT T'''he Messenger family were early and prominent colonial settlers in and around Simsbury and Bloomfield, C'''onnecticut, but historical records suggest they were primarily Puritans/Congregationalists, not Quakers. 
2 CONT Edward Messenger (c. 1617-1688) was the first of the family in the colony, settling in the area that became Bloomfield (originally part of Windsor). A genealogy mentions one specific Andrew Messenger (a different individual from t
2 CONC he Simsbury line's first Edward) was an "informer" against Quakers in Greenwich in 1661, indicating an adversarial relationship with the Quaker faith common among the dominant Puritan population at the time. 
2 CONT Key information about the Messenger family in Simsbury:
2 CONT Settlement: Edward Messenger built the first house in what became "Messenger Farms" (now part of Bloomfield) around 1661. The area was six miles from the main church in Windsor, highlighting the frontier nature of their settlement
2 CONC .
2 CONT Prominence: The family was substantial and well-established, with multiple generations of detailed genealogical records in local histories, such as The Messenger family in the colony of Connecticut.
2 CONT '''Military Service:''' 
2 CONT Many male family members were noted for their service in colonial conflicts, including the Revolutionary War, with ten sons of Isaac Messenger all serving.
2 CONT '''Religious Affiliation:'''
2 CONT  The Connecticut Colony had an established church (Congregationalist, or Puritan). While other religions eventually made inroads, the Messenger family's extensive involvement in the community and military suggests adherence to the
2 CONC  mainstream, dominant faith of the time rather than the persecuted Quaker faith. 
2 CONT For further genealogical information, you may consult resources like the Simsbury Historical Society.
2 CONT
