2 CONT Were there exception like Lodama Messenger Higley?
2 CONT Lodama Messenger, who married John Higley III, was part of the mainstream Simsbury Messenger family line that was predominantly Congregationalist (Puritan), not Quaker. There is no historical evidence suggesting she or her immedia
2 CONC te family were Quakers. 
2 CONT The primary religious history of the Messenger family in colonial Connecticut indicates a strong adherence to the established Puritan church, which was the dominant and official religion of the colony:
2 CONT The first Edward Messenger was a key early settler in the Bloomfield area, which was tied to the Windsor church.
2 CONT One early family member, Andrew Messenger, was even recorded as an "informer" against Quakers in Greenwich in 1661, highlighting the local hostility toward the Quaker faith.
2 CONT Lodama's father, Simeon Messenger Sr., was a lifelong resident of Simsbury/Canton, an area without a known prominent Quaker community at that time. 
2 CONT While there may have been individuals who later converted in subsequent generations, the early colonial Messenger family in Simsbury did not embrace Quakerism. Lodama Messenger Higley was part of the prevailing religious and socia
2 CONC l fabric of Puritan New England.
2 CONT
2 CONT
