2 CONT What religion were Higley family of Simsbury
2 CONT The Higley family of Simsbury were predominantly Puritans/Congregationalists, which was the established church of the Connecticut Colony during the colonial era. 
2 CONT Key information about the Higley family's religious affiliations:
2 CONT Puritan Beliefs: Early family members like Seth Higley (1746-1794) were described as "intensely Puritan in his religious beliefs, and practices, clinging Ridly throughout his life to the law of the father's faith".
2 CONT Active Church Members: They were active in the local church, with records showing individuals like Seth Higley chosen as a "Collector a said Society's rates" for the Simsbury church, and Brewster Higley IV serving as a deacon in t
2 CONC he Castleton Congregational Church after migrating from Simsbury.
2 CONT Community Leaders: Captain John Higley (the first American ancestor) held numerous high offices in the community, including justice of the peace and judge of the county court, which indicates a close alignment with the social and 
2 CONC religious mainstream of the Puritan colony.
2 CONT Later Diversification: While the core colonial family was Congregationalist, some descendants in later generations and different geographical areas did diverge:
2 CONT Shakers: Solomon Higley and his wife Lydia were among those who embraced the Shaker faith in New Lebanon, New York, previous to 1780.
2 CONT Latter-day Saints (Mormons): Some Higleys who moved west to Ohio, Missouri, Illinois, and Utah in the 1800s became members of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints.
2 CONT Free Will Baptist: Sophia Higley married a Free Will Baptist preacher and settled in Ohio. 
2 CONT However, the colonial-era Higley family residing in Simsbury itself adhered to the prevailing Congregationalist faith.
2 CONT
