1 TITL Col.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Captain George Humphrey''' fought in the Revolutionary War.  He answered the'' Lexington Alarm'' on April 19, 1775*, when he was 19 years old.  The Battles of Lexington and Concord were the first military engage
2 CONC ments of the American Revolutionary War.  They were fought on April 19, 1775, in Middlesex County, Province of Massachusetts Bay, within the towns of Lexington, Concord, Lincoln, Menotomy (present-day Arlington), and Cambridge, ne
2 CONC ar Boston. The battles marked the outbreak of open armed conflict between the Kingdom of Great Britain and its thirteen colonies in the mainland of British North America.
2 CONT
2 CONT The inscription on his grave marker reads:
2 CONT >Revolutionary War
2 CONT
2 CONT >George Humphrey
2 CONT
2 CONT >Lexington Alarm
2 CONT
2 CONT >Died July 16, 1813
2 CONT
2 CONT >AE. 57
2 CONT
2 CONT At *the beginning of the Revolutionary War, George Humphrey served as a fifer.  A fifer was a non-combatant military occupation of a foot soldier who originally played the fife during combat. The practice was instituted during the
2 CONC  period of Early Modern warfare to sound signals during changes in formation, such as the line, and were also members of the regiment's military band during marches.  These soldiers, often boys too young to fight, were used to hel
2 CONC p infantry battalions to keep marching pace from the right of the formation in coordination with the drummers positioned at the center, and relayed orders in the form of sequences of musical signals. The fife was particularly usef
2 CONC ul because of its high pitched sound, which could be heard over the sounds of battle.
