1 NAME Serajah /Stratton/
2 GIVN Serajah
2 SURN Stratton
2 _MARNM Stratton
2 NICK Jeremiah
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 MAR 1705
2 ADDR
3 CITY Windsor
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 JUL 1758
2 ADDR
3 CITY Simsbury
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Serajah_Stratton_(3)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 May 1758, Serajah enlisted for service in the French and Indian War. In July of the same year he died in the service of the country as his Father did 49 years before. He was clerk of the company organized by Capt. Holcomb und
2 CONC er Col. Phineas Lyman.
2 CONT
2 CONT --Source of information: "A Book of STRATTONS" (Vol 1, page 231);
2 CONT
2 CONT Records of him are found both in Winsor and in Simsbury,--the adjoining town on the west.  He probably lived very near the line between the two towns.  12 December 1728, he married Eunice CASE, daughter of Elizabeth and Samuel Cas
2 CONC e.  She was born in Simsbury, 8 July 1704.  The Case family lived in that part of the town which later became Broomfield.  A deed is found showing that Serajah Stratton and Eunice, his wife, sold land in Simsbury in 1735.  This wa
2 CONC s not the land upon which their house was situated.  1 May 1758, Serajah enlisted for service in the French and Indian War.  He was clerk of the company organized by Captain Nathaniel Holcomb, under Colonel Pheneas Lyman.  On 30 J
2 CONC uly of the same year Serajah died in the service of his country, --as did his father forty-nine years before.
2 CONT
2 CONT Administration on his estate was granted his widow Eunice.  The inventory of estate includes, besides the farm and house, livestock, farming implements and carpenter's tools, household furniture and clothing; also, a "Beak-horn" a
2 CONC nd books,--including "an old family Bible" (would we could find it to-day!) and a book called Pleasant Companion.
2 CONT
2 CONT --Source of information: Debi <dlelliott@@west.raytheon.com>;
2 CONT
2 CONT I just looked at First Settlers of CT & MA and, for what it's worth, Eunice's  husband is listed there as Jeremiah.  Other than the marriage date (and the CASE line) which matches what you already have, there is no further  inform
2 CONC ation on them.
2 CONT
2 CONT The information on the Stratton line was received from Michael J. Godshalk.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Research notes
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Stratton-300
2 CONT
2 CONT STRATTON AUGER CASE MOORE[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Seraiah Stratton b.7 Jul 1740 poss. Hartford Co., CT d. after 1793 in Pendleton Co.,WV. Need his 1st wifes name. He is said to m/2 Hannah Auger. He was also listed in Greenbried Co.,WV where he was said to be Capt. in the Militia.
2 CONC  He was s/o Seraiah Stratton and Eunice Case. This Seraiah was s/o William Stratton and Abigail Moore all of Windsor, Hartford Co.,CT. Appreciate any help on this family. ~ Bette Butcher Topp" (address redacted)
2 CONT
2 CONT "He was one of the unfortunate commands that set out to invade Canada in May 1709, during the old French War. The Conn., troops got as far as Wood Creek, near Albany, where they lay until fall, suffering great mortality. The town 
2 CONC records of Windsorhave the following entry: "October, 1709, William Stratton died aboard the vessel. coming from Albany." [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
