2 CONT William Spencer, son of Gerard and Alice (Whitbread) Spencer was baptized 11 Oct 1601, at Stotfold, Bedfordshire, England.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He immigrated to New England about 1631, settled in Cambridge, Massachusetts, then removed to Hartford, Connecticut in 1639.[2] His name can be found on the Hartford Founders Monument. He was brother to three other immigrants Thom
2 CONC as Spencer of Cambridge and Hartford, Michael Spencer and Gerard Spencer of Lynn.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT William Spencer married Agnes Harris, daughter of Bartholomew Harris and Elizabeth (Collamore) Harris.[3] After the death of William she married William Edwards.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT William became a freeman of the Massachusetts Bay Colony 4 March 1632/3.[4] This gave him the right to vote and to hold office. He was the Deputy from Cambridge to the General Court eight times between 9 May 1632 and 12 March 1637
2 CONC /8. In April 1637 he was listed as Lieut. Spencer.[5] In addition to serving as Lt. of the Cambridge trainband, William was a Charter member of the Ancient and honorable Artillery Company.[6]Governments can't run without committee
2 CONC s. William served on several Colony committees[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the town clerk at Cambridge 1632-1635, and town selectman 23 November 1635. A committee was appointed to "survey the town lands and enter [mutilated] a book appointed for that purpose" 3 Feb 1634/5, and on 27 Oct 1636 "Newe
2 CONC  Towne presented a book of their records under the hands of Will Andrews, constable, John Beniamin, & Will Spencer." He and George Steele measured the undivided lands and alloted portions in 1635.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Town Records of Cambridge" and the Cambridge Land Inventory show that William owned land and a house, garden, etc. in the years between 1633 and 1638[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Click caption to see larger In Hartford, William had a house, outhouses, yards and gardens, and about 98 acres in various parcels. He had lots 48 and 88 on the map.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT William continued public service after his move to Hartford. He was Deputy for Hartford to the Connecticut General Court five sessions in a row starting 11 April 1639 and ending shortly before his death, 9 April 1640. In addition,
2 CONC  he was the surveyor of armor and other military provisions for Hartford in 1639.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT "A noate of the mynd and Will of Williã Spenser for pr the 4th of May, 1640" Basically the will states that his wife Agnes should get one third, his son Samuel on third, and his daughters Sarah and Elizabeth one third. It refers t
2 CONC o some property that his wife might receive and names overseers "Cosen Mathew Allen, my brother John Pratt and John Taylcoate. These last two items were keys to the identity of Agnes as Agnes Harris. His will and inventory (£67 mo
2 CONC veables plus real estate, debts owed and debts due) are found in the Colony Records.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth b. c. 1633; m. (1) William Wellman, who died at Killingworth 9 Aug 1674; eight children; m. (2) May 23, 1672, Jacob Joy, four children.[1] Sarah b. c. 1636; d. Nov. 3, 1691, Simsbury, Connecticut; m. before 17 Aug 1656, 
2 CONC John Case, who died 21 Feb 1703/4; ten children. He m. (2) Elizabeth (Moore) Loomis.[1] Samuel, b. about 1639; d. about 1716.[1] m. Sarah prob. dau. of John Meakins. (John M. names dau. Sarah Spencer in his will, Nov. 22, 1702.[1]
