2 DATE ABT 1792
2 ADDR
3 CITY Sheffield
3 STAE Massachusetts
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Amy Case''', daughter of Richard Case and Amy Reade, was born 1703 at Simsbury, Connecticut Colony, and died about 1792 in Sheffield, Berkshire, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married first on 4 December 1729 to Jonathan Holcomb (1701-1737).
2 CONT
2 CONT She married second about 1741 to Joshua Boardman (1702- abt 1761).
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Amy Case and Jonathan Holcomb:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Jonathan Holcomb 1731 -
2 CONT # Michael Holcomb 1733 -
2 CONT # Pliny Holcomb 1735 -
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Amy Case and Joshua Boardman:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Rosanna Boardman Abt 1742 - 1761
2 CONT # Elijah Boardman Est 1744 - 1771
2 CONT # Mary Boardman Est 1747 -
2 CONT # Hannah Boardman Est 1749 -
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Goldthwaite, Charlotte. Boardman Genealogy, 1525-1895: The English Home and Ancestry of Samuel Boreman, Wethersfield, Conn.; Thomas Boreman, Ipswich, Mass. : With Some Account of Their Descendants (Now Called Boardman) in America.
2 CONC  (Hartford, Conn.: William F. J. Boardman, 1895) pages 235, 237; page 239; page 240.
2 CONT
2 CONT > 'Joshua Bordman ([son of] Daniel...), of Sheffield, Mass., born in Wethersfield Nov. 18, 1702; married Feb. 14, 1724-5, Sarah Stockwell. ... [The marriage and birth of their first child Elizabeth in 1725 are] all that is known o
2 CONC f Joshua's family, except as it appears in his will, for the records of Sheffield, both church and town, are almost entirely wanting for that period. Sarah Stockwell is supposed to have been the mother of either four or five of th
2 CONC e eight children of whom mention is found, while another wife, Amy, was mother of the remainder.' <BR>
2 CONT
2 CONT >'The name of the widow Amy is discovered on evidence found in the preceding deeds. She was Amy, born 1703, daughter of Capt. Richard Case of Simsbury (North Parish, now Granby), and Amy his wife, who was daughter of Dr. Philip Re
2 CONC ed of Concord, Mass. She married first, Dec. 4, 1729, Jonathan Holcomb, Jr., of Simsbury, born Nov. 25, 1701, ... Jonathan died June 30, 1737, and Amy afterwards married Joshua Bordman, ...' <BR>
2 CONT > ‘The widow Amy survived her husband more than thirty years, dying about 1792, when she must have been 89 years old. Administration on her estate was granted May 1, 1792, to Benjamin Cowles, Jr., Elisha Smith (her son-in-law), an
2 CONC d Azariah Root. Her sickness seems to have been long, for there are three doctor’s bills included in her administration account, the first of which begins June 25, 1790, and contains a long list of drugs amounting to 14s. “Amey Bo
2 CONC rdman’s ” signature in the Probate Records at Pittsfield is in a remark- ably beautiful and delicate hand, which would do honor to any lady of the present day.‘
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
