1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR# [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A044585 A044585]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Gibbs-898
2 CONT
2 CONT * Gershom Gibbs
2 CONT * Born July 28, 1721 in Litchfield, Litchfield, Connecticutmap
2 CONT * Son of Benjamin Gibbs and Abigail Marshall
2 CONT * Brother of Benjamin Gibbs, Zebulon Gibbs, Henry Gibbs, Abigail Gibbs, Hannah Gibbs, David Gibbs, William Gibbs, Zadock Gibbs, Elizabeth Gibbs, Sarah Gibbs, Caleb Gibbs, Justice Gibbs and Remembrance Gibbs	
2 CONT * Husband of Tabitha Moore — married 1747 in Litchfield, Litchfield, Connecticutmap
2 CONT * Father of Lovell Gibbs	
2 CONT * Died December 19, 1776 in New York, New York, New York
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Gibbs, Gershom (born in Litchfield, Litchfield County, CT, on July 28, 1721; died in Wallabout Bay, New York Harbor, New York City, NY, on December 29, 1776), son of Benjamin Gibbs and Abigail Marshall, the granddaughter of Samuel
2 CONC  Marshall who was killed in 1637 in the Great Swamp Fight (at Sasqua Swamp in Southport, Connecticut) that ended the Pequot War.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Gershom Gibbs was the first white male born in Litchfield, Connecticut.  He married Tabitha Moore (1731-1815) and they had six known children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Gershom Gibbs, Jr. (b. July 18, 1750)
2 CONT * Ruth Gibbs (b. May 25, 1752)
2 CONT * Moore Gibbs (b. January 12, 1757)
2 CONT * Isaac Gibbs (b. May 18, 1759 - January 17, 1777) 
2 CONT * Oliver Gibbs (b. May 23, 1762)
2 CONT * Lovel Gibbs (b. August 23, 1765)
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Revolutionary War, he was a member of Captain Beebe's Company.  He was one of the 36 men chosen from Litchfield for the defense of Fort Washington.  Of the 36, only 6 returned.  
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 55, he died of starvation and disease  as a prisoner of war on the Grosvenor prison ship in Wallabout Bay, New York Harbor, New York City, NY.  He is buried at Milton Burying Ground, Litchfield, Connecticut. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:  Gershom and Tabitha's son Isaac Gibbs also served in the Continental Army during the Revolutionary War. He was taken prisoner and also died of starvation and disease on January 17, 1777 at age 17 on the Grosvenor Prison Shi
2 CONC p, one of at least 16 of these floating prisons anchored in Wallabout Bay on the East River in New York City for most of the war. Contributed by Joann Geybels (Find a Grave #46986938)
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/42069263/gershom-gibbs Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jul 3 2021, 2:32:50 UTC''
2 CONT ----
