1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Brewster Higley IV, a Patriot of the American Revolution for CONNECTICUT - VERMONT with the rank of PRIVATE and founder of Rutland, Ohio.  [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A
2 CONC 055446  DAR Ancestor # A055446]
2 CONT
2 CONT from: http://www.remarkableohio.org/HistoricalMarker.aspx?historicalMarkerId=491
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the first settler of Rutland Township in April 1799.
2 CONT
2 CONT Veteran of the Revolutionary War. Served as private in the Connecticut and Vermont troops. Fought in the battle of White Plains (October 27, 1776) under Colonel Ethan Allen. He was in the Vermont Military in the winter of 1778. He
2 CONC  was in one of the boats that crossed the Delaware on Christmas night with General George Washington. He received a pension.
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT Brewster Higley was the first settler in Rutland Township, Meigs County, Ohio. He emigrated to Ohio in 1799 from Simsbury, Connecticut. He was a Revolutionary soldier. He served as a Justice in Vermont. Governor Arthur St. Clair o
2 CONC f Ohio appointed him a justice for Washington County (of which Meigs was yet a part) on Dec. 28, 1791. He was one of the first Associate Judges of Gallia County (of which Meigs was then a part) and in 1815 was made the second post
2 CONC master of Rutland, Ohio. He died June 20, 1847, in his 89th year. Brewster Higley was a learned man and was one of the first trustees of Athens Ohio College.
