1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Please see George Byron Merrick: Genealogy of the Merrick-Mirick-Myrick Family of Massachusetts, 1636-1902; page 266; https://books.google.com/books?id=2rU0AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA266&dq=Merrick+Roxbury&hl=en&ei=Z7iwTp-FHuHv
2 CONC 0gGbv6XVAQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CC0Q6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=Merrick%20Roxbury&f=false. (Steven Ferry, January 20,2019.)
2 CONT
2 CONT =Thomas Merrick=
2 CONT Thomas Merrick was born circa 1620 at probably Wales. He married Elizabeth Tilley on 21 October 1653. Thomas Merrick died on 7 September 1704.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Merrick, b. in 1620, is said to have come from Wales. He married Sarah Stebbins, daughter of Rowland and Sarah, September 14, 1639. After the death of his wife, date not stated, he married Elizabeth Tilley, October 21, 1653
2 CONC . He died September 7, 1704. His children by his first wife were:—
2 CONT
2 CONT # Thomas, b. April 12, 1641, d. young. 
2 CONT # Sarah, b. May 9, 1643, m. Eliakim Hitchcock. 
2 CONT # Mary, b. September 28, 1645, 1. July 28, 1646. 
2 CONT # Mary, -b. August 1647, m. Samuel Foot. 
2 CONT # Hannah, b. February 10, 1649, m. Benjamin Knowlton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children by Second Marriage.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Presumably by second wife:
2 CONT # Elizabeth, b. October 26, 1654, d. January u, 1659. 
2 CONT # Merriam, b. May I, 1656, d. October 4, 1684. 
2 CONT # John, b. November 9, 1658, m. Mary Day.  
2 CONT # Elizabeth, b. July 4, 1661, m. Thomas Day.  
2 CONT # Thomas, b. January 2, 1663, m. Hannah Dumbleton.  
2 CONT # Tilly, b. October 20, 1667, m. Sarah Cooley.  
2 CONT # James, b. March 2, 1670, m. Sarah Hitchcock.  
2 CONT # Abigail, b. September 7, 1673, m. Gershom Ferry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Stockbridge, Past and Present, Or, Records of an Old Mission Station: Past and Present; Or, Records of Old Mission Station" By Electa Fidelia Jones Published by S. Bowles & Co., 1854:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Merrick emigrated from Wales to Roxbury, and thence to Springfield with Mr. Pynchon hi 1636.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The descendants of John Porter of Windsor, Conn. 1635-9" By Henry Porter Andrews Published by G.W. Ball, printer, 1893
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Merrick came from England to Roxbury. Mass., in 1636; removed to Hartford, Conn., in 1638 ; thence to Springfield. Mass. He married (second wife) 1653, Elizabeth Tilley, daughter of William Tilley, from England in the ship 
2 CONC "Abigail," in 1635, with his wife Alice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second Generation, Elizabeth Merrick, born , 1661, married Thomas Day. Thomas Day removed to Colchester, Conn., about 1705, where he died.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thomas Merrick of Springfield by tradition, is said to have come from Wales through Roxbury, and reach Springfiled in 1636; but there is evidence that he was of Hartford early in 1638. He was very young, if he left, as is said, hi
2 CONC s native land in 1630; and no trace is seen at Roxbury of him, pr his family, nor can the name be found there before 1649. At Springfield he married July 14, 1639, Sarah, daughter of Rowland Stebbins which was the third marriage i
2 CONC n that town, of any English "GD FSNE Freeman 1665."
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Moved from Wales to Roxbury, MA in 1630 and moved to Springfield in 1636.  Merrick is known in England as Mirick.
2 CONT
2 CONT The North American branch of the Merrick family came from Bodorgan on the Isle of Anglesey. They were descendants of Llewellyn Merrick who had been the Captain of the Guard during the Coronation of Henry VIII, and later, the first
2 CONC  High Sheriff of Anglesey. In the spring of 1636, four Merrick brothers arrived in Charlestown, Massachusetts, from Bristol on the sailing ship “James”. They ranged in age from thirty-three to sixteen, and the Merrickville branch 
2 CONC sprang from the sixteen year old.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Merrick was the ancestor who started it all. He was a restless pioneer who was destined to make a significant contribution to the development of Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1638, when he was eighteen and married, he and his wife, Elizabeth Tilley, and seven other families founded a settlement in the wilderness that became the City of Springfield. Their grandson, Ebenezer, moved to New York State w
2 CONC here he obtained land at Sarasota and Ballston, including land at Sarasota Springs known as the Deer’s Lick. George Washington and Alexander Hamilton visited this region in 1784, and the records show that they had ridden through a
2 CONC n area known in earlier times as Merricks Mills. The name had been changed after the revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Merrick, the 28 year old grandson of Ebenezer, had remained loyal to the Crown during the American Revolution, and after the war he joined thousands of other loyalists at Sorel, just outside of Montreal. They were gathered
2 CONC  there for dispersal and settlement in the wilds of Upper Canada.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Good info site: http://www.tributaries.us/secondsite/founders2-o/p4389.htm#i43885
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT -------------------- T. M. came from Wales to Roxbury, MA, May 1630 and to Springfield in 1636.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From Below:  Thomas Merrick aka Mirick, Meyrick, Mirricke Born 1620 in St. Davids, Pembrokeshire, Dyfed, Walesmap ANCESTORS ancestors Son of John Meyrick '''[uncertain'''] and Dorothy (Bishop) Meyrick ['''uncertain''']  
2 CONT =(by LJC - I will stop at this point with uncertains)=
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Janet (Meyrick) Merrick, William Merrick, John Merrick, James Merrick, John Merrick and	Sarah (Mirick) Merrick
2 CONT Husband of Sarah (Stebbins) Merrick — married 14 Sep 1639 (to about 1650) in Springfield, Hampden, Massachusettsmap
2 CONT Husband of Elizabeth (Tilley) Merrick — married 21 Oct 1653 in Springfield, Hampden, Massachusettsmap
2 CONT
