0 @N6000000002046092577@ NOTE Robert and John purchased the larger portion of the town of Enfield in March 1681.  They are said to have come to Enfield in 1679 two years before bringing their families and lived the first winter in a dugout in a hillside near w
1 CONC here the first church was later built.
0 @N6000000006348338795@ NOTE (III) Deacon Isaac Pease, son of John Pease, was born July 15, 1672, at Salem, Massachusetts, died July 9, 1731. His gravestone is standing in the burying ground near the central meeting house at Enfield. He married, in 1691, Mind
1 CONC well Osborn, and settled in the northeastern part of Enfield, then in Massachusetts, not far from the Shaker village. He owned much land and was a leading citizen. He was deacon of the Congregational church. Children, born at Enfi
1 CONC eld: Isaac, born May 2, 1693; Abraham, 1695; Mindwell, 1697; Abigail, 1699, died August 28, 1723; Israel, mentioned below; Ann, February, 1705; Benjamin, 1707; Ezekiel, June 20, 1710; Timothy, 1713; Cummings. November 15, 1715.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cutter, William Richard. New England Families Genealogical and Memorial: Third Series, Volume I. 1915. Reprint, Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc., 1996.
0 @N6000000003122799224@ NOTE First Eggleston ancestor in America.  In 1635, left Dorchester, Mass., with members of Rev. John Warham's church to settle Windsor, Connecticut.
0 @N6000000001503924947@ NOTE BIGOD EGGLESTON
1 CONT
1 CONT ORIGIN: Unknown
1 CONT MIGRATION: 1630
1 CONT FIRST RESIDENCE: Dorchester
1 CONT REMOVES: Windsor 1635
1 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP: Bigod Eggleston is in Matthew Grant's list of those who had been members of the church in Dorchester, moved to Windsor with Warham, and remained in the church at Windsor [Grant 9].
1 CONT FREEMAN: Requested 19 October 1630 (as "Bigatt Egglestone") and admitted 18 May 1631 (as "Biggott Egglestone") [MBCR 1:80, 366]. Connecticut freeman, 21 May 1657 [CCCR 1:297].
1 CONT EDUCATION: Signed his will "Beget Egleston."
1 CONT OFFICES: Hartford jury, 8 July 1650 [RPCC 86].
1 CONT ESTATE: Granted "a lot on this side of the way going to Rocksbury," in Dorchester, 5 August 1633 [DTR 2]; "Thomas Geofry to have the lot [which] was Mr. Egelstone's by Mr. Hathorne," 2 June 1634 [DTR 6]; granted three acres "in th
1 CONC e neck behind his lot," 10 February 1634/5 [DTR 10]; granted Lot #52, four acres in the meadow beyond Naponset [DTR 321].
1 CONT    In the Windsor land inventory on 8 December 1640 "Beggatt Eggleston" held five parcels of land which had been granted to him: "an homelot with the addition eleven acres"; eleven acres of meadow; "over the Great River 25 rod in 
1 CONC breadth, length 3 mile"; "near Paquannick 20 acres"; "in the palisado three-quarters of an acre" [WiLR 1:23r].
1 CONT    He purchased other pieces of real estate (dates of the transactions unknown): of Rhoda Taylor, "her dwelling house, outhouses, garden with the land upon which it stands in the palisado a quarter of an acre ..., also in the Grea
1 CONC t Meadow six acres & fifteen rods ..., also across the Great River in breadth ten rods ... in length three miles ... [annotated "sold to Rich: Saxton], also adjoining to Rocky Hill eighteen acres ... [annotated "sold to Wm. Gayler
1 CONC  the elder"]; of land that was Eltweed Pomeroy's, one acre and a half in the palisado with the house on it; of George Hull "in the Great Meadow three acres & half" [annotated "sold to Hen: Clark"]; of William Gaylard the elder twe
1 CONC nty acres of land; and of William Hill "in the Great Meadow twelve acres" [WiLR 23r-22v].
1 CONT    On 4 March 1641 "Beggatt Eggleston of Windsor" sold to Thomas Thornton of the same twenty acres "on the south side of the rivulet near Paquannicke" [WiLR 1:59]. On 8 November 1660 "Thomas Egglston of Windsor" sold to "my father
1 CONC  Beggat Egglestone" six acres of land [WiLR 23v]. On [blank] March 1660 Richard Weller of Farmington sold to "Begat Eaggleston" two acres and a half of marsh [WiLR 23v].
1 CONT    In his will, dated 13 November 1673, "Bigat Egllstone of Windsor" bequeathed to "my son Benjamin" house and land (on the condition "that he shall maintain his mother and pay my debts"), to "my son Joseph" 40s. "should [he] come
1 CONC  and demand a portion," to sons James, Samuel and Thomas and daughters Mary, Sarah and Abigail, 3s. apiece [Hartford PD Case #1890; Manwaring 1:194].
1 CONT    His inventory, taken 24 October 1674, totalled £116 3s. (against which were debts of £39 8s. 5d.), of which £74 was real estate: "his dwelling house & houseland 2 acres & half," £40; "in the Great Meadow six acres," £33; and "f
1 CONC or poor land on the Sandy Plains 20 acres" £1 [Hartford PD Case #1890; Manwaring 1:194].
1 CONT BIRTH: Baptized Settrington, Yorkshire, 20 February 1586[/7], son of "James Egeleston" [TAG 10:198]. (Rosalie Eggleston and Linda Eggleston McBroom have identified the mother of Bigod Eggleston as Margaret, daughter of Miles Harke
1 CONC r of Settrington [TAG 69:193-201].)
1 CONT DEATH: Windsor 1 September 1674 "near 100 year old" (but actually eighty-seven) [Grant 35; also CTVR 28].
1 CONT MARRIAGE: (1) By 1612 _____ _____; not seen in any record.
1 CONT    (2) By about 1634 _____ _____; on 5 June 1645 "Baggett Egleston, for bequeathing his wife to a young man, is fined 20s." [CCCR 1:127; RPCC 34]; living on 13 November 1673 when named in her husband's will.
1 CONT CHILDREN:
1 CONT    With first wife
1 CONT
1 CONT    i   JAMES, bp. St. Margaret's, Norwich, Norfolk, 14 April 1612; bur. there 20 August 1613.
1 CONT
1 CONT    ii   MARY, bp. St. Margaret's, Norwich, Norfolk, 19 January 1613/4; no further record (but see COMMENTS below).
1 CONT
1 CONT    iii   JAMES, b. say 1620 ; m. by 1656 Esther Kelsey, daughter of WILLIAM KELSEY (eldest child b. Windsor 1 January 1656[/7?] [Grant 36; Manwaring 1:300]) [TAG 68:208-15]. After the death of her first husband Esther married Jame
1 CONC s Eno and then John Williams, so that (as George McCracken put it) "she was surnamed Williams at her death, not, as so often claimed, at her birth" [TAG 35:21].
1 CONT
1 CONT    With second wife
1 CONT    iv   SAMUEL, b. say 1634; m. by 1663 Sarah Disborough, daughter of Nicholas Disborough (eldest child b. Middletown 6 March 1663; the inventory of "Nicholas Disbrowe," dated 31 August 1683, carried the names of his four children
1 CONC , including "Samuel Eglestone's wife" [Manwaring 1:298; TAG 38:209-10]).
1 CONT
1 CONT    v   MARY, b. say 1636; m. Windsor 7 June 1655 John Denslow [Grant 33]. (He was not son of NICHOLAS DENSLOW.)
1 CONT
1 CONT    vi   THOMAS, b. Windsor 26 August 1638 [Grant 35]; no evidence that he married. Thomas Eggleston died in May 1697, and on 2 September 1697 the court ordered distribution to "Benjamin Eglestone, brother, and to the wife of Jno. 
1 CONC Pettebone senior of Simsbury, a sister of the deceased"; on 28 August 1701 distribution was made "to Benjamin Eglestone, to John Pettebone, to John, to Thomas, to Nathaniel, to Isaac Eglestone" [Manwaring 1:553-54].
1 CONT
1 CONT    vii   MERCY, b. Windsor 29 May 1641 [Grant 35]; d. there 1657 [Grant 88; see also TAG 37:152 and NGSQ 57:255].
1 CONT
1 CONT    viii   SARAH, b. Windsor 28 March 1643 [Grant 35]; m. 16 February 1664 John Pettibone [Grant 62].
1 CONT
1 CONT    ix   REBECCA, b. Windsor 8 December 1644 [Grant 35]; not named in father's will; no further record.
1 CONT
1 CONT    x   ABIGAIL, b. Windsor 12 June 1648 [Grant 35]; bp. there 18 June 1648; m. 14 October 1669 John Osborn [Grant 54].
1 CONT
1 CONT    xi   JOSEPH, bp. Windsor 30 March 1651 [Grant 35]; in his will of 13 December 1673 his father bequeathed to Joseph 40s. "should [he] come and demand a portion," indicating that he lived at a distance. Stiles claims that Joseph 
1 CONC married, resided in the area of North Stonington, and had three sons and "several daughters" [Windsor Hist 199, 201]. One of these supposed sons had children born in the 1750s and later and another had children born in the 1770s a
1 CONC nd later; these are more likely descendants of a much later generation who have not been properly located among the known branches of the family, and should not be credited to Joseph, for whom no record has been found beyond his f
1 CONC ather's conditional bequest.
1 CONT
1 CONT    xii   BENJAMIN, b. Windsor 18 December 1653 [Grant 35]; m. Windsor 6 March 1678 Hannah Shaddock, daughter of John Osborn and widow of Elias Shaddock ("Elias Shadduck" died at Windsor 26 May 1676 leaving a wife and one child, an
1 CONC d John Osborn left a will dated 27 October 1686 naming daughter "Hanna" [surname not given] and "my grandchild Hana Shadduck" [Manwaring 1:232, 345]).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT COMMENTS: Bigod Eggleston's migration pathway is one of the more interesting encountered among those who came to New England. He was born in Settrington, Yorkshire, in 1586, and then about 1610 accompanied or followed other member
1 CONC s of his family to Norwich, Norfolk. The last record for him at Norwich is the baptism of his daughter Mary on 19 January 1613/4.
1 CONT    He then showed up in 1630 at Dorchester, which has led many to place him on the Mary & John. If he were on this ship, one would expect him to have come from one of the West Country counties of Devon, Dorset or Somerset, but we 
1 CONC have no record for him anywhere from 1615 to 1630.
1 CONT    Was the Yorkshire and Norfolk man the same as the Bigod Eggleston of Dorchester and Windsor? There is at this time no certain evidence, but the age of the New England man is in accord with the baptismal date in Settrington; the
1 CONC  Yorkshire-Norwich man had father and son named James while the Windsor man had son James; and no other man by the name of Bigod Eggleston has been found in England.
1 CONT    A search for records for this man in Devon, Dorset and Somerset may resolve this problem, and tie together the beginning and end of the career of Bigod Eggleston.
1 CONT    In his record of "what children has been born in Windsor from our beginning hither to so far as I am able to find out," Matthew Grant credited "Begat Egelston" with seven [Grant 90].
1 CONT    The claim has been made that the "Mary Egellston" who married at Watertown on 16 October 1645 Edward Sanderson was the daughter of Bigod Eggleston baptized in Norwich in 1614 [WaVR 12], but this is unlikely for many reasons: th
1 CONC e Mary baptized at Norwich would have been thirty at the time of this marriage; Bigod did not name her or any children of hers in his will; and the Egglestons of Windsor had no known connection with Watertown at this early date.
1 CONT    Most accounts of the family state that James and Samuel were twins, but there is no evidence in favor of this, and few dates to help us in sorting out the chronology for these two men. However, James Eggleston has an entry date
1 CONC d 8 December 1640 in the Windsor land inventory whereas Samuel does not; and the two men married about seven years apart.
1 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHIC NOTE: Clarence Almon Torrey published the basic information on the Eggleston family in Yorkshire in 1934 [TAG 10:197-98]. This was supplemented by John G. Hunt, who pointed out the correct reading of the records for 
1 CONC the daughter born in 1641, thus requiring that the daughter Mary named in the will must have been born earlier [TAG 37:151-53]. Genevieve Tylee Kiepura added some further comments in 1969 [NGSQ 57:255-61], expanding considerably o
1 CONC n the clue provided by Torrey regarding the Egglestons in Norwich.
1 CONT    The most complete and extensive treatment of Bigod Eggleston was published in 1991 by Rosalie Eggleston and Linda Eggleston McBroom [Bygod Eggleston: Englishman & Colonist and Some of His Descendants (Toledo 1991)].
