0 @N6000000000421743017@ NOTE John Pease appears to have been a favorite of his grandmother Margaret Pease, as shown by her will which was brought into court soon after action was taken in the settlement of his father's estate. It seems probable that he may ha
1 CONC ve been adopted by his grandmother before his fathers death. She doubtless had full authority for placing him in the care of Thomas Watson, to dispose of him as his own child, rendering it unnecessary for the court to take any act
1 CONC ion on his custody, as in the case of his older brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT John was a yoeman when he settled in that part of Salem called "Northfields". His name was mentioned frequently in the Essex County and Salem Town records as grantee and grantor of deeds, as a witness, as an overseer of wills, as 
1 CONC a constable, and etc. He was made a freeman 29 April 1668 and took the oath before the County court 30 June 1668. He was called "Captain" John Pease. He joined the First Church at Salem on 4 July 1667. It is believed that he, with
1 CONC  his two oldest sons John and Robert, went to what is now called Enfield in 1679, living the first winter in an excavation in the side of a hill about 40 rods from where the first meetinghouse stood On 23 July 1680 John and his tw
1 CONC o eldest sons had land granted to them. He sold his property in Salem in 1682. When they moved to Fresh Water Brook it was still a part of Springfield, Mass., in 1681 Enfield was established and became a part of Connecticut. The a
1 CONC rea is about 2.5 miles east of what is now called Thompsonville, CT. He sickened while making preparations for building, and died suddenly. This was 10 days after his wife died and the day before one of his daughters died.
0 @N6000000006348338895@ NOTE SECOND GENERATION.
1 CONT
1 CONT HISTORY OF JOHN PEASE,2 SEN.
1 CONT
1 CONT JOHN PEASE,2 (ROBERT,1) the ancestor of the Enfield Peases, was the second son of ROBERT and MARIE ( ) PEASE, first of England and last of Salem, Mass. He was probably born in England, about 1630, and came to this country when a l
1 CONC ad. The first notice we have of him is found in the court records at Salem, in the settlement of his father's estate, under date of August 27, 1644. From that time until his death we have no difficulty in tracing his history.
1 CONT
1 CONT He appears to have been a favorite of his grandmother, MARGARET PEASE, as shown by her will(*) which was brought into court, soon after action was taken in the settlement of his father's estate. It seems probable that he may have 
1 CONC been adopted by his grandmother, before his father's decease. She doubtless had full authority for placing him in the care of Thomas Watson, to dispose of him as his own child, rendering it unnecessary for the court to take any ac
1 CONC tion in relation to his custody, as in the case of his older brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married, first, Mary Goodell, daughter of Robert Goodell,(+) of Salem, who died January 5, 1669, three days after the birth of her fifth child. He married, second, Ann Cummings, daughter of Isaac Cummings,?? of Topsfield, Mass.
1 CONC , December 8, 1669, who died at Enfield, June 29, 1689. He settled as a "yeoman," in that part of Salem called "Northfields," which is now within the city limits, although in a farming district.
1 CONT
1 CONT We find this ancestor's name frequently in the Essex County and Salem Town records, as grantee, grantor, or a witness, in deeds, overseer of wills, constable, etc.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was made freeman, April 29, 1668, by the General Court, and took the oath before the County Court, June 30, following. He joined the First Church at Salem, to which his father and grandmother had belonged, July 4, 1667. "Octobe
1 CONC r 6, 1681, on sacrament day, John Pease and wife, had a letter of recommendation granted to the church at Springfield."(*) Not far from this last date, it is supposed he with his family, his two eldest sons' families and numerous 
1 CONC neighbors removed to Fresh Water Brook, then a part of Springfield, where he and his two oldest sons had had land granted them, July 23, of the year previous. We find him back at Salem, November, 1682, when he sold his "house, bar
1 CONC n, and out-buildings, and parcels of land," and acknowledged the deed before a Salem justice of the peace. He says in the deed, he was "late of Salem, now of Enfield." He was again there, February 26, 1683, to give evidence in a s
1 CONC uit at law, involving a boundary line of land once owned and laid out by his "father-in-law, Goodell." He then stated he was "aged about 53 years." In September, 1684, he was appointed administrator on the estate of Lot Killam, a 
1 CONC townsman of his when in Salem, and one of the first persons, it is said, who died at the "new settlement." 
1 CONT
1 CONT (II) John Pease, son of Robert Pease, was born in England, about 1630, and came to this country when a boy. He received by will from his grandmother, Margaret Pease, the greater part of her estate and she placed him in the care of
1 CONC  Thomas Watson, of Salem, to "dispose of as his own child." He settled in that part of Salem called Northfield, where he owned a farm. He was admitted a freeman of the colony, April 29, 1668, and had previously joined the church o
1 CONC f Salem, July 4. 1667. He and his wife were dismissed from the Salem church to the church at Springfield, Hampden Co., MA, and soon afterward he removed to that part of Springfield which is now Enfield. Connecticut. In 1682 he ret
1 CONC urned to Salem to testify in a lawsuit involving a boundary line. He was a good citizen and active in the church. He died suddenly, July 8, 1689, in the midst of his preparations for building a house. He married (first) Mary Goode
1 CONC ll, who died January 5, 1669, daughter of Robert and Catharine Goodell. He married (second) December 8, 1669, Ann Cummings, who died at Enfield, June 29, 1689, daughter of Isaac Cummings. Children by first wife: John, born May 30,
1 CONC  1654; Robert, May 14, 1656; Mary, October 8, 1658; Abraham, June 5, 1662; Jonathan, January 2, 1669. Children by second wife: Isaac, mentioned below; Abigail, born December 15, 1675, died 1689; James, born December 23, 1678.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cutter, William Richard. New England Families Genealogical and Memorial: Third Series, Volume I. 1915. Reprint, Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc., 1996.
0 @N6000000007150057488@ NOTE BIOGRAPHY
1 CONT : Captain John Pease was the son of Robert and Marie Pease and
1 CONT grandson of Margaret Pease, who made him her chief heir under the
1 CONT trusteeship of Thomas Watson, both the grandmother and father dying in
1 CONT 1644. Captain Pease remarried after Mary Goodell's death to Ann
1 CONT Cummings on 8 October 1669. She died 29 June 1689. John obtained a
1 CONT grant of land at Enfield, Hampshire County, Massachusetts in 1681.
1 CONT The town of Enfield, Mass. was later given to Connecticut. Captain
1 CONT Pease was appointed by his brother-in-law, Isaac Goodale, to be the
1 CONT administrator of his estate following his death in 1679. He served in
1 CONT this capacity until his own death on 8 July 1689 at Enfield,
1 CONT Massachusetts.[ewing-glaser.FBC.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT BIOGRAPHY
1 CONT : Captain John Pease was the son of Robert and Marie Pease and
1 CONT grandson of Margaret Pease, who made him her chief heir under the
1 CONT trusteeship of Thomas Watson, both the grandmother and father dying in
1 CONT 1644. Captain Pease remarried after Mary Goodell's death to Ann
1 CONT Cummings on 8 October 1669. She died 29 June 1689. John obtained a
1 CONT grant of land at Enfield, Hampshire County, Massachusetts in 1681.
1 CONT The town of Enfield, Mass. was later given to Connecticut. Captain
1 CONT Pease was appointed by his brother-in-law, Isaac Goodale, to be the
1 CONT administrator of his estate following his death in 1679. He served in
1 CONT this capacity until his own death on 8 July 1689 at Enfield,
1 CONT Massachusetts.
