0 @N6000000000337376796@ NOTE Source:  Nathan Goodwin, "Genealogical Notes on the First Settlers of CT and Mass" (1856):
1 CONT
1 CONT JOHN CASE married, 1. Sarah, daughter of William Spencer, of Hartford, Conn., about 1657. He resided in Windsor, Conn., until the spring of 1669, when he removed to Massacoe, (now Simsbury,) and settled in Weatogue. His first wife
1 CONC  died November 3, 1691, aged 55, and he married Elizabeth, widow of Nathaniel Loomis, of Windsor, and daughter (as supposed) of John Moore, of Windsor. Mr. Case was appointed constable for Massacoe, by the General Court, October 1
1 CONC 4, 1669, being the first person that ever held office at that place. He represented his town at the General Court in 1670, and several times afterwards. 
1 CONT
1 CONT John Case died at Simsbury, February 21, 1703-4. 
1 CONT
1 CONT His widow, Mrs. Elizabeth Case, died at Windsor, July 23, 1728, aged 90. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT FIRST GENERATION.
1 CONT Children of John Case and Sarah his wife.
1 CONT    I. Elizabeth, born about              1658. 
1 CONT   II. Mary,      born June           22, 1660. 
1 CONT  III. John,      born November        5, 1662. 
1 CONT   IV. William,   born June            5, 1665. 
1 CONT    V. Samuel,    born June            1, 1667. 
1 CONT   VI. Richard,   born August         27, 1669. 
1 CONT  VII. Bartholo- 
1 CONT        mew,      born October,           1670. 
1 CONT VIII. Joseph,    born April           6, 1674. 
1 CONT  IX. Sarah,      born August         14, 1676. 
1 CONT   X. Abigail,    born May             4, 1682. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2: Hinman, Royal R. Conneticut Puritan Settlers. Press of Case, Tiffany and Company. Hartford. 1852
1 CONT
1 CONT CASE, JOHN. (This name is occasionally spelled Cass.) He came to Windsor with the early settlers, supposed from Dorchester, Mass. He was a member of the Gen. Court of the Colony in May, 1670, May and October, 1675, and received ot
1 CONC her marks of respect from Windsor and Simsbury. He married Sarah Spencer, and remained at Windsor until about 1668 or '9, when he removed to Weatauge, in Simsbury. A part of his children were born in Windsor, and the others in Sim
1 CONC sbury. Issue, Mary, b. June 22, 1660; John, Jr., b. Nov. 5, 1662; Wm., b. June 5, 1665; Samuel, b. June 1, 1667, d. July 30, 1725; Richard, b. Aug. 27, 1669. The following b. at Simsbury: Sarah, b. April 14, 1676; Abigail, Elizabe
1 CONC th, Joseph and Bartholomew, d. Oct. 25, 1725. John, Sen., the father, d. Feb. 21, 1703-4. 
1 CONT
1 CONT John Case, of Simsbury, will drawn Nov. 21, 1700, but dated Dec. 11, 1700; a codicil to the will was dated Feb. 12, 1703-4. He made his mark to the instrument. Mr. Samuel Spencer, of Hartford, and John Case, his son, executors. In
1 CONC ventory exhibited and sworn to by his son, Richard Case, of Simsbury, March 9, 1703-4. Mr. John Case, Sen., d. Feb. 21, 1703-4. Inventory taken by John Slater, James Cornish and Andrew Robe, March 2, 1703-4, œ562, 5s. 1d. His wife
1 CONC  Elizabeth he gave œ5, to be paid her annually, which was agreed to before marriage, and he directed in his first will, that it should be paid to her as follows: William, Samuel, Richard and Bartholomew, to pay each 15s., and his 
1 CONC son Joseph, œ2 annually during her life, which was altered in his codicil. His daughter Elizabeth m. Tuller; daughters Mary Case, Sarah Phelps, and Abigail Case. His son William d. March 31, 1700, after his father's will was made 
1 CONC and before the death of his father. William's relict was Elizabeth; she with his brother John, of Simsbury, were adm'rs on William's estate in 1701. Inventory, œ276, 9s. 8d., dated May 29, 1700. He left children, viz., Elizabeth, 
1 CONC b. Sept., 1689; W'm., b. March 22, 1690-1; James, b. March 12, 1692-3; Rachel, b. Dec., 1694; Mary, b. 1696; Joshua, b. June 1, 1698; Mindwell, b. March 21, 1700. These children received their father's portion in their grandfather
1 CONC 's estate. 
1 CONT
0 @N6000000000682255112@ NOTE From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
