0 @N6000000003927570571@ NOTE He resided before 1669 in Windsor, Hartford Co., CT.  He resided after 1669 in Weatogue, Massacoe (now Simsbury), Hartford Co., CT.  He was appointed constable for Massocoe (now Simsbury), CT., being the first person that ever hel
1 CONC d office at that place Oct. 14, 1669.  He represented his town at the General Court 1670.  He left a will Nov. 21, 1700.  (names is 2nd wife, Elizabeth, six sons and four daughters, the executors were Samuel Spender of Hartford an
1 CONC d John Case, his son).  He resided Sept. 1, 1701 in Simsbury, Hartford Co., CT.
1 CONT
1 CONT There were no children of John Case and second wife Elizabeth (Moore?).  They were married after Nov. 3, 1691.
1 CONT
1 CONT Information from www.doit.com/tdoyle/generalogy/case.html   1/22/99.
1 CONT
1 CONT Information also in Goodwin's Genealogical Notes of Early Connecticut Settlers on Page 273.   Listed in Seattle Public Library  R 929 G635G[Carey.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT He resided before 1669 in Windsor, Hartford Co., CT.  He resided after 1669 in Weatogue, Massacoe (now Simsbury), Hartford Co., CT.  He was appointed constable for Massocoe (now Simsbury), CT., being the first person that ever hel
1 CONC d office at that place Oct. 14, 1669.  He represented his town at the General Court 1670.  He left a will Nov. 21, 1700.  (names is 2nd wife, Elizabeth, six sons and four daughters, the executors were Samuel Spender of Hartford an
1 CONC d John Case, his son).  He resided Sept. 1, 1701 in Simsbury, Hartford Co., CT.
1 CONT
1 CONT There were no children of John Case and second wife Elizabeth (Moore?).  They were married after Nov. 3, 1691.
1 CONT
1 CONT Information from www.doit.com/tdoyle/generalogy/case.html   1/22/99.
1 CONT
1 CONT Information also in Goodwin's Genealogical Notes of Early Connecticut Settlers on Page 273.   Listed in Seattle Public Library  R 929 G635G
0 @N6000000003017292487@ NOTE Some researchers have John parents as Richard Case and Elizabeth Loomis.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Case family is one of the ancient and honorable families of New England. They were noted as far back as Oliver Cromwell (1599-1659) and accumulated a fortune furnishing leather for the Army, being tanners and farniers. The rec
1 CONC ords show they came from York, England to Aylesham, England in the  year 1200. They held all the land around Aylesham so the town was said to be "Cased" in and were a clan by themselves. Many of them still reside there. Their land
1 CONC  surrounded possessions of Anna Boleyn, who became the second wife of Henry VIII of England, and was beheaded  "for treason" in 1536. The Cases were closely related by intermarriage to the Boleyns.   John arrived in New England on
1 CONC  the ship "Dorsett" on September 3, 1635 from Gravesend, England, at the age of 19 years. The ship landed around Long Island. He then moved to Dorchester, MA.  They next moved to Hartford, then moved to Maspeth Kills, NY (now Newt
1 CONC on according to our Genealogy). John and Sarah resided in Windsor CT from 1656  until 1669, when they removed to Simsbury and settled in the area known as "Weatogue".
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1667 John, with twenty others, received the first grant of land in Simsbury which was at Meadow Plain, Massaco (Simsbury). He lived about one mile south of the Pettibone Tavern, the second house south. He was a shoemaker and ha
1 CONC rness maker as well as a farmer. He was appointed constable for Massaco by the General Court Oct. 14, 1669, (Ct Colonial Records, II 484), and represented his town at the General Court in 1670 and  several times afterwards. He wit
1 CONC h six sons would go from Weatogue to what now (1900) is West Simsbury and cultivate the land there.  Hence it was first called Case's Farms, afterwards Farms Village, then West Simsbury. They carried their guns with them as a prot
1 CONC ection  from the Indians as well as to shoot game which was very plentiful there. The first of 5 children to John and Sarah were born in Windsor, the last 5 were born in Simsbury:
1 CONT
1 CONT John died in 1703 in Meadow Plains or the southern part of Simsbury. His place of burial is unknown but it is supposed to be by the side of his wife Sarah in Simsbury Cemetery, although no stone was erected. He had an estate of 56
1 CONC 2 pounds, making him a man of wealth at that time. John's will, dated 21 Nov 1700 named his wife Elizabeth, six sons, and four daughters. Samuel Spencer of Hartford (Sarah's brother), and John Case Jr. (John's son) were executors 
1 CONC of the estate. (records of Windsor, Hartford, Ct, NEHGR Jan 1851, pg 65). A codicil to the will is dated 12 Feb 1703.  He made his mark to the instrument. Mr. Samuel Spencer, of Hartford, and John Case, his son, executors. Invento
1 CONC ry exhibited and sworn to by his son, Richard Case, of Simsbury, March 9, 1703-4. Mr. John Case, Sen., d. Feb. 21, 1703-4. Inventory taken by John Slater, James Cornish and Andrew Robe, March 2, 1703-4, œ562, 5s. 1d. His wife Eliz
1 CONC abeth he gave œ5, to be paid her annually, which was agreed to before marriage, and he directed in his first will, that it should be paid to her as follows: William, Samuel, Richard and Bartholomew, to pay each 15s., and his son J
1 CONC oseph, œ2 annually during her life, which was altered in his codicil. His daughter Elizabeth m. Tuller; daughters Mary Case, Sarah Phelps, and Abigail Case. His son William d. March 31, 1700, after his father's will was made and b
1 CONC efore the death of his father. William's relict was Elizabeth; she with his brother John, of Simsbury, were adm'rs on William's estate in 1701. Inventory, œ276, 9s. 8d., dated May 29, 1700. He left children, viz., Elizabeth, b. Se
1 CONC pt., 1689; W'm., b. March 22, 1690-1; James, b. March 12, 1692-3; Rachel, b. Dec., 1694; Mary, b. 1696; Joshua, b. June 1, 1698; Mindwell, b. March 21, 1700. These children received their father's portion in their grandfather's es
1 CONC tate.
1 CONT
1 CONT There appear to be several errors in the below John Case data.  The referenced passenger list shows a William Case and a John Casson as passengers on the Dorset.  Could this be John Case?  Possibly, but why the difference in spell
1 CONC ing between brothers?  It could have been a transcriber's error or and original error of the ship list. Have also seen the last name spelled Cass. Note-The English did not have middle names in the 1500's and early 1600's, research
1 CONC  into the early sources shows this.  Have seen John Case who came to Simsbury listed as John Trustin Case, but this is without a credible source. The credible source recs. list him only as John Case & the immigrant to Rhode Island
1 CONC  as William Case as well as Henry Case who settled Long Island.
1 CONT
1 CONT List of Official Civil, Military, and Ecclesiastical of Connecticut Colony Genealogy Library.com Main Page, pg 10 Case, John (d. 1704). Constable (Simsbury), Oct. 1669; Deputy (Simsbury), May 1670, Oct. 1674, May 1675.
1 CONT
1 CONT Denver Public Library - Genealogy section has a book "CASE" "A BRIEF ACCOUNT OF THE LIFE OF JOHN CASSE" or  929.2 -- c 337.2 c --as printed inside. 24 pages. small book and VERY interesting..by A. P. Case of Vernon, N.Y....Name la
1 CONC ter spelled Case when he signed his will.  John came over on "The Ship , Dorset, John Flower, master, sailed from Gravesend, Eng, 1635 - John age 19. . Gives will, and  list of children plus many interesting stories .
1 CONT This is transcribed from a copy of "A brief account of the life of John Casse" that someone mailed me.
