0 @N6000000002358584906@ NOTE A Genealogical and Historic Record of John Pease Sen., Part II, pages9-15:
1 CONT Among those who took passage from Ipswich, England, in the ship Francis, John Cutting, master, bound for New England, toward the last of April, 1634, as shown by the custom-house books, were ROBERT and JOHN PEASE. Their ages upon 
1 CONC the books were twenty-seven years each. It has been supposed that Robert Pease was the elder, and it may be that the probable ages of passengers were set down by the custom-house officers without questioning. The vessel was report
1 CONC ed back to Ipswich as having arrived at Boston, MA, without the loss of a single passenger. Neither of the men had wives with them. They must have come in a later vessel of which no record is extant as is the case of hundreds of o
1 CONC thers among the early settlers. 
1 CONT A boy aged three years, named Robert Pease, was among the list of passengers of the ship Francis. It had been supposed that the child belonged to John Pease because of the proximity of their names upon the customhouse book, but th
1 CONC e development of additional facts show that he must have been the son of Robert Pease. For more than thirty years after this emigration, Salem, Essex, MA, is the only place where the name of Robert Pease is found in history in thi
1 CONC s country. 
1 CONT In January, 1637, ROBERT and JOHN PEASE had land granted them at Salem. A transaction growing out of these grants shows that the men were brothers. It seems conclusive that the Peases of the ship Francis in 1634 are identical with
1 CONC  those who had land granted them at Salem in 1637. To this ROBERT PEASE of Salem belongs the distinction of being the progenitor of the Salem and Enfield Peases. 
1 CONT It is not known positively from what part of England these men originally came, yet evidence has been produced within a few years past which throws considerable light upon their history and seems to point us to the family and loca
1 CONC lity in England from which they sprang. 
1 CONT The late Mr. Frederick S. Pease prepared a paper which was published in the New England Historical and Genealogical Register for 1856, Vol. X., which is directly to the point. He says: 
1 CONT "In Vol. III., page 30, New England Historical and Genealogical Register, there is mention of John Pease, aged 27, and Robert Pease, aged 27, who came from Ipswich, England, to Boston on board the ship Francis, in April, 1634, and
1 CONC  removed to Salem where they were known to have been in 1637. It was assumed that John was the ancester of the families embraced in the account which was published in that volume and his name accordingly placed at the head; but fa
1 CONC rther research and more reflection have served to transfer the honor to Robert. 
1 CONT "Mr. Somberby, to whom much is due for his researches in England, writes under date of June 6, 1854, that while making some genealogical investigations in Essex, he met with the will of Robert Pease of Great Baddow, and considerin
1 CONC g it worth the trouble he visited that place and made extracts from the parish register: "'Robert Pease, Co. of Essex, Locksmith, Will, dated May 10th 1623, Mentions his wife Margaret, sons Robert and John, daughter Elizabeth, son
1 CONC -in-law Abraham Page and brother-in-law Francis King. Will proved June 12th 1623.' 
1 CONT "From a long list of baptisms, marriages and burials dating from 1540 to 1623, the following have been selected: "'John, son of Robert Pease baptized May 24, 1593. John infant son of Robert Pease buried Jan. 10, 1599. John son of 
1 CONC Robert Pease baptized Nov. 20, 1608.' 
1 CONT "There is no record of the birth of Robert, the other son mentioned in the will, and Mr. Somberby thinks he must have been baptized in some other parish. "It would be imprudent to assert positively that John and Robert, whose name
1 CONC s are mentioned in the will, are the same who came in the ship Francis, but it seems not improbable that Great Baddow is the locality and the family of Robert Pease, that to which the ancestry of the family may be traced. "Great B
1 CONC addow is in what is called the Hundred of Chelmsford, about thirty miles north-east from London on the thoroughfare to Ipswich; the most convenient place of embarkation from that neighborhood, and old Norfolk and Essex here were s
1 CONC ettled chiefly by people from counties of the same name in England." 
1 CONT Since Mr. Pease published the above article it has been shown what was before only supposed, that Robert and John Pease who had land granted them at Salem in 1637, were brothers, and that the given name of the mother of Robert was
1 CONC  Margaret. She probably emigrated to this country with the family of her son, Robert Pease. She was undoubtedly the "widow Pease" who joined the First Church at Salem, in 1639. Her will, made during her last sickness, and the inve
1 CONC ntory of her estate, were presented to the Court at Salem, September 1, 1644. We can find no history of these brothers from the time of their embarkation at Ipswich until January 2, 1637, when they had land granted them at Salem. 
1 CONC At this time Robert Pease was granted ten acres and John Pease twenty acres. It need not be inferred necessarily that they did not dwell at Salem before the above date. The records, of land grants made at Salem prior to January 19
1 CONC , 1635, are not extant. There were but few grants made, comparatively from January 19, 1635, to January 2, 1637. 
1 CONT It is known that there were certain persons living at Salem as early as 1635, and some earlier, but we can find no evidence that land was granted them until 1637. Yet the subsequent history of these men show that they must have ow
1 CONC ned land there before 1637. These brothers sold the precise amount which was granted them in 1637, and there is no record that Robert Pease had land granted him afterwards, although when he died he was in possession of 11 acres. T
1 CONC hese facts show that the brothers may have gone to Salem the year they landed in America. 
1 CONT There is a transaction recorded at Salem in the book of land grants, bearing date February 13, 1652, which furnishes us an important fact. A portion of the record reads thus: 
1 CONT "Robert Goodell having forty acres of land granted him long since by the town and he having bought land of several other persons that had land granted them" [then follow the names of the persons of whom he bought and the amount he
1 CONC  bought of each] "in the whole 480 acres, it is ordered that the said Goodell shall enjoy the said 480 acres, &c." 
1 CONT The record mentions the names of fifteen different persons of whom he made the purchases. Thirteen of these persons had precisely the amount granted them in 1637, which the record says Goodell bought of them. The names of the othe
1 CONC r two persons are found among the grantees in 1637, though the amount of land specified in the two transactions do not agree. 
1 CONT Among these persons of whom he made the purchases, were (to use the language of the record,) "Robert Pease and brother, 30 acres." This action of the authorities occurred after the two oldest sons of Robert Pease, Sen., were old e
1 CONC nough to transact business for themselves. But the sons had not had land granted them before that time as shown by the book of land grants. Besides, had they made such sales after their majority, they would probably have been foun
1 CONC d on record in the office of Registry of Deeds. No transfers of land prior to 1644 are found recorded in the Registry's office. Sales after that date seem generally to have been recorded there. It is probable that Goodell presente
1 CONC d the subject before the authorities for the purpose of having his purchases of land, that were made many years before, and of which no record had been made, properly sanctioned and legalized. It seems conclusive, therefore, that 
1 CONC the men who had land granted them in 1637, were involved in the transaction, and not the sons of one of them. If this point is made clear it is equally clear that Robort and John Pease, to whom land was granted in 1637, were broth
1 CONC ers. This incident furnishes us with the only direct proof we have of their relationship, and establishes the fact that there were two John Peases living in Salem, cotemporaneously, for a time before 1644. The one the son of Mrs. 
1 CONC Weston, the other the son of Widow Margaret Pease.
1 CONT The history of ROBERT PEASE, Sen., in the "New World" is brief, as he lived here only a decade. On the first day of October, 1643, he joined the First Church at Salem. Two weeks later three of his children, viz: Nathaniel, Sarah a
1 CONC nd Mary were baptized. Less than one year from this he was dead. The inventory of his estate, signed by two appraisers but without date, was brought into court at Salem, August 27, 1644. His estate was a small one though valuable 
1 CONC to his posterity. For the records made at its settlement compared with the last will and testament of his mother, throw light upon his family history and furnish us with unmistakable evidences that he was the father of JOHN PEASE,
1 CONC  SEN., last of Enfield, Ct. Since these records have been carefully examined, the given name of the father of the latter need no longer be "assumed" or held in doubt.(*) 
1 CONT The given name of the wife of Robert Pease was Marie,(+) the French orthography for Mary. Her name furnishes presumptive evidence that her parents may have been Protestant refugees from France. The time of her decease is not known
1 CONC , as no record of the settlement of her estate or division of her property can be found. Her name appears only in connection with the settlement of her husband's estate and in the inventory of his mother's. We are unable to ascert
1 CONC ain the exact number of children there were in the family. Five are all we have any knowledge of. 
1 CONT There are some reasons for supposing there may have been another child in the family. 
1 CONT A petition was gotten up in 1714 and signed by those "having cottages or dwelling-places before the year 1661" who it appears had rights in the common lands in Salem which others did not possess. Among those petitioners were Natha
1 CONC niel and Isaac Pease. This is the only instance where the name of Isaac Pease occurs, except in reference to the grandchildren of Robert Pease, Sen., or their posterity. This Isaac Pease may have been a son of Robert Pease, Sen., 
1 CONC born and baptized in England, who with his brother Nathaniel, shared an interest in the common lands of Salem as an heir to his mother's small estate. Quite likely he had no family and his brothers named sons for him. 
1 CONT
1 CONT 1643: Joined the church at Salem.31,p94 at which time three of his children were baptised
1 CONT Died before his mother, his estate inventoried 3 Nov 1644 and the widow Marie appointed administratrix. Wdow Marie remarried Richard Haines of Beverly, MA, taking the three other childred with her.77, p12
1 CONT He first married Lydia
1 CONT Robert Pease b. c. 1603, md. 2 Dec. 1628 at Widford, Essex to Lydia West, spinster of Great Baddow. This information from Essex Mg. Lic. In London This information from Marsall McCoy via Scott Foster. Is this Robert and Lydia????
1 CONT The Complete Book of Emigrants, 1607-1776
