1 CONT Hartford, or Windsor, Hartford Co., CT. Married JOYCE SMITH, Springfield, MA, or Windsor, Hartford Co., CT;
1 CONT b. Abt. 1625, England; d. in CT or MA.
1 CONT Children of GILES1 GIBBS and KATHERINE CARWYTHAM are:
1 CONT v. JACOB2 GIBBS, b. Bet. January - March, 1629/30, Exeter, Co. Devon, England; d. March 18, 1708/09, Windsor,
1 CONT Harford Co., CT.
1 CONT vi. SARAH2 GIBBS, b. Bef. June 05, 1631, Exeter, Co. Devon, England; d. November 01, 1689, Windsor, Harford Co.?, CT.
1 CONT vii. SAMUEL2 GIBBS, b. 1635, Dorchester, Suffolk Co., MA or Windsor, Harford Co., CT; d. February 08, 1718/19,
1 CONT (East?) Windsor, Hartford Co., CT.
1 CONT viii. CAPT. BENJAMIN2 GIBBS, b. Bet. 1637 - 1640, Dorchester, Suffolk Co., MA, or Windsor, Hartford Co., CT; d. 1676,
1 CONT Windsor, Hartford Co., CT?
1 CONT
1 CONT Generations No. 3 through 7
1 CONT See Descendants of Giles Gibbs compiled by James Hemenway Gibbs. This 66 page genealogical report is available as a .pdf file
1 CONT which may be downloaded and viewed via Adobe Acrobat Reader.
1 CONT Additional notes on Giles Gibbs and other Gibbs lines in Colonial New England
1 CONT
1 CONT     I have done extensive traditional research into Giles' line, and am currently trying to verify Giles' supposed English line that, if correct, would go back to before 1400. I have also gathered a fair amount on another early Ne
1 CONC w England Gibbs immigrant, Matthew Gibbs, as I descend from him along a maternal line.
1 CONT
1 CONT     According to my research, there should be three distinct Gibbs lines in New England prior to 1650, who may or may not connect at some earlier time in England, and who thus may represent distinct DNA groups:
1 CONT
1 CONT     1. Giles Gibbs of Co. Dorset and/or Co. Somerset, England, who emigrated to Dorchester, Massachusetts Bay Colony, between 1630-33, and who soon after removed to Windsor, Connecticut, dying in Windsor in 1641.
1 CONT
1 CONT     2. Matthew Gibbs, supposedly of Co. Kent, England, who emigrated to Charlestown, Massachusetts Bay Colony, in 1639, and who later removed to Framingham and Sudbury, Massachusetts, dying in 1697.
1 CONT
1 CONT     3. Thomas Gibbs who emigrated from England to Sandwich, Cape Cod, Plymouth Colony.
1 CONT
1 CONT     There appear to be several other Gibbs lines that came to New England later during the 1700s, including at least one in Rhode Island that is thought possibly to connect to a South Carolina line of Gibbs.
1 CONT
1 CONT     There is also a Barbados line that is thought possibly to connect to the Dorset line of Giles Gibbs.
1 CONT
1 CONT     And there is the Charles City, Virginia, line of John Gibbs posted at the Gibbs Surname DNA Project web site that is said to have also come from Dorset. The DNA project shows John and Giles' lines as separate.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Giles Gibbs is believed to be the great grandson of Anthony Gibbes of Netherbury, Co. Dorset, England (Anthony > John > John > Giles). Anthony's line was documented in the Visitation of Dorsetshire of 1623 back to Honington, W
1 CONC arwickshire, where the Gibbs were gentry. The Visitation of Warwickshire carries this Gibbs line further back to about 1400. I have been communicating with an English participant in the Gibbs Surname DNA Project with regard to thi
1 CONC s English line -- George Gibbs, who was born in Dorset. Interestingly, his DNA is not a match to either of the two American DNA groups that claim to come from Dorset, though George has only been able to confirm his own line back t
1 CONC o about 1800. I would be very interested in trying to establish a DNA link that would prove the Gibbs migration from Honington to Netherbury as recorded in the Visitations, and then to prove Giles' link to the same. Or is the Virg
1 CONC inia City John Gibbs the rightful descendant of Anthony?
1 CONT
1 CONT     The earliest English Gibbs record I have found an index listing for is John Gybbys who is recorded in the Glastonbury Abbey Compotus Roll of 1338. That would seem to put a Gibbs clan in Somerset, possibly prior to the establis
1 CONC hment of the Honington clan in Warwickshire. Gibbs is also name found in modern day France -- at least some of the English Gibbses are believed to be Anglo-Norman, i.e. associated with the Norman Conquest. My understanding is that
1 CONC  Gibb and Gibson are typically Scottish names, and thus not Anglo-Norman. Let's see how the DNA plays out.
1 CONT
1 CONT =========================================================
0 @N6000000001503949805@ NOTE GILES GIBBS
1 CONT
1 CONT ORIGIN: St. Sidwell, Exeter, Devonshire
1 CONT MIGRATION: 1632
1 CONT FIRST RESIDENCE: Dorchester
1 CONT REMOVES: Windsor 1636
1 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP: Admission to Dorchester church prior to 4 March 1632/3 implied by freemanship.
1 CONT FREEMAN: 4 March 1632/3 [MBCR 1:367].
1 CONT EDUCATION: The widow's inventory included 12s. in books.
1 CONT OFFICES: Committee to make Dorchester rate, 3 November 1633 [DTR 4]; chosen Dorchester selectman (as "Go[odman] Gibbes"), 28 October 1634 [DTR 7].
1 CONT ESTATE: Ordered to build twenty feet of fence, for one cow, 3 April 1633 [DTR 2]; granted three acres of land, 22 November 1634 [DTR 9]; granted two acres of meadow, and lot formerly Simon Hoyt's to be divided between Giles Gibbs 
1 CONC and Mr. Roger Williams, 17 April 1635 [DTR 11]; granted ten acres of land, 1 February 1635/6 [DTR 15]; received Lot #31, four acres, in meadow beyond Naponset [DTR 321].
1 CONT    In the Windsor land inventory of 28 February 1640/1, Giles Gibbs had been granted four parcels, and had purchased one: seven acre homelot; ten and a half acres at the Great Meade; a parcel over the Great River, thirty rods in b
1 CONC readth by three miles in length; forty-seven acres of upland above Rocky Hill; and (purchased of George Hull) "half an acre of the west end of his homelot." This was annotated to show that Gregory Gibbs had received by bequest fro
1 CONC m Giles Gibbs the lot over the great river thirty rods in breadth and in length three miles and that Katherine Gibbs had purchased this lot from Gregory Gibbs. Two other purchases had been made (perhaps by the widow Gibbs): four a
1 CONC cres from Thomas Stayres and half an acre from Steven Terry [WiLR 1:94].
1 CONT    In his will, dated 18 May 1641 (but date of probate not known), "Gyles Gibbs, of Wyndsor, on Connecticutt, yeoman, being weak in body" ordered "my son Gregory" to be apprenticed to "some godly man for the space of five years" a
1 CONC nd if he stay out his time he is to have my lot over the great river, also if "my overseers have any encouragement to judge him worthy ... £5 at age twenty-one years"; and bequeathed to "my two sons Samuel & Benjamin" £20 each; to
1 CONC  "my daughter Sarah" £20 at age twenty-one; to "Jacob, my son, ... my house and lots, meadows, home lot and great lot and lots whatsoever on this side the great river after his mother's life"; to "my wife" all my lots, houses, hou
1 CONC sehold goods, cattle, chattels, discharge of my debts; "provided that in case my said overseers have no good encouragement concerning the disposition of my son Gregory, but do judge him unworth a father's blessing under their hand
1 CONC s, my will is that my executor shall have the said lot toward the education of my children until my son Jacob shall attain the age of twenty-one years, and then my will is that my son Jacob shall have it"; "Katharine my wife" exec
1 CONC utrix; the deacons of the church of Windsor overseers. In a postscript he bequeathed to "Elizaphatt Gregory ten bushels of corn"; and to "Richard Wellar" 40s. [Manwaring 1:14-15; CCCR 1:504-05].
1 CONT    In her will, dated 12 September 1660 and proved 6 December 1660, "Catharine Gibbs of Windsor" bequeathed to "my eldest son Jacob my dwelling house, barn & orchard, with the land whereon they stand, & all the land adjoining ther
1 CONC eunto, which is my proper right by purchase," but if he dies without an heir living to the age of twenty-one then this land to go to "my second son Samuell"; to "my son Benjamin ... my lot on the other side the Great River, the wh
1 CONC ich I purchased of Gregory Gibbs my son-in-law [i.e., stepson]"; her moveables to "my son Jacob ... & to my daughter-in-law his wife," to "my son Benjamin," to "my son Jacob's daughter Mary," and to "my youngest son and executor B
1 CONC enjamin"; the residue to be divided among "my three youngest children," with specific reference to "my son Samuel's part" and "my daughter Sarah's part" [Hartford PD Case #2148; Manwaring 1:116-18].
1 CONT    The inventory of the estate "left by the widdoe Gibbs Deceased" was taken 21 November 1660 and totalled £220 7s., including real estate valued at £160: "one home lot, seven acres," £30; "one meadow lot, ten acres," £50; "one lo
1 CONC t of upland, forty-eight acres," £20; "the dwelling house with barn & land added to the homelot by the said widow's purchase," £30; and "one lot over the great River," £30 [Hartford PD Case #2148].
1 CONT    Considerable mention appears in the Suffolk deeds regarding the estate of Katherine Gibbs in Windsor and Wethersfield, particularly as it related to her son, Benjamin, who died in Boston [SLR 11:192, 197, 252, 369].
1 CONT BIRTH: By about 1600 based on estimated date of first marriage.
1 CONT DEATH: Buried Windsor 21 May 1641 [WiVR].
1 CONT MARRIAGE: (1) By about 1625 _____ _____; she died by 1629.
1 CONT    (2) St. Sidwell, Exeter, 13 April 1629 Katherine Carwithe [TAG 61:33]; she died Windsor 24 October 1660 [TAG 61:32].
1 CONT CHILDREN:
1 CONT    With first wife
1 CONT
1 CONT    i   GREGORY, b. say 1625 (to be apprenticed five years in 1641; certainly adult in 1649 [RPCC 71]); m. after 1676 Joyce (Smith) Osborn, widow of James [evidence not seen, but so stated in Windsor Hist 2:287].
1 CONT
1 CONT    With second wife
1 CONT    ii   JACOB, bp. St. Sidwell, Exeter, 28 March 1629/30 [TAG 61:33]; m. Windsor 4 December 1657 Elizabeth Androus [Grant 43].
1 CONT
1 CONT    iii   SARAH, bp. St. Sidwell, Exeter, 5 June 1631 [TAG 61:33]; m. Windsor 5 December 1661 John Share [Grant 71]. (On 5 February 1665/6 John Winthrop Jr. treated "Shire Sarah wife of John Shire a Cornish man, she was formerly Sa
1 CONC rah Gibs" [WMJ 622].)
1 CONT
1 CONT    iv   SAMUEL, b. say 1633; m. Windsor 15 April 1664 Hepzibah Dibble [Grant 44].
1 CONT
1 CONT    v   BENJAMIN, b. say 1635 (adult in 1660 [RPCC 215]); m. by 1676 Lydia Scottow [SLR 11:192], daughter of Joshua Scottow.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT COMMENTS: In 1986 James A. Rasmussen published the marriage record and the two baptisms from St. Sidwell, Exeter, Devonshire, which settle much of the chronology of the Gibbs family [TAG 61:32-34]. These three records show that Gi
1 CONC les Gibbs and his family could not have come on the Mary & John, and, in conjunction with the will of the widow Katherine Gibbs, determine unequivocally the birth order of all five children.
1 CONT    As Rasmussen notes, the minister at St. Sidwell, Exeter, when the Gibbs family appeared there was Rev. JOHN WARHAM, and it may be that Gibbs was attracted to that minister, and followed him to New England in 1632.
1 CONT    On 4 August 1635 Massachusetts Bay court ordered "Will[ia]m Wills to pay to Gyles Gibbs the sum of 16s., for felony by him committed" [MBCR 1:153].
1 CONT    In the years after Giles Gibbs died, the family was often in court for unacceptable behavior. In one court on 7 March 1650/1 Jacob Gibbs was complained of for several misdemeanors and fined 40s., Samuel Gibbs was "to be correct
1 CONC ed by his master" and Sarah Gibbs "the daughter of the widow Gibbs hath carried herself very sinfully and disorderly the court judges it necessary that she should be put to service, and therefore if her mother does not provide her
1 CONC  a good service ... Sarah Gibbs is to appear at the court ... and from thence be sent to the house of correction" [RPCC 97]. She was still in trouble in 1660 [RPCC 217].
