0 @N6000000006348338923@ NOTE ROBERT PEASE,3 (JOHN,2 ROBERT,1) second son of JOHN, Sen., and MARY (GOODELL) PEASE, born at Salem, May 14, 1656; married Abigail Randall, December 16, 1678, and first settled at Salem in the "Northfields." He shared with his elde
1 CONC r brother the enterprise of leaving the sea-coast to seek out and make ready new homes for himself, his relatives and neighbors, in the Connecticut Valley. To which place his family removed in 1681.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is said to have been one of the first constables chosen by vote of the town of Enfield.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died at Enfield, 1744, aged 88 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT His children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT  17.  1. WILLIAM, b. at Salem, September 26, 1679. d. at Enfield, 168
1 CONT  18.  2. MARY, b. at Salem, March 11, 1681. m. Israel Phelps, 1703. 
1 CONT  19.  3. ABIGAIL, b. at Enfield, 1682. m. Nathan Hayward. 
1 CONT  20.  4. ROBERT, b. at Enfield, "February 2, 1684."(*) 
1 CONT  21.  5. SAMUEL, b. at Enfield, December 30, 1686. 
1 CONT  22.  6. EPHRAIM, b. at Enfield, April 9, 1689. Probably d. an infan
1 CONT  23.  7. DANIEL, b. at Enfield, May 23, 1692. 
1 CONT  24.  8. HANNAH, b. at Enfield, June, 1694. m. Daniel Miller, 2
1 CONT           Gersham Sexton. 
1 CONT  25.  9. MARGARET, b. at Enfield, December, 1695. 
1 CONT  26. 10. EBENEZER, b. at Enfield, 1699. 
1 CONT           [Salem and Enfield Town Records.] 
0 @N6000000000632084593@ NOTE Jonathan "Gilburt" was bound over to the Hampshire Co., Mass., court on 28 March 1676, "for that he being one Yea a Cheife Actor in that Riotious Assembly at Hadley being then Present with them and haveing his Cudgell, Sideing wit
1 CONC h them in their Publique affronteing of Authority" (Pynchon Court Rec. p. 285). This Jonathan Gilbert was probably Jonathan3 (jonathan2, Thomas1) of Hartford and Middletown, who, about 1674, had admitted to "youthful vanities" (Gi
1 CONC lbert Fam. p. 72).
1 CONT
1 CONT (THE ENGLISH ORIGINS OF THOMAS GILBERT OF BRAINTREE MASS., AND WETHERSFIELD CONN., from THE AMERICAN GENEALOGIST, JULY 1992, by Douglas Richardson)
0 @N6000000001383955693@ NOTE In January 1691, William and Elizabeth are mentioned by Rev. Moses Fiske, then pastor of the Braintree church, as having been long in full communion. 
1 CONT _______________________________________________________-
1 CONT there may have been a case of OWEN vs. DAVIES in the Chancery Court in London, suggesting prior acquaintance of the families
1 CONT _________________________________________-
1 CONT William OWEN1 was probably born about 1611 in England, possibly London, from which he immigrated in 1634 first to Barbados at age 23,[1]( 1]Ship Passenger Lists)  then to Boston sometime before his 1650 marriage.[3]( Marriages to 
1 CONC 1850[3]Connection between Ship Passenger List & presence in MA)
1 CONT  He married 29th day of the 7th month at Braintree, Norfolk Co., MA[2] to Elizabeth DAVIS-DAVIES of London. The unpublished OWEN genealogy suggests further:
1 CONT "The ship Hopewell, Capt. Thomas Wood, which cleared from the port of London for Barbados 17 Feb 1634 (1635 New Style) contained among 150 passengers a William OWEN, age 23; a John OWEN age 20, and an Owen WILLIAMS, age 21. 
1 CONT _____________________
1 CONT The following April the ship Elizabeth, Capt. William Stagg, sailing from London for New England, carried among 28 passengers, mostly women, Margaret DAVIES, aged 32, and her three children of whom the youngest was Elizabeth, aged
1 CONC  one year. It is possible that William OWEN of the Hopewell was the same appearing 15 years later in Braintree. The infant Elizabeth DAVIES on the ship Elizabeth is known to be the same who married William OWEN at Braintree. Her m
1 CONC other, Margaret, became the wife of Charles GRICE of Braintree who in his will described William as his "son.""9( 9]Unpublished OWEN Genealogy by Henry W. Owen, Jr. circa 1935)http://www.rootsweb.com/~mnichols/owendesc/owenwm.htm
1 CONT ________________________________________________________
1 CONT "There were OWEN families at both Salem and the adjoining town of Marblehead prior to 1723 and both these families seem to have been closely connected with the Braintree family. Morgan OWEN of Salem married there in 1670 [to Eliza
1 CONC beth DICKASON] and died some two years later leaving a son John born in 1671 and a son Nathaniel who "lived not long." John married in 1687 Mary TUCKSBERRY. I have found no record that this John and Mary had a son John. If they di
1 CONC d and it could be shown, he might possibly qualify as the Falmouth settler. In the Marblehead family there were a number of Johns, but each of these is definitely otherwise accounted for. Since, however, both these families seem t
1 CONC o have been related to the Braintree family, and were seamen, what more natural than that a member of the Braintree family, settingout to seek his fortune in Maine, should set out via Salem?"http://www.rootsweb.com/~mnichols/owend
1 CONC esc/owenri.htm
1 CONT _____________________________________
