0 @N6000000000337352691@ NOTE Source #1:  Goodwin, Nathaniel. Genealogical Notes of Connecticut and Massachusetts. F.A.Brown. Hartford. 1856. 
1 CONT
1 CONT DANIEL CLARK, one of the first settlers of Windsor, Conn., was twice married. 1. To Mary Newberry, of Windsor, daughter of Thomas Newberry,(+) June 13, 1644. She died August 29, 1688. 2. To Martha Wolcott, widow of Simon Wolcott, 
1 CONC deceased, of the same town, and sister of William Pitkin, Esq., one of the early settlers of Hartford, Conn. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Hon. Daniel Clark died August 12, 1710, "in the 88th year of his age, or thereabouts." [Windsor Records.] He was an Attorney at Law, and, during his long life, was generally in public office.?? 
1 CONT
1 CONT Mrs. Martha Clark, alias Wolcott, died October 13, 1719, aged --. [Grave-stone East Windsor burying-ground, where several of her children rest.] 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT FIRST GENERATION.
1 CONT Children of Daniel Clark,--by his first wife.
1 CONT    I. Mary,       born April      4, 1645.  Died in childhood. 
1 CONT   II. Josiah,     born January   21, 1648. 
1 CONT  III. Elizabeth,  born October   28, 1651. 
1 CONT   IV. Daniel,     born April      5, 1654. 
1 CONT    V. John,       born April     10, 1656. 
1 CONT   VI. Mary,       born September 22, 1658. 
1 CONT  VII. Samuel,     born July       6, 1661. 
1 CONT VIII. Sarah,      born August     7, 1663. 
1 CONT   IX. Hannah,     born August    25, 1665.  Died in early life. 
1 CONT    X. Nathaniel,  born September  8, 1666. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Children,--by his second wife,--none.
1 CONT
1 CONT (*)The principal part of this paper was prepared from statistics collected in 1851 and 1852. 
1 CONT
1 CONT (+)Mr. Thomas Newberry died at Dorchester, Mass., not long after his arrival in New England. 
1 CONT He left a will, which is of record in Suffolk County Probate Court. 
1 CONT
1 CONT ??For the use of their Magistrates, the town of Windsor appropriated a particular pew in 
1 CONT their Meeting House and in addition to the ordinary finish, ordered it to be "wainscotted." 
1 CONT On the elevation of Mr. Clark to the Magistracy, the Town, in Town meeting assembled, 
1 CONT passed the following vote:" 
1 CONT
1 CONT "May 5, 1651. At a meeting of the Towne, Mr. Clark was appointed to sitt in the greate 
1 CONT pew." 
1 CONT
