1 CONT One of first settler of Windsor, CT.. Died in 88th year.
1 CONT
1 CONT __________________________________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT From: "Paul B. Phelps" <pbphelps
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is excerpted  loosely from Frank Thistlethwaite, _Dorset Pilgrims: The Story of West  County Pilgrims Who Went to New England in the 17th Century_ (London:  Barrie & Jenkins, 1989; reprinted Interlaken NY: Heart of t
1 CONC he Lakes  Publishing, 1993).  You'll find more information on their descendants in standard references like H.R. Stiles, _History and Genealogies of Ancient  Windsor CT_ (1891), Savage's _Genealogical Dictionary_, or any good work
1 CONC   on New England genealogy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Daniel Clark (b.a. 1620) emigrated to Windsor CT about 1640 in the company of a new minister, Ephraim Huitt, probably from the West Country of England.  He soon became a leading citizen, serving as an officer (eventually Captain) 
1 CONC in the Troop of Horse, appointed Assistant for the royal charter in 1662, serving on a committee to establish the first public school in 1673 (and later teaching for one year), and appearing in lawsuits as an elder of the church i
1 CONC n 1693 -- among many other duties and
1 CONT accomplishments.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary Newberry (b. 1626) was from one of the richest families in Windsor.   Her father was Thomas Newberry (1594-1636), "a younger son of a younger  son of fairly prominent Dorset gentry," had made a good living as a  London lawyer
1 CONC  before retiring to the country and then emigrating to CT in  1634 with his second wife and seven children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Daniel and Mary were married in 1644, and there were almost certainly  several other children before John was born in 1656.
1 CONT (Thistlethwaite  doesn't talk about the children of this "second generation.")  Daniel remained active and energetic in his later years.  When Mary died in  1688, Daniel quickly married Martha (Pritkin) Wolcott, widow of Simon Wol
1 CONC cott who had died less than a year earlier.  Daniel died in 1688, but Martha (b. 1639) lived on until 1719.
1 CONT
1 CONT       ___________________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT from Descendants of John Porter:  m. 15 Jul 1685 Mary Newberry.
1 CONT
1 CONT       ___________________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT from Search for the Passengers of the Mary & John - 1630, Volume 17, West Country Ancestries, 1620-1643, Part 1, ISBN 0-941273-17-2.  The Mary & John Clearing House [Burton w. Spear; 5602-305th st.; Toledo, OH 43611].  sent by Rob
1 CONC ert W. Oliphant; 3 Robinson Road; Westford, MA  01886; Email:  oliphant pages 103-110:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary NEWBERRY - Bpt. 22-Oct-1626, Whitchurch Canonicorum, Dorset.
1 CONT Died 29-Aug-1688.  She married Capt. Daniel CLARK, 13-Jun-1644, Windsor (1622- 1710) who m. (2) Mrs. Martha (PITKIN) WOLCOTT.  He came to Windsor with Rev. Huit.  He was Deputy (1657-1661), Assistant (1662-1664), Secretary of the 
1 CONC Connecticut Colony (1658-1663), and captain (1664).  Ten children.
1 CONT     __________________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 15022 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   NOTE From:     BOB CAULFIELD <ajrotc01@email.badkreuznach.army.mil>
1 CONT
1 CONT Daniel CLARK #1842 of Windsor, CT, came 1639 in company with Rev.
1 CONT Ephraim HUET, by which he was named admin 1644 of his will.
1 CONT Daniel was rep 1657-61; sec't of the Colony 1658-63; Asst.
1 CONT 1662-64; Capt of the cavalry troop 1664, and at his death 12 Aug 1710, age 87, left Josiah, Eliz, Daniel, John, Mary, Samuel, and Sarah.
1 CONT
1 CONT     ____________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT Descendants of John Porter via Iris Grimmett: m. 15-Jul-1644
1 CONT
1 CONT     ____________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT [from Noral Baughman; 3830 80th Ave SE; Olympia, WA  98501; (360) 491-4193 <noral
1 CONT
1 CONT               HISTORY OF NATHANIEL CLARK AND FAMILY
1 CONT                            AS TOLD BY
1 CONT                     ELIZABETH CLARK BERRYMAN
1 CONT     [This is Noral's wife's grandmother, who died at age 99.]
1 CONT
1 CONT      In the summer of 1817, my great grandfather Nathaniel Clark went from his home in Connecticut to find a suitable place in the wilderness for his son Ahira to start adult life   Nathaniel's wife was dead.  On his trips back an
1 CONC d forth Nathaniel stopped with a family in Canadaigua Lake, New York.  There he met a very bright young woman eminently equipped both physically and mentally to cope with life on the new frontier.  Nathaniel arranged for this girl
1 CONC 's marriage to his son in spite of the fact that Ahira was in love with a charming girl in Connecticut.
1 CONT (Nathaniel decided that she was too frail and delicate) Ahira obediently agreed to his father's wishes and the arrangements that Eleanor Case be ready ready on their return with hope chest packed and wedding cake ready.  The young
1 CONC  couple settled in Ohio, state of magnificent trees on the banks of beautiful (then) Lake Erie.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Nathaniel lived only a few years   Ahira became the administrator of law on the Western Reserve and for years continued to be the Justice or whatever the office was called.
1 CONT
1 CONT      They raised ten children - five girls and five boys.  Either Ahira or Eleanor must have enjoyed Greek history - the girls were named Samantha, Almena, Marilla, Urania and Calista.  The boys were Dewitt, Byron, Sedgwick, Harve
1 CONC y and George who was youngest son and my father. At his death, Ahira was able to leave a farm to each of his five sons and an equal dowery to his daughters.
1 CONT My father inherited the beautiful Colonial Home place alomg with the care of his mother.  Thus my mother was able to tell many tales of her mother-in-law's character.  I'm sure she was imposed on and remembered the unpleasant trai
1 CONC ts and trials of caring for an elderly woman.  My father, who was not much for making sharp deals soon invested in stock or some patent on wire fencing  and so had to mortgage the home farm.  Later selling it and buying a smaller 
1 CONC farm in Mother's hometown of Claridon   A nice house set on a hill with the road and a gurgling stream at the foot of the yard.  I remember Eleanor Clark, with great scorn calling it "Klip Knocket".
1 CONT
1 CONT             ____________________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT [from Noral Baughman; 3830 80th Ave SE; Olympia, WA  98501; (360) 491-4193 <noral
1 CONT
1 CONT                                THE
1 CONT                HONORABLE DANIEL ClARK OF WINDSOR,
1 CONT                            CONNECTICUT
1 CONT
1 CONT                      The story of his life.
1 CONT
1 CONT      In 1639, when he was seventeen years old, Daniel Clark of Weaxil, (rear Kenilworth), England, came to America with his uncle, Rev. Ephraim Huit, who became pastor of Old Windsor's Congregational Society, as a collegue of Rev.
1 CONC  John Wareham.  On June 15, 1644, Daniel Clark married Mary Newberry, daughter of Thomas Newberry and Jane Newberry, who came to America in the "Mary and John," 1630.  Thomas Newberry had two brothers among Cromwell's Dragoons, an
1 CONC d was descended from William Newberry, "Chronicler of his District."  To William Newberry was accorded the honor, granted to but one man in a town, of adopting as his surrame the name of the town where he resided.  Thomas Newberry
1 CONC  died in 1635 and his widow married the Rev. John Wareham, thus becoming to the second time an ancestress of many of the Clarks.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Tradition states that Daniel Clark went home after he had been here some years, overcome by a longing to see his people and his old haunts. He knocked at his parents' door and pretending to be a stranger from America was made
1 CONC  welcome, their pleasure in his coming being doubled when they found him to be from Connecticut and acquainted with their son.  It was Martha, his sister, who at length discovered the fraud and cried out that their Daniel had some
1 CONC  home at last.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Daniel Clark held many offices in the colony.  In 1654 he was tax assessor, appearing on the second tax list himself as having a "Family horse and two oxen."  He was Representative from 1657 to 1661, secretary of the Colony, 
1 CONC 1658 to 1662, member of the Court of Assistants, 1662-4, Commissioner, 1662, and Captain of the Colonial Troops, 1664.  He was made Attorney in the Andrus Court at Hartford March, l687, was a member of the Particular Court, a Depu
1 CONC ty, Clerk of the County Court, Member of the Committee to appoint and commission officers of the militia, one of the nineteen signers and grantees of the Charter of Connecticut, a member of the Committee to Advise the Indians, a m
1 CONC ember of the church, 1634, Recorder, 1658, and a member of numrous other committees on which be was appointed.  He was one of the magistrates in Windsor and on the appointmant of Daniel Clark, the Town Meeting made the following r
1 CONC ecord:  May 5, 1651, At a meeting of the Towne Mr. Clark was appointed to Sitt in the greate pew."  He sat in trial for witchcraft of Nathaniel Greensmith and wife in 1662.
1 CONT
1 CONT      In 1663 William Edwards, whose descendants later married those of Daniel Clark, had him removed from the Secretaryship for "infringement of Royal Prerogative" and he was offered the office of Assistant Secretary which he prou
1 CONC dly declined.  He was later reinstated for a season.  In 1679 he was made teacher and kept school for eighteen months, receiving forty pounds for this and other work.  The house lot which fell to him was a triangular piece on the 
1 CONC west side of Farrington River, the "Riverlet" near the Newberry lot and that of Rev. John Wareham.  In 1662 Daniel Clark and John Mason had four hundred acres granted to them, and in 1665 the former received another grant which wa
1 CONC s sold to Daniel Gilbert in 1672.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Daniel Clark Jr., afforded his father considerable trouble and anxiety at times.  He was hasty of speech and was fined twenty pounds for making contemptuous remarks about Major Treat, who had him sent to jail.  Having confess
1 CONC ed his fault and "Manifested some signs of repentance" the Court, earnestly entreated by Major Treat (urged on by Daniel Clark, Senior), ordered the youth released from prison and later remitted the fine in order that he might, as
1 CONC  his father expressed it, "pursue his art and trade" and settle among then undiscouraged by fines.
1 CONT In 1704 Daniel Jr., Locksmith, was fined ten shillings and sent to jail for voting out of his district but was later pardoned.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Daniel Clark's second wife was Martha (Pitkin) Walcott, widow of Simon Walcott, whose six children he took under his roof wIth their mother in 1689.  Governor Roger Walcott in his private journal wrote, "In the year 1689 my m
1 CONC other marryed with Dlaniel Clark, Esq., and I went with her to live on the West side of the river."  Again he said, "In the year 1691 thro' the constant monitions of my father- in-law and my mother I had many convictions of my sin
1 CONC  and danger but after a while they wore off."(!)  Finally we have the last brief entry with its simple tribut to the worth of the man, "In the year 1710--the 10th of August dyed my kind father-in-law Daniel Clark, Esq.,in the 86th
1 CONC  year of his age, with which I was much affected."
1 CONT
1 CONT      Daniel Clark has been called "the great-grandfather of governors."  He educated his stepson, Roger Walcott (who became governor), who married Sarah Drake, Daniel Clark's grandaughter, and had Oliver Wolcott, Governor of Conne
1 CONC cticut, whose son, Oliver, was Governor 1817-27.  Roger Wolcott, a descendant, was Governor of Massachusetts and Clark Bissell, another descendant was Governor of Connecticut, 1847-49.  Ursula Wolcott, a dauther of Roger Wolcott a
1 CONC nd Sarah Drake, married Mathew Griswold, Governor of Connecticut, and of their descendant it is said no less than twelve became governors.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Daniel Clark's father-in-law was a man of property and influence   He received from the General Court of Dorchester, a grant of land, 100 acres, in Neposett March, 1634, and many grants from the Dorchester Proprietary.  He la
1 CONC id out a large farm in Squantum and built a house there.  He lived on "The Rock", 1634, where he was freeman and selectman.  In 1635 he was appointed to oversee works at the castle; was early engaged in the Connecticut enterprise 
1 CONC and sold his property and prepared to remove to Windsor, his death preventing.  His widow and children removed to Windsor before 1639.  His will, dated Dec. 12, 1635, gave his wife two hundred pounds and the inventory included lan
1 CONC d in England valued at three hundred pounds.
1 CONT             ____________________________________
