1 CONT
1 CONT Provided by Tim Tucker:
1 CONT
1 CONT WILLIAM (REV) (HARTFORD FOUNDER)  was born October 11, 1601 in Stotfold, Bedfordshire, England, and died May 04, 1640 in Hartford, Hartford, CT.  He married AGNES (TUCKER) HARRIS 1633 in England, daughter of BARTHOLOMEW HARRIS and
1 CONC  ELIZABETH COLLAMORE.
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes for WILLIAM (REV) (HARTFORD FOUNDER) SPENCER:
1 CONT William Spencer was a Deputy to MA General Court, 1634 thru 1637, and representative to CT General Court, 1639-1640. He graduated from Trinity College at Cambridge. He came to this country with the friends of Winthrop in 1631 (?) 
1 CONC and settled Cambridge, MA. He was on the committee to frame a body of fundamental laws for the colony and was a founder of the Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company of Boston, his name appearing in the original charter. In 1634 
1 CONC he was on the committee to confer with the governor and assistants in regard to the care of the common stock, which led to the formation of the House of Representatives. In 1633, he returned to England to bring his wife Agnes Harr
1 CONC is (one book calls her Tucker).  They moved to Hartford, CT. in 1639, where his brother Thomas had settled. He died in 1640 and his widow Agnes married Mr. Edwards. These notes from the CT. Biographies, The Spencer Family by Glora
1 CONC  Clark, Willa Mae Heisler's research in CT. and PA. CeCilia Seiber's research in KY and IL records, and the Census.
1 CONT
1 CONT *****
1 CONT
1 CONT From the files of Stephen M. Lawson.
1 CONT
1 CONT William and Agnes Spencer settled at New Town (Cambridge), MA in 1632, an
1 CONT d he took the Freeman's Oath on Mar. 4, 1632/3. He was Deputy to the General Court seven times 1634-1638, Selectman in 1635, Lt. of the Train Band 1632, and charter member of the Ancient and Honourable Artillery Company in 1638/9.
1 CONC  The family moved to Hartford, CT in 1639. Children of william and Agnes: Elizabeth married first William Wellman, and second Jacob Jay, Sarah married John Case, and Samuel, Sr. married Sarah Meakins(a 4-great grandson, Bibliophil
1 CONC e William Augustus Spencer, Perished in the sinking of the Titanic). 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of WILLIAM SPENCER and AGNES HARRIS are:
1 CONT 30.    i.    ELIZABETH14 SPENCER, b. 1633, England; d. September 16, 1718, Killingsworth, Middlesex, CT.
1 CONT 31.    ii.    SARAH SPENCER, b. 1635, Simsbury, Hartford, CT; d. November 03, 1691, Simsbury, Hartford, CT.
1 CONT 32.    iii.    JOHN SPENCER, b. 1638; d. 1684.
1 CONT     iv.    RICHARD SPENCER, b. 1640.
1 CONT 33.    v.    SAMUEL SPENCER, b. 1642; d. 1716, Hartford, Hartford, CT.
1 CONT
1 CONT *****
1 CONT Pioneers of Massachusetts by Charles Henry Pope:  page 427
1 CONT
1 CONT William, Cambridge, frm March 4, 1632; prop. 1633; town officer and deputy 1634.  Perhaps he returned to England, and was the William who came in the Mary and John in March, 1633-4.
1 CONT *****
1 CONT Connectictut Nutmegger Article Volume 27, #4, page 570
1 CONT
1 CONT William Spencer played an important role in the activities of the Massachusetts Bay Company and was a co-founder of both Cambridge(Newe Towne), Massachusetts, and Hartford, CT.
1 CONT *****
1 CONT CT Nutmegger vol.29, issue 1, page 14 by Jack T. Spencer
1 CONT
1 CONT William Spencer was the first Town clerk of Cambridge and later served as a "Selectman" for several years.  William played even more important roles for the General Court of the Massachusetts Bay Company, especially in regard to s
1 CONC urveying and setting the boundary lines for many of the towns under the control of the Court.  For many years he served as a Deputy representing Newe Towne at the frequent meetings of the General Court in Cambridge and in Boston.
1 CONT *****
1 CONT CT Nutmegger, vol. 27,  by Jack T. Spencer and Edith W. Spencer
1 CONT
1 CONT Page 32-33:    The genealogy of the Five Spencer Siblings of Bedfordshire, England, was explored in detail in the famous articles published by Donald Lines Jacobus in The American Genealogist nearly a half century ago.  William, E
1 CONC lizabeth, Thomas, Michael, and Gerard Spencer were all born in county Bedfordshire in the early 1600's when Puritanism was assuming a major force in the religious live of England.  All five of the Spencer siblings came to America.
1 CONC   The two oldest brothers (William and Thomas) almost certainly came in the earliest contingents of the Winthrop Fleet, probably in 1630 or 1631.  William Spencer (1601-1640) played an important role in the history of the Massachu
1 CONC setts Bay Company and the founding of both Cambridge, Massachusetts and Hartford, Connecticut.
1 CONT     The five Spencer siblings had been raised in Stotfold, county Bedfordshire in the "heartland" of England.  Most of their ancestors had lived in the town of Edworth in the same county for several centuries.  There were numerous
1 CONC  other branches of the Spencer line who had lived in Bedforshire as well as in some of the neighboring counties.  The exact relationships among these earlier Spencer lines are not entirely clear.  However, as we have written in an
1 CONC  earlier account, the lines down to Gerard Spencer (father of the five siblings) have been depicted for nine generations (back to generation "H") in England with a  considerable degree of reliability.
1 CONT     Of the five Spencer siblings who came to America in the early 1600's, William undoubtedly was the best prepared for the task from the standpoint of age and educational attainments.  He was thirty years of age in 1631, while hi
1 CONC s siblings ranged in age from 29 (Elizabeth) to 17 (Gerard).  It has been stated by some historians that William was a close associate of the Rev. Thomas Hooker, the leader of the "Braintree Company" of county Essex.  Before comin
1 CONC g to America, Hooker had his headquarters at Little Baddow near Chelmsford in county Essex.  A recent search of the vital records for county Essex and several towns around Chelmsford has given no indication that the parents of the
1 CONC  five Spencer siblings (Gerard and Alice Spencer) had moved to county Essex in order to be near to the Rev. Hooker's congregation, nor is there any indication that any of the siblings had lived in county Essex.  This leads to the 
1 CONC conclusion that if William Spencer was in fact a close associate of the Rev. Hooker, this relationship may have developed in New England rather having taken place at an earlier date in the home country.
1 CONT     A careful search of all the original and secondary records on The Great Mirgration(1630-1640) has offered no clue as to the ship or ships on which the Spencer siblings embarked for New England.  We do not know for certain if t
1 CONC hey came as an individual group or singly or in pairs.  Despite these gaps in our knowledge, we can with a high degree of certainty designate the year in which William Spencer arrived in New England.  The same can be said for his 
1 CONC next younger brother Thomas Spencer.  These conclusions are based on the fact that the early ships from England uniformly left their home ports early in the spring and arrived at their destination in New England in mid-summer.  (T
1 CONC here was only a slight deviation from this pattern).  From the original records of the Massachusetts Bay Company, we know that William Spencer was in New England on 4 March 1632 when he took the oath of Freeman.  Hence, he must ha
1 CONC ve arrived in Boston at least by 1631.  Could William possibly have come in the first ships of the Winthrop Fleet in 1630?  Certainly the possibility cannot be ruled out.  In addition, we know William was in the original group of 
1 CONC eight proprietors who were the first residents of Newe Towne (later Cambridge).
1 CONT     Thomas Spencer took the oath of Freeman on 1 April 1633.  Likewise, Gerard was accepted on 9 March 1637; Michael in March of 1638.  The gap between the oldest pair of brothers and the youngest pair is considerable, amounting t
1 CONC o five or six years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Page 34:  From the detailed records of the General Courts of the Massachusetts Bay Comany, the name of William Spencer first appears in the listings on 9 January 1632.  William's name does not appear again until March of 1634 in t
1 CONC he Court meeting at Newe Towne.     According to Tepper, William Spencer and John Spencer (the latter of Ipswich) were on the "Mary and John" which left England on 24 March 1633.  (C.E. Banks gives the year as 1634, but probably t
1 CONC his was due to the confusion of listing some dates as 1633/34).  In any event, William Spencer was back in England for perhaps a year and apparently returned to Newe Towne in the summer of 1633.  It was not until nearly a year lat
1 CONC er (March 1634) that his name again appears in the recors of the General Court.  One might speculate that William's trip to England was a matrimonial mission related to Agnes Harris.  We wonder immediately how he was acquainted wi
1 CONC th a female from Devonshire in the far southwest of England while William originally came from eastern England.
1 CONT     On the other hand, if Agnes Harris came with the Matthew Allyn family in 1632 on the "Charles", she would have been in Newe Towne and easily could have established contact with William Spencer.  If this is a possible scenario,
1 CONC  why would William have made the long voyage back to England in 1632 and 1633?  Possibly we shall never know which of the two possible scenarios is the correct one.  Based purely on geographic considerations, it seems more likely 
1 CONC that William would have had a better opportunity to meet Agnes Harris at Newe Towne than would have been the case back in England.
1 CONT     William Spencer and Agnes Harris had three children:
1 CONT     Elizabeth Spencer, b. c1633; m (1) William Wellman, 1649. m (2) Jacob Joy.
1 CONT     Sarah Spencer, b. c1635-d. 1691; m. John Case
1 CONT     Samuel Spencer, b. c1636; m. Sarah Meakins.
1 CONT     All three children were born at Newe Towne before the family moved to Hartford, probably in 1639.  William Spencer died a short time later in 1640.  There were several land transactions in Hartford under William Spencer's name
1 CONC  which we will review in a later article.
1 CONT *****
1 CONT William Spencer, born 1611,  brothers to America,
1 CONT >     in 1632.  He took the freeman's oath in 1633 at Cambridge, Mass.  He was
1 CONT >     one of the first five founders of the military company, "The Ancient and
1 CONT >     Honorable Company of Artillery of Boston, Mass."  The Charter of this was 
1 CONT >     granted by the General Court in 1638.  In March 1636 he was appointed     
1 CONT >     "Lieutenant of the Military Company of Newton."  He was one of the first  
1 CONT >     settlers at Hartford, Conn., and was a Representative to the General 
1 CONT >     Court of Connecticut from Hartford, 1639-40.  He was Lieutenant of the 
1 CONT >     Train Band in the Militia.  He was appointed with Mr. Wyllis and Mr. 
1 CONT >     Webster to revise the laws of Connecticut.  In his will dated May 4,
1 CONT >     1640, and probated March 4, 1641, he mentioned his wife, Agnes, also his
1 CONT >     son, Samuel, and his daughters, Sarah and Elizabeth, and he appointed as
1 CONT >     overseers, "my cousin" Mathew Allen, John Pratt, and John Taylor.  After
1 CONT >     his death his widow, Agnes Spencer, married William Edwards.
1 CONT *****
1 CONT from Norris M. Taylor, Jr. (nmt1@ix.netcom.com) http://members.tripod.com/~ntgen/index.html
1 CONT (Sources: For Thomas and William Spencer, and Timothy Tomlins, the prime source is: "The Great Migration Begins", by Robert Charles Anderson, 1995. For Michael and Gerard, I have relied on various sources given me, primarily the T
1 CONC AG articles in the 1950's on the Spencer family by Jacobus and Connecticut Families.
1 CONT
1 CONT William. The earliest documentation available is for William (as deputy from Cambridge to the General Court in May, 1632 - no ships would have arrived in 1632 by then), showing arrival by the end of 1631. He was 30 years old then,
1 CONC  and married Agnes Harris. The "Spencers of the Great Migration reports he returned to England to marry her about 1633 and then brought her back. He first settled in Cambridge, but was in Hartford, Connecticut by 1639. He was very
1 CONC  active in the community and the Great Migration Begins shows many committees and positions he held in the community. He owned at least 11 parcels of land in Cambridge in 1635. He was the first to die, at a relatively young age of
1 CONC  only 39, leaving three kids 1-7 years of age. 
1 CONT *****
1 CONT trails@ni.net (Jerry England)
1 CONT William SPENCER. Born bef 11 Oct 1601 in Stotfold, Bedfordshire,
1 CONT England. William was baptized in Stotfold, Bedfordshire, England on 11 Oct
1 CONT 1601. William died in Hartford, Hartford, CT on 4 May 1640. Education:
1 CONT Cambridge Town Clerk - Able To Write Well. Religion: Admitted To MA Bay
1 CONT Church (Prob. Watertown) Bef 4 Mar 1632/3. In 1633 he married Agnes HARRIS,
1 CONT in Stotfold, Bedfordshire, England. Born abt 1604 in Barnstaple,
1 CONT Devonshire, England. Agnes was baptized in Barnstaple, Devonshire, England
1 CONT on 6 Apr 1604. Agnes died in Hartford, Hartford, CT aft 1680.
1 CONT *****
1 CONT A DIGEST OF THE EARLY CONNECTICUT PROBATE RECORDS.
1 CONT 1635 to 1650.
1 CONT
1 CONT Page 449   Name: Willia Spencer   Location: 
1 CONT Invt. œ231-12-02.
1 CONT A coppy of the will & testament of Willia Spencer, late of Hartiord, deceased, prsented in Court vpon oath by John Taylcott & John Pratt, of the said towne. A noate of the mynd and will of Willia Spencer, for prsent the 4th of May
1 CONC , 1640.
1 CONT Imprs. His will is that the estate that he hath in New Ingland, and also that wch may com to his wife hereafter, that is, any prte of his wifes portion yf any doe com, that all the estate be dyuyded as followeth: I give to my wife
1 CONC  one third prte of all my estate. I give to my sonne Samuell one third prte: I give to my two daughters, Sarah and Elizabeth, one third partte.
1 CONT The children to be brought vppe wth the improvement of the whole estate that I leaue both to my wife and children.
1 CONT Also my mynd is, my Cosen Matthew Allyn, my brother John Pratt, and John Taylcoate, that these three partyes or any two of them shall have the Ouersight of my Estate; and in case that they shall see in theire judgement the Estate 
1 CONC to be wasted. that they shall haue power to take the children and their portions ( ) for their bringing vppe, and to pay the Children their portions that remayne at the severall tymes above written.
1 CONT Also my mynd is, that my wife shall have no power to alienatt or make sale of my howse or any prte of my land I leave wthout the eansent of two of the prtyes that are to ouersee my Estate.
1 CONT These last three lines were added subsequently in the hand writing of Secretary John Allyn. The distribution of the estate appeares in the Records of the perticular Courte, the 24th June, 1650, fol. 10, and the Coppyes of the bill
1 CONC s given to the Courte for the payment of the œ30 to the children are in the Booke of Records of Lands for the Severall townes, at ye other end of it. The original Bills are vppon fyle of wills & Inventories.
1 CONT Land Records. Vol. II, Page--(Not paged in this side of the Book). Know all men by these prss, that I, John Tayllcott, of Hartford, upon Connecticut River, do hereby Bynde my selfe, my Heires, Executors, Administers and Assigns, t
1 CONC o pay or cause to be paid unto Sara Spencer, and to Elizabeth Spencer, the full value of tenn pounds when they shall bee of full age of eighteen yeares, in some pay that is Current for so much when the said payments are to be made
1 CONC .
1 CONT John Tallcott.
1 CONT Witness: Richard Lord. John Steele
1 CONT 14 March, 1650.
1 CONT It is also agreed, that if either of the children dye before the time that they are of age, then the survivour & survivours shall receive it at the time when it should have been paid to the deceased, if he or she had lived. And if
1 CONC  they all dye before the said time, then it shall bee paid to Agnes Edwards, or her Lawfull Attorney of the said Agnes, the mother of the said children.
1 CONT John Tallcott, John Steele.
1 CONT March the twelth, one thousand Six hundreth and fifty. Know all men by these prsents, that I, Richard Lord, of Hartford, doe owe and confess my self to be Indebted unto the Executors & supervisors of William Spencers Last will and
1 CONC  Testament, the full sum of Twenty pounds Sterling for the aloane use of Samuel Spencer and Sara Spencer, to be paid as follows: fifteene pounds when the said Samuel shall be of full age, which will bee in the yeare 1660, in any p
1 CONC ay that shall be Current in the Country at that time for so much; and five pounds unto Sara Spencer, at the age of eighteen yeares, wch will be in the yeare 1653.
1 CONT Richard Lord.
1 CONT Witness: John Steele. William Edwards.
1 CONT Court Record, Page 10--(Vol. II) 24 June, 1650.
1 CONT With the Information of the Overseers, in presence of Thomas Spencer, the brother of sd. Decd, with the Consent of the wife of William Edwards, this Court do judge that œ30 is as much as the Estate here will bear to be sequestered
1 CONC  for the Use of the Children, also provided that what shall be paid here or in England of any Estate due to the wife of the sd. William Spencer, 2-3 shall be and remain to the Children
0 @N6000000003927570663@ NOTE In 1639 William Spencer joined the Hooker settlement at Hartford. He is listed on the monument in the Ancient Burying Ground in Hartford as one of the founders of the Connecticut Colony. There he was elected a selectman, became a 
1 CONC Deputy to the first session of the General Court in 1639, and was appointed on a committee of that body, to make the first code of laws for the Colony. No doubt his untimely death in 1640 ended a brilliant career.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to some references, William Spencer was a graduate of Trinity College, Cambridge, England coming to New England with friends of John Winthrop in 1631; they settled in Cambridge. In 1633 he may have then returned to bring
1 CONC  his wife and came with her in the ship "Mary and John" (according to page 203 Wm. R. Cutter's Genealogy and History of Connecticut (Vol. 3)). Passenger lists searched do not seem to agree on the arrival of the four Spencer brothe
1 CONC rs. One John and an William Spencer are listed by Banks as passengers on the "Mary and John" which sailed from Southampton 24 Mar 1633/34 but no wives are listed for them (page 111) and the other three brothers, Garrett, Michael a
1 CONC nd Thomas are not included here. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The list of passengers arriving 1630 on the "Mary and John" which numbered 160 does not include any Spencers. John Farmer's "Genealogical Register of First Settlers of N.E." (with additions by Samuel Drake) pp. 270-271 lists the f
1 CONC ollowing four believed to be the four Spencer brothers: 1) Garrett Spencer 1634 in Cambridge; 1637 in Lynn, Mass. 2) Michael Spencer 1634 in Cambridge; 1637 in Lynn, Mass. 3) William Spencer 1632 in Cambridge; freeman 1633; repres
1 CONC entative 1635; one of the founders of the Ancient and Honorable Artillery Co. of Boston. 4) Thomas Spencer 1632 in Cambridge; brother of John; freeman 1634. The history by Cutter lists several important positions held by William S
1 CONC pencer, including: - In 1634 he was on the committee to confer with the governor and assistants in regard to the care of the common stock, which led to the formation of the house of representatives. He was the first representative
1 CONC  chosen, and held the office until he left the colony. - In 1639 he went with his family to Hartford where his brother, Thomas had settled. - In 1639/40 he was representative in the general assembly in Hartford and while there he 
1 CONC prepared the first revised laws of the colony. - 4 May 1640 William made his Will and died at Hartford 1640. The "Alderman History" reveals the Will of William Spencer devided his estate into thirds: to his son, Samuel one-third; 
1 CONC to his widow, Agnes, one-third; and to his two daughters Sarah and Elizabeth, one-third. Following from "Catalogue of First Puritan Settlers of Conn." by R. R. Hinman: Spencer, William, Hartford, deputy in August and September, 16
1 CONC 39 -- committee to inspect arms once in three months, and provide powder for Hartford in '39. He was appointed with Mr. George Wyllys and Mr. Welles to revise the laws of the colony in '39 -- selectman in '39 -- an original propri
1 CONC etor of Hartford -- ancestor of Hon. Joshua Spencer, of Utica, N.Y. Spencer, William, of Hartford -- died in 1640. He left a wife and 3 children, viz. Sarah, Elizabeth and Samuel. He was a kinsman of Matthew Allyn, a brother in la
1 CONC w of John Pratt and John Talcott. William Spencer was the ancestor of the Hon. Judge Spencer and Hon. John C. Spencer, of the State of New York. He was one of the first Puritan settlers of Hartford.
1 CONT
1 CONT 249John Hall sold his home-lot to William Spencer, which was on the brow of the hill about where the Catlin place is now (1886).
1 CONT
1 CONT 250William Spencer, Cambridge, 1831, brother of Thomas; freeman, Mass., March 4, 1632/3; deputy for Newtown, May, 1632; May, 1634; March, 1534/5; March 163/6; Sept., 1636; May, 1637; Sept., 1637; March, 1637/8; chosen Lieut. for N
1 CONC ewtown, March 163/7; one of the founders of the Ancient andhonorable Artillery Co., and had other evidences of the public favor bestowed upon him. Remoed to Hartford, 1639, where his home-lot was about where the Union depot now st
1 CONC ands. He was deputy in August and September, 1639 and appointed Wylls and Webster to revise the laws of the colony; townsman, 1640; d. in 1640, leaving a widow, Agnes, whom he m. about 1633, who became afterward the wife of Willia
1 CONC m Edwards; inv. #291.12.2. He appoints "my cosin Matthew Allyn, my brother John Pratt," and John Talcott to be overseers of this estate. Will dated March 4, 1640, presented May 4, 1640. Ch.: i. Elizabeth, m. (1) William Wellman, o
1 CONC f Gloucester, New London, and Killingworth. (2) May 23, 1672, Jacob Joy, of Killingworth. ii. Sarah, b. 1636; m. about 1657, John Case, of Windsor, afterward of Simsbury; d. Nov. 3, 1691. iii. Samuel, b. about 1639; m. Sarah, prob
1 CONC . dau. of John Meakins.(John M. names dau. Sarah Spencer in his will, Nov. 22, 1702.) He d. about 1716, in Hartford.
