1 CONT Agnes Harris Spencer and William Edwards
1 CONT
1 CONT There have been a few published reports on the chronology of Agnes Harris, wife of William Spencer, who five years after the death of her first husband, married William Edwards in Hartford, Connecticut.  Agnes Harris was a native 
1 CONC of Barnstaple, county Devon, England, where she was born 6 April 1604, daughter of Bartholomew and Elizabeth (Collamore) Harris.  A descendant of Anges Harris (Donald C. Berquist) believes it likely that Agnes came to New England 
1 CONC in 1632 on the "Charles".  C.E. Banks in his passenger list notes that the Allyn relatives of Agnes Harris "probably" were on board the "Charles", including Matthew Allyn, his wife Margaret Allyn (cousin to Agnes) and children Mar
1 CONC y and John Allyn.  Banks did not list Agnes Harris as a possible passenger on this particular voyage.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Genealogical  Dictionary of New England Settlers, Volume 2
1 CONT page 104
1 CONT Edwards, WILLIAM, Hartford 1639, but not freem. bef. 1658; he was brot. in childhood, says the fam. tradit by his mo. Ann, w. of James Cole, m. Agnes, wid. of William Spencer, had only ch. Richard, bef. ment. b. May 1647. 
1 CONT
1 CONT *****
1 CONT
1 CONT This is the same Agnes Harris who is the matriarch of the William
1 CONT Spencer line of Hartford, Conn. Has royalty lines waaaay back. See
1 CONT Roberts Royal Descents of 500 Immigrants.
1 CONT
1 CONT *****
1 CONT Agnes (Harris) Spencer has a royal line to Edward I, King of England
1 CONT (p 226, Ancestors of American Presidents and the RD500)
1 CONT
1 CONT *****
1 CONT As the wife of William Edwards, Agnes Harris also became matriarch of
1 CONT the American Edwards clan that includes Jonathan Edwards of the Great
1 CONT Awakening and Aaron Burr. So all descendants of William Spencer are
1 CONT half cousins to those luminaries.
1 CONT *****
1 CONT
1 CONT from Norris M. Taylor, Jr. (nmt1@ix.netcom.com) http://members.tripod.com/~ntgen/index.html
1 CONT
1 CONT His widow, Agnes Harris, married, second,William Edwards in 1645, and he became the step-father to these children. By her second marriage, she became matriarch of the Edwards clan that includes Jonathan Edwards, the noted preacher
1 CONC  of the Great Awakening, and Aaron Burr. Thus, descendants of William Spencer are half-cousins of their Edwards kin, by sharing Agnes Harris as their ancestral "mom." Agnes Harris is of royal descent. See Royal Descents of 500 Imm
1 CONC igrants, by Roberts. 
1 CONT *****
0 @N6000000003017297478@ NOTE The article that identified Agnes Harris, identified her English family, and started the linkage of her into her royal lines, started with The American Genealogist Vol. 63, 33-45, 1988.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her royal lineage is covered in the RD500 by Gary Boyd Roberts and several references to her intertwining relationships in Roberts' Notable Kin, 1998, both for sale at NEHGS.
0 @N6000000003927570673@ NOTE might be Agnes Tucker Heane?
1 CONT The genealogist for the New England Historic and Genealogical Society, Gary Boyd Roberts in his master work, ""The Royal Descents of 500 Immigrants to the American Colonies or the United States" has her as Agnes Harris, descendant
1 CONC  of Hugh Capet, King of France. (page 445) She married William Spencer first, William Edwards second. Her parents were Bartholomew Harris and Elizabeth Collamore.
0 @N6000000000337357519@ NOTE Source: Stiles, Windsor:  a wealthy cloth weaver of Waterbury, CT.  One of his children was Rev. Thomas Lewis, graduate of Yale College, 1741.
0 @N6000000003017292688@ NOTE Third Generation: Deacon Joseph Lewis, son of Joseph, settles in Waterbury, Connecticut. In 1700 he married Sarah Andrews [Andrus]. He died November 26, 1749. Joseph was one of the respected and substantial men of the town of Wate
1 CONC rbury. He was town Treasurer in 1711 [he also was the town fence surveyor]. Another son, Thomas, graduated from Yale College in 1741 and became a Congregational Minister and died in Georgia in 1804. (Yale College was established i
1 CONC n 1700). A third son, John, also lived in Naugatuck, Connecticut."
